34561165,Artificial Intelligence in Radiology Education: A Longitudinal Approach.,10.1016/j.acra.2021.08.022,,,,,,Vrushab Gowda; Sheryl Gillikin Jordan; Omer A Awan,W3201458485,,W1036172382; W2855469196; W2972275637; W2996055412; W3003903532; W3005891124; W3086667591; W3130756609; W3134712086; W3134864973; W3140744160; W6774155559; W6792859051,20,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34569300,Artificial Intelligence in Radiology: A Canadian Environmental Scan,10.1177/08465371211038940,,,,,,David Li; Paul H. Yi,W3202359725,"Recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) have the potential to transform radiology. Despite Canada’s leadership in deep learning and growing interest among radiologists, the prevalence of AI initiatives in Canadian academic radiology departments has not been evaluated.Anenvironmental scanwill highlight strengths and opportunities in the Canadian radiology AI ecosystem. The objective of this study was to conduct an environmental scan of initiatives related to AI education, research, and industry partnerships in Canadian academic radiology departments. We hypothesized that AI-related initiatives would be common in Canadian radiology departments.",W2042933531; W2796925382; W3008714421; W3130756609,1,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
32997133,Embracing the role of artificial intelligence in the medication-use process,10.1093/ajhp/zxaa304,,,,,,Daniel J. Cobaugh; Kasey K. Thompson,W3091138458,"Artificial intelligence (AI)—the use of computer systems to perform tasks that would normally require human cognition1—has become ubiquitous in 21st-century society, including extensive deployment in healthcare. By some projections, the public- and private-sector investments in AI in healthcare will reach $6.6 billion by 2021,2 with AI applications in clinical settings ranging from the automation of the differential diagnosis to health-system operations. Given the rapid implementation of AI in healthcare and the potential for its expanded use in the medication-use process, this issue of AJHP includes a timely and necessary ASHP Statement on the Use of Artificial Intelligence in Pharmacy.3 This statement envisions the role of pharmacy departments, pharmacists, and pharmacy technicians in the use of AI in patient care and optimizing medication use. With the Statement on the Use of Artificial Intelligence in Pharmacy, ASHP seeks to influence AI development to ensure that patients benefit from the use of this technology while minimizing unintended consequences and errors. The ASHP statement addresses several essential domains that require the engagement of pharmacists, including determination of which areas of the medication-use process would benefit from the use of AI, a determination of which AI approaches (ie, supervised, unsupervised, or reinforcement learning) are most appropriate for the tasks under consideration, and evaluation of AI, including development of clinical validation standards. In addition, the statement also calls for health systems to develop AI integration roadmaps as they undertake strategic planning. Accordingly, pharmacists should also assume a leadership role in these planning processes to ensure that AI is integrated appropriately into the medication-use process. Given the rapidly changing nature of technology in general, and AI specifically, an ASHP statement of this nature will surely need to be updated frequently to reflect the evolution of AI in healthcare.",W3085728852,8,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
32066276,Artificial Intelligence Pipeline for Risk Prediction in Cardiovascular Imaging,10.1161/CIRCIMAGING.120.010427,,,,,,Karen G. Ordovas; Youngho Seo,W3008299515,,W1130218261; W1967543638; W2100763980; W2104231124; W2105923479; W2155573254; W2155820750; W2562251009; W2803176574; W2919383900; W2921335396; W2969828256; W2970272920; W3008871094,2,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38009886,"Artificial Intelligence: Basics, Impact, and How Nurses Can Contribute",10.1188/23.CJON.595-601,,,,,,Britney Starr,W4389047434,"Artificial intelligence (AI) experts have deemed AI a “revolutionary technology” (Mills, 2020, para. 2) that is projected to radically change health care (Zhou et al., 2023). The healthcare AI market is a $14.6 billion enterprise and is expected to reach $102.7 billion by 2028 (MarketsandMarkets, 2023). AI has the potential to greatly affect oncology care, including cancer prevention and screening, treatment, survivorship, and end-of-life care (Tawfik et al., 2023).",,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
