38705741,The Integration and Impact of Artificial Intelligence in Otolaryngology—Head and Neck Surgery,10.1016/j.otc.2024.04.001,,,,,,Matthew G. Crowson; Obinna I. Nwosu,W4396635708,"Incorporating artificial Intelligence and machine learning into otolaryngology requires careful data handling, security, and ethical considerations. Success depends on interdisciplinary cooperation, consistent innovation, and regulatory compliance to improve clinical outcomes, provider experience, and operational effectiveness.",W1487801014; W2913945298; W2914425100; W2943876388; W3000223525; W3007210411; W3009505516; W3018610833; W3033882614; W3036067296; W3172260675; W4210681325; W4283366687; W4285315819; W4293029444; W4361003621; W4376602294; W4383872984; W4385716071; W4386223805; W6763576368; W6773123919; W6776224749; W6855833118,5,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
30821036,Artificial intelligence‐augmented ECG assessment: The promise and the challenge,10.1111/jce.13891,,,,,,Kelley P. Anderson,W2919332062,"Since Einthoven developed the electrocardiogram (ECG) investigators and clinicians have sought to expand the diagnostic potential of this convenient, widely available and relatively inexpensive noninvasive examination [1]. In this issue of the Journal of Cardiovascular Electrophysiology, Attia and colleagues demonstrate the ability of a novel algorithm based on the standard 12 lead ECG developed with deep learning artificial intelligence (AI) to detect patients with reduced left ventricular ejection fraction (rLVEF) [2]. This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.",W1962132018; W2025042121; W2081311610; W2092699252; W2122565792; W2319068247; W2747849569; W2888341161; W2901226889,10,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
