40540962,Use of artificial intelligence processing tools to evaluate qualitative data: Student researchers compared to faculty researchers,10.1016/j.cptl.2025.102418,,,,,,Shanna K. O'Connor; Erin E. Miller; Alyssa R. Zweifel; Danielle M. Schievelbein; Anjali R. Parmar; James W. Amell,W4411438047,"Artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a promising tool to support qualitative data analysis, yet its role in faculty-led studies that incorporate student researchers remains under investigation. This study examined differences in inductive thematic analysis generated by student and faculty researchers using AI compared to traditional faculty-led coding. Three qualitative datasets were analyzed using OpenAI's ChatGPT by faculty and student researchers. Findings showed AI-assisted analyses identified most themes accurately, though faculty-generated AI results aligned more closely with expert-reviewed themes than student-generated AI results. AI may be a valuable tool to enhance efficiency particularly in initial evaluation of qualitative data.",W643388607; W2079567258; W2103666798; W4386347795; W4386908233; W4387265171; W4388103056; W4399211433; W4401507329; W4404061054; W4407355408; W6856959534; W6858842398,4,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
42555787,Artificial Intelligence Readiness Among Oncology Nurses: Results of a Cross-Sectional Survey,10.1188/26.CJON.314-320,,,,,,Samina Asfandiyar; Roselyn Ogunkunle,W7172328652,"Artificial intelligence (AI) tools relevant to oncology nursing, including predictive analytics, natural language processing, and wearable technologies, are rapidly advancing, but low AI literacy, limited training opportunities, ethical concerns, and usability challenges continue to limit their adoption (Colomer-Lahiguera et al., 2024; von Gerich et al., 2022).",,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
