27441580,The Future of Artificial Intelligence,10.1089/big.2016.29004.vda,,,,,,Vasant Dhar,W2299633305,,W1904325912; W2008645545; W2164424353; W2165053334; W2243132409; W2294668285; W2333018025; W3121961986,45,2016,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
40577582,Expertise Is the Special Sauce That Makes Using AI an Essential Tool in Academics,10.1188/25.ONF.244-245,,,,,,Janine A Overcash,W4411717558,"This is my 25th year as a professor, and some of my friends have begun to ask when I plan to retire. The truth is, I love my career and have never seriously considered retirement. I am healthy, will turn 60 next year, and continue to enjoy teaching. I have adapted to countless changes and upgrades that have reshaped academia. However, their question made me reflect on what drives people to retire. For me, the answer is relevance.",W4400697408; W4402503172,0,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
42101229,The 3C model: a simple prompt engineering framework for generative artificial intelligence,10.1093/acamed/wvag098,,,,,,Rahul Damania; Michael Hrdy; Neil Mehta; Traci Wolbrink,W7160612974,"This Last Page introduces the intuitive 3C Model (Context, Creation, Clarity), a simplified prompt engineering framework designed to help medical educators effectively and confidently integrate generative artificial intelligence into teaching practices.",W4379598302; W4382632493; W4386867830,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41991460,Ethical Considerations for Artificial Intelligence Tools in Small Animal Veterinary Medicine,10.1016/j.cvsm.2026.03.001,,,,,,Simon Coghlan; Thomas Quinn,W7154495473,"As veterinary artificial intelligence (AI) tools become more available, it is vital to ensure that they genuinely promote patient well-being and avoid harm. This article examines the ethics of AI in small animal veterinary medicine, considering both ""machine"" and ""human"" aspects. The article then outlines appropriate accountability responsibilities for veterinary practitioners, as well as for AI developers and purveyors, hospital and clinic managers, and professional veterinary bodies. Understanding these ethical issues and accountability measures should help steer the creation, maintenance, and use of AI tools toward meeting the core veterinary medical obligation of improving animal health and welfare.",W1941254941; W1996223773; W2135027935; W2164619742; W2252533186; W2435495728; W2581508908; W2786587812; W2789684805; W2908201961; W2912786586; W2915292867; W2981869278; W3013872196; W3024012391; W3048650942; W3091232338; W3145875739; W3198654417; W3200762103; W3201613726; W3207214105; W4205164650; W4206160619; W4220654314; W4220691866; W4221055928; W4229368486; W4281732150; W4283271363; W4286630386; W4307902875; W4313648826; W4362664950; W4376137749; W4387861163; W4389948084; W4391400075; W4393177740; W4394693092; W4399209391; W4405991176; W4406237418; W4406432544; W4407353697; W4407809449; W4408367171; W4408508969; W4408596322; W4409147850; W4409484060; W4412528223; W4414465746; W4415107779; W4417049610; W7118475598; W7128816044,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39331456,Authors' Response: Ethical use of artificial intelligence in academic writing,10.4103/IJO.IJO_356_24,,,,,,Himel Mondal; Shaikat Mondal,W4402902928,"Dear Editor, We thank the authors[1] for taking the time to read our article, “ChatGPT in academic writing: Maximizing its benefits and minimizing the risks,”[2] and sharing their thoughtful concerns regarding the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in academic or scientific writing. We acknowledge the valid points they raised regarding potential challenges and risks associated with AI-generated content from large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT, Google Bard, Microsoft Bing Chat, Perplexity, and Claude. The potential emergence of biased content in the generated output may come from biased training data. Hence, authors should exercise caution and refrain from unquestioningly relying on AI-generated content, recognizing that LLMs function as a tool and not as the primary author.[3] For example, a typist was instructed by an officer to type a letter to a friend to convey birthday wishes. Due to the habit of drafting official letters, the typist addressed the letter with the salutation “Respected friend.” However, when the officer checked it, the typist was asked to modify it to “Dear friend.” The amended letter was signed by the officer and was dispatched to the friend. The recipient experienced joy upon receiving the heartfelt wish as if the letter was written by her friend. In the contemporary era, AI takes on a role akin to the typist, allowing users to command its functions for content creation and editing in accordance with their preferences. In this context, we would like to share why the AI is not an author. According to the International Committee of Medical Journal Editors criteria, an author should satisfy at least one criterion from all four categories of tasks as shown in Table 1. While AI can satisfy the first and second criteria, it cannot satisfy the third and fourth criteria.[4]Table 1: International Committee of Medical Journal Editors criteria for authorship and capability of AIThe concern raised about overreliance on AI is an interesting and futuristic thought. When a new technology comes, we start using it and gradually it evolves. For example, the internet is such a technology. Can we think now about writing a research protocol without searching literature on the internet? However, think about the difficulty researchers faced before the introduction of PubMed in 1997.[5] Generative AI is evolving, and the future will tell us how much we will be dependent on that. In this context, we would like to share that leading publishers are not against the use of AI in medical writing. However, they suggest cautious use of the technology. Perspectives of three publishers are shown in Fig. 1.Figure 1: Portion of texts from guidelines laid by three publishers about the use of artificial intelligence in writingIn response to the suggestion for more concrete examples or case studies, we appreciate the feedback. We recognize the importance of providing practical guidance, and we will consider incorporating relevant examples and case studies in future publications to enhance the clarity of our recommendations. The insights regarding the vulnerability of residents under training and postgraduate practitioners to potential misuse of AI tools are particularly valuable. Any technology comes with its own set of advantages and disadvantages. As the technology is with us and comes with free access, any resident can access it for any purpose. Hence, we presume that a widespread training program on the ethical use of AI technology for such vulnerable users is the need of the hour. Financial support and sponsorship: Nil. Conflicts of interest: There are no conflicts of interest.",W2907013361; W4387232566; W4388832642; W4389991382; W4402902866,0,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37789243,Avoid patenting AI-generated inventions,10.1038/d41586-023-03116-0,,,,,,Daniel Gervais,W4387303802,,,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
29052645,The future of work,10.1038/550315a,,,,,,,W4230224928,,,1,2017,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
42174927,Toward an Ethical Framework for Patient-Facing Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Settings,10.3233/SHTI260252,,,,,,Meghan Reading Turchioe; Pooja Desai; Zayan Reza; Natalie Benda,W7161966650,"Patient-facing artificial intelligence in clinical settings raises distinct ethical challenges that require explicit attention as patients transition from passive recipients to active users of AI-augmented healthcare. We conducted 36 qualitative interviews with patients, healthcare professionals, and AI developers using a postpartum depression prediction tool as a use case to identify ethical considerations for patient-facing AI implementation. Six interconnected themes emerged, which synthesized into a preliminary ethical framework positioning transparency as the foundational enabler, the four core principles (autonomy, beneficence, justice, privacy) in productive tension, and trust as both an emergent outcome and prerequisite for engagement. Our findings suggest that ethical implementation requires making explicit the trade-offs between principles and involving patients in deliberating context-specific tensions rather than treating ethics as a compliance checklist.",,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41440549,Ethical Horizons in Robotic Rehabilitation: Ensuring Safe AI Use Under the EU AI Act,10.3390/medsci13040317,,,,,,Rocco Salvatore Calabrò,W4417318370,Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping robotic rehabilitation and shifting practice beyond pre-programmed repetitive movement patterns toward data-driven and personalised therapeutic interventions for people with neurological and musculoskeletal impairments [...].,W3042276730; W3165866606; W3206840963; W4382394524; W4391689860; W4392169153; W4392293571; W4402051188; W4402313440; W4402651787; W4407173730; W4409625123; W4411621687; W4412085120; W4412392770; W4413309469; W4414090729; W4415237333,2,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37758895,AI will transform science — now researchers must tame it,10.1038/d41586-023-02988-6,,,,,,,W4387106914,,,24,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
25462637,Deep learning in neural networks: An overview,10.1016/j.neunet.2014.09.003,,,,,,Jürgen Schmidhuber,W2076063813,"In recent years, deep artificial neural networks (including recurrent ones) have won numerous contests in pattern recognition and machine learning. This historical survey compactly summarizes relevant work, much of it from the previous millennium. Shallow and Deep Learners are distinguished by the depth of their credit assignment paths, which are chains of possibly learnable, causal links between actions and effects. I review deep supervised learning (also recapitulating the history of backpropagation), unsupervised learning, reinforcement learning & evolutionary computation, and indirect search for short programs encoding deep and large networks.",W6908809; W12277440; W16144991; W19621276; W20283819; W22040386; W22297218; W23758216; W25946478; W28412257; W32403112; W34912900; W35527955; W44465109; W48479683; W53522008; W58921469; W65738273; W66427752; W71723769; W73247952; W86972778; W88216543; W91815983; W99063960; W99073943; W101771737; W104912628; W109020964; W117096852; W122801842; W130805539; W131207632; W132264163; W134309601; W139877375; W139960808; W145032828; W154549827; W155854392; W156157444; W158151893; W158468099; W164569299; W165295313; W166862392; W175136955; W179648542; W194249466; W194391859; W196393734; W196761320; W196871588; W198187986; W205043379; W215825141; W269193847; W285212305; W351653889; W355724390; W359568995; W563274145; W566068903; W569156507; W574962919; W578108286; W632630573; W656732519; W755046805; W870929296; W961347149; W1176136657; W1278167707; W1408639475; W1479911746; W1480376833; W1481230657; W1482827835; W1484443217; W1485981043; W1486056878; W1486707268; W1486852018; W1487408605; W1487583988; W1490454746; W1497256448; W1499060359; W1501657095; W1504820690; W1506806321; W1508856789; W1509672901; W1510657949; W1512947503; W1514175292; W1514875444; W1515851193; W1515891729; W1520048352; W1521977997; W152349