31581288,Executive summary of the 2019 ASHP Commission on Goals: Impact of artificial intelligence on healthcare and pharmacy practice,10.1093/ajhp/zxz205,,,,,,,W4238930314,"Journal Article Executive summary of the 2019 ASHP Commission on Goals: Impact of artificial intelligence on healthcare and pharmacy practice Get access American Journal of Health-System Pharmacy, Volume 76, Issue 24, 15 December 2019, Pages 2087–2092, https://doi.org/10.1093/ajhp/zxz205 Published: 04 October 2019",W2172767501; W2558050786; W2561454645; W2580657852; W2604186734; W2613479577; W2613682154; W2651948199; W2664267452; W2735580341; W2761525601; W2775396371; W2807155721; W2896853655; W2899921784; W2904251400; W2911526543; W4235893153,19,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
33217781,Medicolegal considerations of radiology report writing in the new pjmirodigm of artificial intelligence,10.1111/1754-9485.13126,,,,,,Robert James Johnson; Don MacKenzie,W3106333912,"As artificial intelligence (AI) use in radiology increases, the medicolegal considerations are increasingly important for practising radiologists, many of whom are being exposed to this technology for the first time. We discuss new considerations of liability with particular reference to not utilising AI when it is available, when a radiologist disagrees with AI and how AI outputs and radiologist’s interpretations should be recorded.",W2889498778; W2935971621,3,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39192111,AI firms must play fair when they use academic data in training,10.1038/d41586-024-02757-z,,,,,,,W4401911390,Researchers are among those who feel uneasy about the unrestrained use of their intellectual property in training commercial large language models. Firms and regulators need to agree the rules of engagement. Researchers are among those who feel uneasy about the unrestrained use of their intellectual property in training commercial large language models. Firms and regulators need to agree the rules of engagement.,,10,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41639029,The Roboagents Are Coming! The Promise and Challenge of Artificial Intelligence Advance Directives,10.1002/hast.70042,,,,,,Jacob M. Appel,W7128072258,"Advance directives have historically relied upon human agents. But what happens when a patient appoints an artificial intelligence system as an agent? This essay introduces the idea of roboagents-chatbots authorized to make medical decisions when individuals lose capacity. After describing potential models, including a personal AI companion and a chatbot that has not been trained on a patient's values and preferences, the essay explores the ethical tensions these roboagents generate regarding autonomy, bias, consent, family trust, and physician well-being. This essay then calls for legal clarity and ethical guidance regarding the status of roboagents in light of their potential as alternative health care agents.",W63417030; W1857610898; W2000397034; W2005763353; W2168361806; W2557671501; W2605712076; W2730923810; W2981869278; W3019293901; W3027367391; W3080706116; W3165214380; W3203845411; W4315880904; W4400883010; W4402024809; W4402860127; W4410325474,1,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38263299,Computers make mistakes and AI will make things worse — the law must recognize that,10.1038/d41586-024-00168-8,,,,,,,W4391140472,,,11,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
30870214,Acid–Base Reports Need a Text Explanation,10.1097/ALN.0000000000002628,,,,,,Alan W. Grogono,W2922182536,,W1966232918; W1976335339; W1976516202; W2001203315; W2014912413; W2016922471; W2018243576; W2027056476; W2099070673; W2109283698; W2144369611; W2408278111; W2747849569,2,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
32883682,Sexual reproductive health chatbots: should we be so quick to throw artificial intelligence out with the bathwater?,10.1136/bmjsrh-2020-200823,,,,,,Elizabeth Jane Wasson; Katie Driver; Megan Hughes; Jasmine Bailey,W3083361151,"We found the concepts discussed by Nadarzynski et al on the acceptability of artificial intelligence (AI)-embedded chatbots, video consultations and live webchats in sexual and reproductive health (SRH) interesting.1 However, we think the broad conclusion made that among patients there is currently little support for SRH chatbots ignores potentially important uses of this technology.

A recent systematic review on AI in primary care published in BMJ Health and Care Informatics highlighted the importance of testing AI in a similar scenario to that in which it would be used.2 In Nadarzynski et al …",W2519087234; W2883512086; W3002223964; W3022563182,7,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41723594,Holding the Scalpel: Scientific Authorship and Responsibility in the Era of Generative Artificial Intelligence,10.1177/00031348261429461,,,,,,Javier Arredondo Montero,W7130930090,"Scientific writing and authorship are fundamentally acts of professional judgment and responsibility. This essay examines these principles in the era of increasingly fluent generative artificial intelligence (AI), arguing that scientific integrity-much like surgical mastery-depends on a level of earned comprehension and accountability that no algorithm can simulate. Drawing on a surgical experience in a resource-limited setting to illustrate the nature of judgment under uncertainty, the piece explores how the rise of AI risks replacing genuine expertise with hollow fluency. The essay concludes that judgment and responsibility must remain irreducibly human in both surgical practice and scientific authorship.",W2790209545; W4327946446; W4384561707; W4390937667; W4413357894,1,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34634477,AI for drug discovery is advancing rapidly: we need smart biology for it to fulfill its mission.,10.1016/j.drudis.2021.09.023,,,,,,Misti Ushio; Zachary Carpenter,W3207728743,,W2118822739; W3016722055,4,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37306152,The Path to Solving the Protein Folding Problem,10.2144/btn-2023-0031,,,,,,Jenny Straiton,W4380290489,"[Formula: see text] With advances in imaging technologies and the development of artificial intelligence-based predictive software, has the protein folding problem finally been solved?",W2087729059; W2107696655; W2109476464; W2974149524; W2995502823; W3005146385; W3094492244; W3177828909; W3183475563; W3211795435; W4205620281; W4210805759; W4221068086; W4283642176; W4319723617; W4321354559,4,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
42317196,"Perceptions, Attitudes, and Expectations of Surgical Patients Toward Artificial Intelligence–Supported Nursing Care: A Mixed Methods Study",10.1177/10436596261457098,,,,,,Songül Güngör; Kezban Koraş Sözen; Gülseren Maraş Baydoğan,W7165195079,"INTRODUCTION: Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly influencing nursing and surgical care. Although AI-supported systems may improve efficiency, they also raise ethical and humanistic concerns, highlighting the importance of patient perspectives. METHODS: An explanatory sequential mixed methods design was used. Quantitative data were collected from 312 postoperative surgical patients to assess knowledge and attitudes toward AI-supported care. Based on these results, 15 participants were selected for in-depth qualitative interviews using purposive sampling. Descriptive qualitative content analysis was applied, and the findings were integrated. RESULTS: Patients' attitudes toward AI were generally moderate and cautious. Most participants reported limited knowledge and perceived AI as a media-driven and abstract concept. Major concerns included loss of human touch, system errors, data security, and trust. AI was viewed as a supportive tool rather than a replacement for nursing care. DISCUSSION: Acceptance of AI-supported nursing care is shaped by cultural values, trust, human-centered expectations, requiring empathy and professional oversight.",W2146024835; W2908201961; W2942858056; W3003419678; W3028447429; W3111254667; W3183372445; W3186209406; W3193549799; W4282034224; W4290607622; W4299627282; W4387359174; W4405527741; W4406081553; W4407224756; W4407766994; W4410391070; W4410988545; W4414773325; W4415606315,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
29345678,China needs to listen to its researchers to become a scientific superpower,10.1038/d41586-018-00659-5,,,,,,,W4234128651,"The country’s research could soon dominate the world stage, but pitfalls lie in wait.",,1,2018,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
26135432,Machine ethics: The robot’s dilemma,10.1038/523024a,,,,,,Boer Deng,W1569397376,This video explains how to build ethical robots with real-time decision-making capabilities and shows how simple and easy it is to design and build ethical machines.,W100785191; W1221428646; W2000343360; W2133460105,88,2015,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
40090590,"Augmented Intelligence and Dermatology - Part II: Bias, Benchmarks, Guidelines, Ethics, Regulation, and Future Directions",10.1016/j.jaad.2024.10.132,,,,,,Edwin Dovigi; Shannon Wongvibulsin; Ivy Lee; Rob Novoa; Zhuo Cai; Veronica Rotemberg; Roxana Daneshjou,W4408462493,"Despite the proliferation of artificial intelligence (AI) tools, numerous challenges remain in the development and evaluation of AI-related technologies. Furthermore, a practice gap exists in how to responsibly implement AI effectively, ethically, and legally in dermatology. Part 2 of this CME review series will explain the key technical challenges and principles of trustworthy and responsible AI as well as describe the ethical challenges and review resources to help guide safe, effective, and ethical AI in dermatology. This content will empower learners to critically analyze these tools and determine whether an AI solution is appropriate for their specific clinical need, patient, and practice setting.",,1,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
30920688,Self‐driving cars and AI‐assisted endoscopy: Who should take the responsibility when things go wrong?,10.1111/jgh.14641,,,,,,Nicholas CH Poon; Joseph JY Sung,W2930683642,"The era of artificial intelligence (AI) revolution has arrived. Self-driving vehicles (SDVs) are no longer science fictions but are already on the street of Silicon Valley. In 2020, Japan is likely to approve the use of AI-assisted endoscopy (AIE) to make diagnosis of gastrointestinal lesions. When someone dash out into the highway and hit by an SDV, or when a malignant polyp is misdiagnosed as benign by AIE and left untreated, who should take the responsibility: the driver sitting inside the SDV, the endoscopist holding the instrument, or the company that develops and markets these technologies? There is a famous scenario in SDV: two kids on skate-board dashed into back ally while an SDV is incoming. The algorithm of SDV can direct the car to divert to opposite lane and crash with another vehicle injuring the innocent driver there, or ran over the two kids. Which is the better outcome and who should be held responsible? In the endoscopy room, an AIE directed the endoscopist to remove a polyp which was machine assessed as malignant yet complicated by bleeding after polypectomy. In another incident, a malignant polyp which needs to be taken out was ignored. Who should be blamed for the misdiagnosis and adverse outcome? Application of artificial intelligence technology in gastrointestinal endoscopy, specifically deep learning and neural networking, allows almost instantaneous differentiation of adenoma versus hyperplastic polyps in the colon, and perhaps in foreseeable future, gastritis from gastric atrophy. This technology is already mature enough to be put on the market for daily use. In the future, endoscopists will rely on the little screen by the side of the endoscopic picture, with flashing signal and a beeping sound to indicate the likelihood of the existence of an adenoma or just a hyperplastic polyp. Cybernet Systems Co., Ltd has announced that EndoBRAIN—AI that supports optical diagnosis of colorectal polyps—was approved by PMDA (Pharmaceuticals and Medical Devices Agency) in Japan. This is just the beginning. Currently, medical robots that perform sophisticated surgical procedures have little autonomy and decision-making authority of the operation. Gastroenterologists and surgeons have control over and can override the judgement made by AI-assisted endoscopes or surgical robots. Naturally, the liability rests upon the operator (the gastroenterologist or the surgeon). However, scientists and engineers envisage progressive advancement of AI-assisted procedure will emerge from no autonomy/robot assistant, to task autonomy or conditional autonomy and, eventually, full automation. As clinical operations become increasingly automated, the notion that clinicians are in control and are therefore responsible for misdiagnoses or adverse events becomes more strained. With AI taking over clinical decision-making process and surgical operations, is it still fair to ask the clinicians to shoulder all the responsibilities and liability in the event of adverse outcome.Technology is advancing faster than regulatory, ethical, and legal framework. Yet risk management in this settings will be quite a paradigm shift. This is the time to get ourselves prepared in the dawn of AI-assisted medicine.",W2598070036,10,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
36261566,The unseen Black faces of AI algorithms,10.1038/d41586-022-03050-7,,,,,,Abeba Birhane,W4306846241,"An audit of commercial facial-analysis tools found that dark-skinned faces are misclassified at a much higher rate than are faces from any other group. Four years on, the study is shaping research, regulation and commercial practices. Pivotal study of facial recognition algorithms revealed racial bias.",W2761974878; W2952272238; W2962059918; W3128525775; W3166488533; W3199665477,51,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
