34466625,"Artificial Intelligence, Important Assistant of Scientists and Physicians",10.31661/gmj.v9i0.2048,,,,,,Aliasghar Karimi; Hamed HaddadPajouh,W3102342516,"T human mind has several obstacles and limitations to remember and apply the thousands of medical information learned at medical school quickly. On the other hand, knowledge of medicine is proliferating. Hence, the analysis of the hundreds of papers, journals, and textbooks are impossible for a clinician. However, in evidence-based medical practice, physicians must be used recent guidelines and papers. Due to a report, most diagnostic errors in medical care are related to the wrong cognitive by health care workers [1, 2]. Also, medical errors are one of the significant causes of death in the United States that most related to human errors [3, 4]. Since the 1950s, physicians and computer scientists have tried to use the capacity of computers as a decision support system to facilitate clinical decisions [5]. Artificial intelligence (AI) is a branch of computer sciences rapidly being adopted in medicine and many other fields. AI technology tries to mimic human behaviors and cognitive functions to be learning and solve a problem similar to a human [6]. Since AI uses computer capabilities in processing and memory management, it does not have human limitations to analyze and interpret millions of information about disorders in less than a minute. In 1976, for the first usage of AI in medicine, Gunn et al. used this technology to diagnose acute abdominal pain [7]. the usage of AI in medicine was in medical algorithms to help physicians diagnose a disease between different diagnoses. Today, AI agents are significantly developed in different environments. The common AI technology is image processing and computer vision that are useful for analyzing and classifying the radiologic or microscopic images. Therefore, AI assists the measurements and diagnosis could be more convenient and faster for radiologists and pathologists. The other AI technologies are Artificial neural networks (ANN), Machine learning, Convolutional neural network (CNN), and Deep learning that are useful to other medical specialists [8]. on the other hand, the AI has a significant role in preventing and managing chronic diseases such as diabetes mellitus [9]. Furthermore, developing smart gadgets and wearable devices that use AI has a beneficial impact on preventing, diagnosing, predicting prognosis, and managing non-communicable diseases [10]. The usage of this technology is growing up every day; however, there are some ethical concerns about apply AI in medicine [11]. on the other hand, there is not enough evidence about the proficiency of AI in medical practice. So, several high-quality studies must be done to understand the pros and cons of AI usage in medicine. Also, several websites and software work with AI to help clinicians and researchers interpret and analyze the papers. However, the AI could be more useful to scientific writ-",W883893393; W2141222241; W2141874256; W2177939541; W2346834216; W2464288150; W2804354698; W2916559661; W2959065917; W3016864907,13,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
31804731,Conversations on the nature of reality,10.1111/nyas.14287,,,,,,Richard Rass,W2994177370,"The eighth consecutive season in the ongoing collaboration between the Nour Foundation and the New York Academy of Sciences set its sights on a conundrum that has both preoccupied and eluded human understanding for millennia: What is the relationship between conscious, subjective experience—what we see, hear, smell, taste, and think—and the physical world that pervades us? Is the reality of the physical world constructed through our subjective experience, or does the physical world we perceive have an objective reality beyond the observer? To date, no scientific theory has succeeded in unifying consciousness (that is, conscious experience) and what we take to be the physical world. To better grasp the breadth and complexity of the topic at hand, we invited some of the world’s leading scientists and thinkers to present their novel ideas and working hypotheses on the fundamental nature of reality. Moderated by Steve Paulson, executive producer of Wisconsin Public Radio’s To the Best of Our Knowledge, each of the sessions in this three-part series featured two prominent speakers who shared how their personal experiences and stories have come to shape their current thinking and work around this problem in their respective fields of expertise. The first of the three panel discussions, “The Mystery of Our Mathematical Universe,” explored why mathematics has proven so “unreasonably effective,” as Nobel laureate physicist Eugene Wigner put it, in explaining the laws of nature that govern our universe. Theoretical physicist S. James Gates Jr. and science writer Margaret Wertheim joined forces to shed light on why fundamental laws discovered through pure mathematics—from the law of gravitation to Maxwell’s electromagnetic equations—describe the behavior of our physical world with such remarkable precision. In particular, the speakers expressed their original and somewhat provocative views on whether the mathematical properties of our physical universe reveal a hidden order embedded in the natural world. In the second panel, “Human Cognition and the AI Revolution,” the focus of the conversation shifted from the realm of mathematics to the nature and essence of human understanding. Logician and mathematician Roger Antonsen teamed up with Harvard computer science pioneer Barbara J. Grosz to explain how superintelligent machines are challenging our current ideas about cognition, reality, and the limits of humanunderstanding.While lauding the exciting possibilities offered by tremendous advances in the field of artificial intelligence, the speakers expressed some reservation as to whether AI will someday succeed in “knowledge engineering” that would enable machines to share some of our cognitive abilities rather than to merely imitate the results of our thinking. In the final panel, “Reality Is Not as It Seems,” cognitive scientist Donald D. Hoffman and British neurologist and best-selling author Suzanne O’Sullivan tackled the enduring mind–body problem—namely, whether neural activity in the brain constitutes the foundation of our reality, with consciousness emerging from the dynamics of complicated neural networks. Challenging the prevalent view that we construct our perception of reality in real time, Hoffman presented a novel theory positing that what we perceive with our brain and physical senses does not, in fact, reflect the true nature of reality, but rather only a version of reality that has been shaped by evolutionary forces to guide adaptive behavior. Thus, while we should",,0,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
31312167,Artificial Intelligence: Does Consciousness Matter?,10.3389/fpsyg.2019.01535,,,,,,Elisabeth Hildt,W2953884621,"OPINION article Front. Psychol., 02 July 2019Sec. Theoretical and Philosophical Psychology Volume 10 - 2019 | https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.01535",W638211264; W1509643880; W1533180338; W1638773224; W2017685107; W2088106615; W2116547831; W2164446969; W2251410821; W2339027962; W2520111737; W2533137496; W2539563437; W2550953130; W2742760489; W2765831433; W2795593767; W2800789377; W2885648612; W4251136349; W4389412426,174,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
