39611808,Stop Training Artificial Intelligence Algorithms Now. Start Prospective Trials!,10.1093/jbi/wbae083,,,,,,Robert M. Nishikawa; Alisa Sumkin,W4404873464,"Artificial intelligence (AI) for breast imaging, and as applied to screening mammography, has made major technical advances. Some AI tools now have performance matching that of the average radiologist.1 With this high performance, AI detection tools are being proposed for triaging screening cases to reduce radiologists’ workload2 and, for centers that practice double reading, replacing 1 of the 2 radiologists.3 Going beyond that, AI tools that can estimate the risk of a woman developing breast cancer have been developed that are vastly more accurate than existing models currently being used clinically.4 And, as described in this issue of Journal of Breast Imaging by Pacilè et al, AI now can streamline radiologists’ dictation by automatically determining lesion type and location.5 Furthermore, they showed that this can reduce variability among radiologists. The article by Pacilè et al further demonstrates technological improvements in AI detection tools with the addition of combining results on the 2D mammogram and the 3D digital breast tomosynthesis (DBT) scan marking the corresponding lesion in both the mammogram and all the sections in the DBT scan.5 Furthermore, the algorithm incorporates analysis of prior mammograms, which can improve the performance of an AI algorithm.6 These refinements incrementally improve the performance of AI detection systems and will eventually lead to systems that rival of an expert breast radiologist in accuracy.",W2089887167; W2766636512; W2964642168; W2966665347; W3080997487; W3213232584; W4205557412; W4308509744; W4362469562; W4377564079; W4385448527; W4386533578; W4388728630; W4396730518; W4399326459; W4401578317; W4404793863,1,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
