38228351,Generative artificial intelligence can have a role in combating vaccine hesitancy,10.1136/bmj.q69,,,,,,Heidi Jane Larson; Leesa K. Lin,W4390910445,"Artificial intelligence has potential to counter vaccine hesitancy while building trust in vaccines, but it must be deployed ethically and responsibly, argue Heidi Larson and Leesa Lin",W2116926372; W3000970298; W3087125241; W4206236567; W4282833194; W4294839358; W4309702579; W4309821742; W4310780108; W4323314501; W4362522403; W4366588626; W4367310920; W4378232676; W4379932147; W4386766469; W4387583347; W4388627527; W4389816534,22,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
31670722,Role of Artificial Intelligence in Cardiovascular Imaging.,10.14744/AnatolJCardiol.2019.93727,,,,,,Nurgül Keser,W2982081668,,,1,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
42384579,Mapping Current Use of Artificial Intelligence in Pharmacology Education via a Scoping Review,10.1002/prp2.70290,,,,,,Werner Cordier; Princess Ijeoma,W7166876189,"Pharmacology education, often reputed as complex, overtly didactic and decontextualised, may benefit from artificial intelligence-supported strategies. However, current guidance is fragmented across disciplines and contexts, thus weakening evidence-based curricular implementation. This scoping review mapped existing research to identify applications, strengths, limitations, and areas for future development. A double-blinded screening process facilitated by Covidence yielded 17 eligible studies from four databases. Studies mostly comprised cross-sectional studies from high-income countries in the medical context, with generative artificial intelligence being predominant (ChatGPT-3.5 and ChatGPT-4.0). Research comprised assessment (n = 12), paedagogy (n = 4), curriculum design (n = 1), and programme evaluation (n = 1). Most studies assessed tools' ability to answer examinations, with mixed success depending on the version, question type, and inclusion of context in prompt engineering. Few studies incorporated students, limiting insights into learning impact. Zero-shot prompting was mostly used, limited further by unclear design frameworks, which may bias outcomes considering downstream inefficiencies. Current research prioritises the performance of artificial intelligence, rather than its integration or impact in learning, which reduces its applicability for curriculum design and competency development. Although promising, the impact is limited, requiring clearer instructional design and rationalisation within the education ecosystem. Although there is considerable potential for pharmacology education, research requires greater structure, longitudinal design, and incorporation of students to inform clear impact. Purposeful, context-aligned implementation and continuous evaluation are needed to ensure ethical, valid, and meaningful use in pharmacology education. To support future research, recommendations are provided for practical reporting, scientific design, and impact measurement.",W1499368215; W2137233656; W2155787878; W2772541385; W3017131514; W3197659619; W3198304219; W3217134098; W4210729650; W4304943299; W4310775480; W4322758367; W4324020772; W4377220156; W4383818129; W4386322180; W4388624829; W4388822338; W4390229778; W4390701140; W4390907288; W4391890163; W4392198021; W4392501426; W4393410497; W4394994020; W4395002516; W4395666858; W4399994575; W4400383276; W4400906741; W4401666625; W4402354394; W4402540843; W4402739132; W4403046349; W4403501444; W4403784981; W4403906927; W4406200047; W4406871476; W4407498832; W4415303556; W4416250092; W4417125483,1,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
40988146,Reliability of AI-generated responses on frequently-posed questions by patients with chronic kidney disease,10.1177/14604582251381996,,,,,,Emi Furukawa; Tsuyoshi Okuhara; Hiroko Okada; Yuriko Nishiie; Takahiro Kiuchi,W4414484155,"Background AI tools are becoming primary information sources for patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD). However, as AI sometimes generates factual or inaccurate information, the reliability of information must be assessed. Methods This study assessed the AI-generated responses to frequently asked questions on CKD. We entered Japanese prompts with top CKD-related keywords into ChatGPT, Copilot, and Gemini. The Quality Analysis of Medical Artificial Intelligence (QAMAI) tool was used to evaluate the reliability of the information. Results We included 207 AI responses from 23 prompts. The AI tools generated reliable information, with a median QAMAI score of 23 (interquartile range: 7) out of 30. However, information accuracy and resource availability varied (median (IQR): ChatGPT versus Copilot versus Gemini = 18 (2) versus 25 (3) versus 24 (5), p < 0.01). Among AI tools, ChatGPT provided the least accurate information and did not provide any resources. Conclusion The quality of AI responses on CKD was generally acceptable. While most information provided was reliable and comprehensive, some information lacked accuracy and references.",W1990637701; W2123109647; W3005957464; W4309674289; W4327946446; W4362521774; W4367310920; W4380423243; W4385380523; W4386191831; W4386757039; W4387950352; W4390771646; W4391443824; W4392777592; W4393866602; W4396639153; W4399276821; W4404339170; W4404341571,2,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
30120573,Conclusion: harmonisation in genomic and health data sharing for research: an impossible dream?,10.1007/s00439-018-1924-x,,,,,,David M. R. Townend,W2885926752,"There are clear benefits from genomics and health data sharing in research and in therapy for individuals across societies. At the same time, citizens have different expectations and fears about that data sharing. International legislation in relation with research ethics and practice and, particularly, data protection create a particular environment that, as is seen in the articles in part two of this special issue, are crying out for harmonisation both at a procedural but at fundamental conceptual levels. The law of data sharing is pulling in different directions. This paper poses the question, 'harmonisation, an impossible dream?' and the answer is a qualified 'no'. The paper reflects on what can be seen in the papers in part two of the special issue. It then identifies three major areas of conceptual uncertainty in the new EU General Data Protection Regulation (not because it has superiority over other jurisdictions, but because it is a recent revision of data protection law that leaves universal conceptual questions unclear). Thereafter, the potential for Artificial Intelligence to meet some of the shortcomings is discussed. The paper ends with a consideration of the conditions under which data sharing harmonisation might be achieved: an understanding of a human rights approach and citizen sensitivities in considering the 'public interest'; social liberalism as a basis of solidarity; and the profession of 'researcher'.",W1930692192; W2054580590; W2137395817; W2302501749; W2336107943; W2532417081; W2582566416; W2614473624; W2773787581; W2786753391; W2798596644; W2885437164; W2885812235; W2887921957; W4230197593; W4292528167,45,2018,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
42525862,AI in the OR: Ethics and the Evolving Role of Surgeons,10.2196/107619,,,,,,Jenna Congdon,W7171726874,"Unlabelled: AI is rapidly entering the operating room, both as a clinical tool and as increasingly autonomous systems. In this News and Perspectives article, JMIR Correspondent and intensive care unit nurse Jenna Congdon reports on the ethical implications and evolving role of human surgeons.",,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
42394058,A Conceptual Framework for Integrated and Collaborative Orthodontic AI,10.3233/SHTI260896,,,,,,Lucas Krenmayr,W7167104366,"Artificial Intelligence (AI) has improved orthodontic tasks, but clinical adoption remains fragmented. This paper presents a multi-layered framework that combines privacy-preserving data infrastructure, multimodal intelligence, and human-AI collaboration into one coherent system. Its main contribution is a structured design blueprint that integrates isolated AI tools into a clinically deployable ecosystem while addressing governance, integration, and trust.",,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
40693529,Artificial intelligence and the death of the academic author,10.1080/0142159X.2025.2523463,,,,,,Ken Masters,W4412554149,"In his essay 'The Death of the Author,' Roland Barthes describes the fiction author as a blender of cultural artifacts. This paper identifies parallels between that activity and the actions currently performed by Large Language Models (LLMs). It then goes further to argue that, in Health Professions Education academia (indeed, all academia), authorship is a mechanical role ideally suited to LLMs, and should be given to LLMs, freeing academic researchers to focus on the actual creative and contributive portion of any work: the research.",W3133702157; W4318263917; W4392187992; W4404986218,13,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
40353930,Artificial intelligence for enhanced classification of intraoperative complications in urology,10.1007/s00345-025-05623-3,,,,,,Zine-Eddine Khene; Frédéric Panthier; Lucas Bento; Alexandre de La Taille,W4410283203,,W3093404842; W3176877408; W4205460858; W4390096279; W4407748669,0,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39176796,Designing a Social Snus Cessation Mobile Application with an Integrated AI Function,10.3233/SHTI240467,,,,,,Alba Puyuelo Citoler; Eunji Lee,W4401822696,"The application of artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare is expected to be increased in the coming years. There has been little attention paid on exploring how social aspects and AI can be integrated into mobile applications to support the people who want to quit snus. Our research aims to design a prototype of a social snus cessation mobile application for university students in Sweden, incorporating an AI function. This paper presents a work-in-progress of our research.",W2122328334; W3007880119; W3035566364; W3114711190; W4361008179; W4386479528; W4387970754,1,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39159825,"Upholding Artificial Intelligence Transparency in Ophthalmology: A Call for Collaboration between Academia, Industry, and Government for Patient Care in the 21st Century.",10.1016/j.apjo.2024.100093,,,,,,Alexandra Thaler; Joshua Ong; Lama A. Al-Aswad,W4401662348,,W3089653673; W3128076801; W3136552952; W3136960503; W3150285870; W3192447662; W4229063350; W4282003828; W4381716616,3,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38131389,A new era is emerging at scientific user facilities,10.1107/S2052252523010928,,,,,,D. N. Argyriou,W4390103598,"Global scientific exchange has been profoundly perturbed by the COVID-19 pandemic, altering user travel behaviours and accelerating the use of remote access. Combined with the advent of artificial intelligence (AI), these trends together can change how large-scale user scientific facilities are used by the community and managed by operators.",,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38073939,Harnessing Artificial Intelligence for Qualitative and Mixed Methods in Nursing Research,10.7759/cureus.48570,,,,,,Abdulqadir J. J. Nashwan; Hana J Abukhadijah,W4388523391,"This editorial discusses the transformative potential of artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language models (LLMs), in enhancing traditional nursing research methodologies, specifically qualitative and mixed methods. The article emphasizes the benefits of AI such as LLMs in data processing, analysis, integration, and triangulation while also underscoring the importance of addressing ethical concerns and the need for proper training for researchers.",W1976113344; W2067652191; W2396848064; W2518758262; W2810914538; W2960067330; W2995660892; W3144293453; W3207360334; W4200440018; W4381095083; W4385380683,9,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37804932,Assessing the ability of an artificial intelligence chatbot to translate dermatopathology reports into patient-friendly language: a cross-sectional study.,10.1016/j.jaad.2023.09.072,,,,,,Yuying Zhang; Ryan Chen; Dan Nguyen; Stephanie Choi; Colleen Gabel; Nicholas Leonard; Kaitlyn Yim; Patrick O'Donnell; Zendee Elaba; April Deng; Nikki A. Levin,W4387358868,,W4361289889; W4383315757; W4387356888,22,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37578157,Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Nanomedicine. What Do We Expect for 2030?,10.2217/nnm-2023-0084,,,,,,João Paulo Figueiró Longo; Paulo Eduardo Narcizo de Souza; Paulo César Morais,W4385798953,,W2087773447; W2889540447; W3039289988; W3040766129; W3153418506; W3202221626; W4213189228; W4322617256; W4322720178; W4323304766; W4323347737; W4360849792,13,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37458097,How can artificial intelligence be utilized for de novo drug design against COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2)?,10.1080/17460441.2023.2236930,,,,,,Giuseppe Floresta; Chiara Zagni; Vincenzo Patamia; Antonio Rescifina,W4384493042,"The COVID-19 virus was first identified in Wuhan, China, in December 2019. On 11 March 2020, the World Health Organization declared the COVID-19 virus to be a pandemic. It continues to be a major worry for humanity today. The fact that a virus-based illness can generate a wide range of symptoms and disease consequences is challenging for the doctor to handle. The virus typically causes common influenza-like symptoms (cough, fever, and lethargy) or goes unnoticed. However, in 10–20% of patients, the inflammation leads to more severe illnesses that, at the beginning of 2022, killed millions of people worldwide [1]. The pandemic has greatly increased the importance and effect of research into developing novel antiviral treatments, cures, and vaccines. Time is a crucial factor in this arena. Unfortunately, developing an entirely new approved pharmaceutical is time-consuming and expensive. From 2000 to 2015, each approved molecule was estimated to cost over USD 2.5 billion! Only 15% of chosen substances tested on people are safe, effective, and can be utilized. However, it typically takes up to 15 years to develop a single medicine before it can be released [2]. Humanity cannot possibly wait 15 years to discover a new drug to cure COVID-19; in this instance, computational chemistry is being used to speed up research. Since November 2022, with the first release of ChatGPT, society has managed to handle the power of Artificial Intelligence (AI); the news immediately triggered a public discussion after it was revealed. From the editorial world to creative works, AI is here to stay and to revolutionize the way we think and work. Nevertheless, despite being tangible for a few months to all the civilized world, AI had made its way to the real world since 1956, when it was founded as an academic discipline [3]. To put the working mechanism in a nutshell, AI describes devices that behave like humans. Machines perform activities like speech recognition, learning, and problem-solving in AI. Machines can function and behave just like people if given enough training and information. AI can be categorized into four basic types based on the machine’s capacity to predict future actions based on the past: 1. Machine Learning – This is an application of AI in which machines automatically learn through training rather than being explicitly programmed to carry out tasks. A branch of machine learning called ‘Deep Learning’ uses artificial neural networks for predictive analysis. 2. Natural Language Processing (NLP), the interaction of computer systems with human language. 3. Automation and Robotics – Automation aims to have machines perform boring and repetitive jobs. 4. Machine Vision – Machines can capture and analyze visual data [4]. In both industry and academia, applications of AI have been employed to solve particular challenges. AI is a general-purpose technology with many uses, like electricity or computers. Of course, AI has also impacted the way we design drugs. The scientific community worldwide is constantly creating new technologies and algorithms to find helpful hit compounds quickly and at a lower cost overall in computeraided drug development (CADD). Today, the adoption of AI and all related techniques, including deep learning (DL), machine learning (ML), and other traditional computational chemistry tools, has demonstrated a considerable impact on the speed of innovative pharmaceutical identification [5]. A wide variety of algorithms with improved prediction skills can be integrated into new ways using AI and traditional CADD tools, either separately or in combination. On different sides, AI algorithms can analyze vast amounts of data, including genetic information, biological pathways, and chemical structures, to identify potential drug candidates. By leveraging machine learning and data mining techniques, AI can efficiently sift through extensive databases, identifying patterns and relationships that would be nearly impossible for humans to detect. Furthermore, AI plays a crucial role in optimizing the drug design process, particularly: Predictive Modeling, Virtual Screening, De Novo Drug Design, Optimization and Lead Optimization, Data Integration, and Knowledge Discovery (Figure 1). The rapid development of novel compounds active against COVID-19 is required due to the mutability of the virus, the lack of total protection from vaccination or potent medications for the treatment of infection, and the death rate; CADD is seen to be an important technique to accomplish this [6,7].",W2951934944; W2992586577; W3013998503; W3042711950; W3115655654; W3120408379; W3127493072; W3176005660; W3186504953; W3197107524; W3212119853; W3213042299; W4220776658; W4294733399; W4303043294; W4327946446; W4367843775,2,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37407364,Pearls and pitfalls of ChatGPT in medical oncology,10.1016/j.trecan.2023.06.007,,,,,,Jacob Blum; Arjun K. Menta; Xiyu Zhao; Victor B. Yang; Mohamed A. Gouda; Vivek Subbiah,W4383105835,"Recently, ChatGPT has drawn attention to the potential uses of artificial intelligence (AI) in academia. Here, we discuss how ChatGPT can be of value to medicine and medical oncology and the potential pitfalls that may be encountered.",W2967598999; W4292779060; W4297253404; W4313384047; W4319662928; W4361289889; W6778883912,19,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37387092,AI Techniques to Identify Nerve Cell Alterations in Digital Images,10.3233/SHTI230558,,,,,,Ilias Argyros; Emmanouil Zoulias; Joseph Liaskos; Antonios Stamatakis; John Mantas,W4382630828,"Artificial intelligence (AI), utilising computing power, has managed to influence the health sector with many applications based on algorithms, tools, and automated processes. In this work, neuronbiological images acquired by an electronic microscope are assessed under appropriate image processing to identify areas of interest. The algorithmic steps identified the alterations of nerve cells that appeared as areas in the red channel of each digital image.",W1524992328; W1997866278; W2167279371,0,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
32840141,Latest Advancements in Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Technologies in Treating Type 1 Diabetes,10.1177/1932296820949940,,,,,,Feng Qian; Patrick J. Schumacher,W3080857735,"Type 1 diabetes (T1D) is an autoimmune chronic disease characterized by absolute insulin deficiency, which causes abnormal blood glucose regulation. Currently, there are 1.25 million Americans diagnosed with T1D with approximately 64 000 new cases of T1D being diagnosed every year.1,2 There is no cure for T1D and patients with T1D rely on daily insulin administration. So, self-management of T1D is crucial to prevent or delay major complications. However, only a very small proportion of T1D patients have met the target range set by the American Diabetes Association.3 Recent years have witnessed an explosion of artificial intelligence (AI)-enabled and data-empowered innovations in treating T1D patients. For instance, closedloop systems (ie, “artificial pancreas”) and insulin pumps are reported to effectively improve T1D management as well as patient outcomes. To summarize the latest research in AI-enabled T1D treatment, we conducted a literature review with PubMed and Google Scholar to retrieve publications in peer-reviewed healthcare journals from January 2019 to May 2020 (see Table 1). Overall, continuous improvements in safety, effectiveness, efficiency, and patient satisfaction using closed-loop systems to control blood glucose level in a broader T1D population (especially pediatric patients) were reported. The newest evidence was derived from various types of studies ranging from well-designed multicenter randomized clinical trial with six-month follow-up on closedloop system to comparative study between real-time and intermittent-scanning continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) and to cost-effectiveness study on a hybrid closed-loop system compared with a continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion system.4-6 Notably, machine learning techniques applied in CGMs were reported to lead to significant reductions in postprandial hypoglycemia, decreased rates of closed-loop exits and alerts, improved glycemic control without detectable changes in psychosocial outcomes, and accurate prediction and prevention of nocturnal hypoglycemia. Also, the autonomous diagnostic AI system demonstrated superior sensitivity and specificity for diagnosing referable diabetic retinopathy and macular edema. Additionally, higher glycated hemoglobin level and workload required to use hybrid closed loop were suggested to be the significant factors associated with discontinuation. In a nutshell, there have been remarkable progress in AI-enabled technology in treating T1D reported in literature since 2019. Yet, practical challenges and opportunities exist. Whether the observed benefits will sustain for prolonged use in real-world settings and how AI technologies will protect patient data privacy and security are crucial questions to address. During the past few years, personalized data, machine learning (in particular, deep learning and convolutional neural network techniques), and realtime adaptive algorithms combined with wearable devices have improved prevention and early detection, clinical decision support, deterioration risk prediction, and patient self-management of T1D. Future research endeavors and technological innovations should meet patients’ needs such as mitigating user’s workload and further enhancing outcomes, optimizing data protection, and realizing patientcentered care. Unquestionably, AI, Big Data, interdisciplinary research, and global collaboration will play an increasingly important role in achieving precision medicine for T1D patients. 949940 DSTXXX10.1177/1932296820949940Journal of Diabetes Science and TechnologyQian et al letter2020",W2768019234; W2905988917; W2909102766; W2917355348; W2947163799; W2949554208; W2980281598; W2980370581; W2981310284; W2995085977; W2995505400; W3010682727; W3016344200,7,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
32223349,How could artificial intelligence aid in the fight against coronavirus?,10.1080/14787210.2020.1744275,,,,,,Hadi Mohamad Yassine; Zubair Shah,W3013697951,1. Dr Hadi:Artificial intelligence (AI) is one of the means or avenues to understand the virus and develop preventative and control measures. This includes but is not limited to: the usage of mathe...,W2011692260; W2160544378; W2603954265; W2790166049; W3008047170,33,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
29686578,"Anesthesiology, automation, and artificial intelligence",10.1080/08998280.2017.1391036,,,,,,John C. Alexander; Girish P. Joshi,W2773320514,"There have been many attempts to incorporate automation into the practice of anesthesiology, though none have been successful. Fundamentally, these failures are due to the underlying complexity of anesthesia practice and the inability of rule-based feedback loops to fully master it. Recent innovations in artificial intelligence, especially machine learning, may usher in a new era of automation across many industries, including anesthesiology. It would be wise to consider the implications of such potential changes before they have been fully realized.",W603776396; W641505739; W1869673055; W1990323120; W2037712511; W2046061050; W2283522127; W2317084106; W2317422852; W2319382533; W2322826186; W2334256660; W2362587878; W2483698312; W2493756966,40,2017,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
31337656,Artificial Intelligence Systems Assisting Oncologists? Resist and Desist or Enlist and Coexist,10.1634/theoncologist.2019-0267,,,,,,Jacob J. Adashek; Ishwaria Mohan Subbiah; Vivek Subbiah,W2964285313,"The use of artificial intelligence (AI) has become an integral part of patient care, but there are concerns about the impact of non‐human decision assistance on patient outcomes. This commentary focuses on how AI can assist oncologists and benefit patients.",W2407107555; W2613716520; W2770203518; W2886281300; W2889450541; W2916528750; W2946185430; W2948930564; W4240338964; W4293280672,6,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41586511,Expectations and Limitations of Artificial Intelligence in Blood Cancer Diagnosis.,10.1158/2643-3230.BCD-25-0443,,,,,,Charlotte Syrykh; Sarah Bertoli; Jean-Marc Alliot; P. Brousset,W7125683914,"In this commentary, we open the debate on what can be expected from artificial intelligence (AI) in the diagnosis of hematologic cancers. We discuss the key factors that make AI solutions robust, trustworthy, and, above all, generalizable, with particular emphasis on the importance of dataset quality in shaping the performance and effectiveness of AI models.",W2015185311; W2760946358; W3021413349; W3120224790; W3136768428; W3164740578; W4311887602; W4317498947; W4388419173; W4393026481; W4396636942; W4398201291; W4403775133; W4408705738; W4415675959,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
42622965,Artificial intelligence in pituitary medicine: what should an endocrinologist know?,10.1007/s11102-026-01747-w,,,,,,Damla N. Costa; Rozalina G. McCoy; Debraj Mukherjee; Roberto Salvatori,W7203840632,"Pituitary disorders often include complex radiology imaging, rare clinical presentations, and need for multidisciplinary decision-making and prolonged follow-up, all features that make them a natural target for artificial intelligence (AI). The resulting literature is expanding rapidly, but carries an unfamiliar vocabulary and set of methods, which can leave clinicians without a clear entry point. This short perspective offers such an entry point, succinctly outlining how AI learns from data, how a clinical AI application moves from question to deployment, providing examples, and addressing how to read the field critically.",W1576777764; W2165331022; W2900524248; W2966938302; W2997845929; W3012582382; W3139021473; W3201930040; W4213150448; W4220716821; W4220855210; W4226020729; W4226078333; W4281657980; W4282834325; W4302307182; W4361270736; W4381716616; W4385362172; W4392817561; W4393023000; W4396916383; W4400460078; W4401422442; W4403140675; W4404020092; W4404027051; W4408428133; W4409155036; W4409538841; W4409650920; W4410851246; W4412543818; W4412822773; W4412823030; W4414161403; W4414606017; W4416202047; W7131890011,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39523271,"Artificial Intelligence, Radiomics, and Computational Modeling in Skull Base Surgery",10.1007/978-3-031-64892-2_16,,,,,,Eric Suero Molina; Antonio Di Ieva,W4404213331,"This chapter explores current artificial intelligence (AI), radiomics, and computational modeling applications in skull base surgery. AI advancements are providing opportunities to improve diagnostic accuracy, surgical planning, and postoperative care. Currently, computational models can assist in diagnosis, simulate surgical scenarios, and improve safety during surgical procedures by identifying critical structures. AI-powered technologies, such as liquid biopsy, machine learning, radiomic analysis, computer vision, and label-free optical imaging, aim to revolutionize skull base surgery. AI-driven advancements promise safer, more precise, and effective surgeries, improving patient outcomes and preoperative assessment.",W1501474050; W1900291629; W2063552084; W2074583773; W2076285703; W2266464013; W2746574162; W2810024032; W2900843314; W2910928559; W2953413445; W2972424085; W2986300209; W2992511782; W2998669042; W2998763451; W3014970872; W3031638321; W3032019872; W3032194126; W3034035069; W3049712949; W3091812914; W3095596710; W3124164798; W3126339458; W3133847634; W3136180988; W3166087548; W3174943263; W3175665049; W3187426677; W3191197587; W3197636907; W3201930040; W3202926319; W3206095354; W3208370483; W3213181131; W3215280536; W4205365810; W4205373255; W4205525897; W4205705455; W4210452173; W4213447255; W4214601041; W4220964251; W4221011526; W4221116684; W4223978218; W4224236903; W4225146467; W4225760208; W4226020729; W4280540611; W4283381652; W4283525732; W4283791101; W4288067784; W4294941868; W4360619428; W4366465252; W4376109912; W4378189371; W4379094518; W4385219651; W4385422236; W4387009643; W4390640259,3,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
36419010,Priorities for successful use of artificial intelligence by public health organizations: a literature review,10.1186/s12889-022-14422-z,,,,,,Stacey Fisher; Laura C. Rosella,W4309702579,"Artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to improve public health's ability to promote the health of all people in all communities. To successfully realize this potential and use AI for public health functions it is important for public health organizations to thoughtfully develop strategies for AI implementation. Six key priorities for successful use of AI technologies by public health organizations are discussed: 1) Contemporary data governance; 2) Investment in modernized data and analytic infrastructure and procedures; 3) Addressing the skills gap in the workforce; 4) Development of strategic collaborative partnerships; 5) Use of good AI practices for transparency and reproducibility, and; 6) Explicit consideration of equity and bias.",W429766147; W1467526212; W1978290918; W1985823426; W1994682257; W2015620729; W2015929254; W2024002488; W2035102984; W2119487756; W2123728527; W2147469877; W2174723599; W2211242807; W2235639886; W2302501749; W2345889990; W2483215953; W2515138377; W2755366291; W2765363580; W2781122472; W2787894218; W2790653501; W2795942236; W2806113561; W2886705558; W2892370045; W2898435545; W2902008863; W2913997948; W2937186836; W2945384117; W2946016028; W2952132030; W2953522645; W2962707339; W2973032093; W2973683152; W2980007581; W2980822619; W2981869278; W2988716771; W2989738939; W2990767007; W3000603264; W3014853910; W3025218691; W3030827488; W3032158497; W3036458852; W3039050487; W3084413139; W3087059313; W3087893815; W3097208481; W3106499542; W3115247823; W3116286104; W3159623667; W3165845449; W3212630367; W4206713405; W4211229738; W4220848513; W4225733838; W4240149506; W4301969137; W4313371821; W4385490920; W6767839947,180,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34790953,Artificial Intelligence in Education (AIEd): a high-level academic and industry note 2021,10.1007/s43681-021-00074-z,,,,,,Muhammad Ali Chaudhry; Emre Kazim,W3165783357,"In the past few decades, technology has completely transformed the world around us. Indeed, experts believe that the next big digital transformation in how we live, communicate, work, trade and learn will be driven by Artificial Intelligence (AI) [83]. This paper presents a high-level industrial and academic overview of AI in Education (AIEd). It presents the focus of latest research in AIEd on reducing teachers' workload, contextualized learning for students, revolutionizing assessments and developments in intelligent tutoring systems. It also discusses the ethical dimension of AIEd and the potential impact of the Covid-19 pandemic on the future of AIEd's research and practice. The intended readership of this article is policy makers and institutional leaders who are looking for an introductory state of play in AIEd.",W58813315; W789331148; W1511777619; W1525421687; W1526122805; W1977655452; W1983352506; W1995864528; W1997819184; W2000976551; W2024105943; W2026696399; W2034543389; W2046149904; W2063649921; W2069874839; W2075028344; W2092161214; W2109492167; W2112248565; W2117655204; W2122410182; W2130700601; W2154041216; W2154104231; W2156889164; W2158376170; W2162670686; W2187354526; W2248008177; W2257979135; W2275548225; W2294691030; W2339255380; W2463852393; W2511325517; W2543240265; W2582336686; W2585105539; W2588865267; W2589759783; W2714908099; W2742658477; W2746986659; W2752828033; W2769019609; W2782973222; W2783507141; W2789958057; W2801542955; W2803876593; W2806369404; W2823329128; W2831988194; W2889432122; W2897383465; W2898977791; W2901279185; W2903826588; W2905180035; W2999637955; W3017156287; W3017343191; W3044633941; W3046507749; W3085118377; W3092080391; W3099398637; W3103751997; W3108943181; W3126390659; W3127128771; W3128506960; W3133447322; W3137587579; W3199717448; W4212793374; W4231094922; W4235691657; W4249187927; W4298443378; W4396928770,383,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
32239350,Artificial Intelligence in Hematology: Current Challenges and Opportunities,10.1007/s11899-020-00575-4,,,,,,Nathan Radakovich; Matthew Nagy; Aziz Nazha,W3014679960,"Artificial intelligence (AI), and in particular its subcategory machine learning, is finding an increasing number of applications in medicine, driven in large part by an abundance of data and powerful, accessible tools that have made AI accessible to a larger circle of investigators. AI has been employed in the analysis of hematopathological, radiographic, laboratory, genomic, pharmacological, and chemical data to better inform diagnosis, prognosis, treatment planning, and foundational knowledge related to benign and malignant hematology. As more widespread implementation of clinical AI nears, attention has also turned to the effects this will have on other areas in medicine. AI offers many promising tools to clinicians broadly, and specifically in the practice of hematology. Ongoing research into its various applications will likely result in an increasing utilization of AI by a broader swath of clinicians.",W1990482377; W2131415585; W2148309496; W2177870565; W2265300136; W2528051702; W2602165918; W2609260042; W2610332124; W2618530766; W2618851150; W2619951717; W2745394387; W2760946358; W2766119845; W2768033041; W2777794149; W2782993895; W2788565855; W2788948370; W2793079232; W2803760365; W2807463161; W2809628680; W2839861321; W2855469196; W2883147591; W2884472849; W2888424632; W2892741787; W2896930435; W2903920099; W2905810301; W2907066882; W2908280849; W2911071704; W2913956055; W2914393040; W2917675508; W2918956129; W2919115771; W2942951865; W2947890016; W2950814274; W2951404625; W2953311188; W2953681063; W2954178930; W2958983506; W2962411443; W2963284126; W2964696298; W2966884791; W2972689037; W2973490577; W2974725190; W2975107127; W2975956387; W2982764014; W2986408762; W2987485492; W2988646312; W2989801115; W2993303538; W3004859565; W3005386144,54,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
30794129,Making Policy on Augmented Intelligence in Health Care,10.1001/amajethics.2019.188,,,,,,Elliott Crigger; Christopher Khoury,W2913847760,"In June 2018, the American Medical Association adopted new policy to provide a broad framework for the evolution of artificial intelligence (AI) in health care that is designed to help ensure that AI realizes the benefits it promises for patients, physicians, and the health care community. Responding to Industry Activity on Artificial IntelligenceIn June 2018, the American Medical Association (AMA) adopted a new policy, H-480.940,""Augmented Intelligence in Health Care,"" 1 to provide a broad framework for the evolution of artificial intelligence (AI) in health care that is designed to help ensure that AI realizes the benefits it promises for patients, physicians, and the health care community.In parallel, a wave of scientific and investment activity is cresting, focused on AI and its applications in health care and medicine.Advances in computing power, storage, sensors, and multidisciplinary research have laid the groundwork for the increased development and use of AI techniques in health care.AI is the ability of a computer to function appropriately and with foresight in its environment, that is, to complete tasks in a manner typically associated with a rational human being.Augmented intelligence is an alternative conceptualization that focuses on AI's assistive role, emphasizing a design approach and implementation that enhances human intelligence rather than replaces it.Collectively, these areas of scientific research and health care industry activity represent thousands of peer-reviewed studies and, by one estimate, over $2.7 billion in investment across 121 digital health companies and 206 funding deals between 2011 and 2017, just within the United States. 2 The background report that informs this new policy focused on 2 fundamental normative conditions for appropriate integration of AI into health care.3 First, health care AI should be understood as a tool to augment professional clinical judgment, not a technology to replace or override it.Second, the development of health care AI tools should attend carefully to the design and evaluation of individual applications, to issues of patient privacy, and to thoughtful clinical implementation.",W2616399473; W2789970635; W2913847760; W4293865127,82,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39001698,Current status and future directions in artificial intelligence for nuclear cardiology,10.1080/14779072.2024.2380764,,,,,,Robert J H Miller; Piotr J. Slomka,W4400608075,"INTRODUCTION: Myocardial perfusion imaging (MPI) is one of the most commonly ordered cardiac imaging tests. Accurate motion correction, image registration, and reconstruction are critical for high-quality imaging, but this can be technically challenging and has traditionally relied on expert manual processing. With accurate processing, there is a rich variety of clinical, stress, functional, and anatomic data that can be integrated to guide patient management. AREAS COVERED: PubMed and Google Scholar were reviewed for articles related to artificial intelligence in nuclear cardiology published between 2020 and 2024. We will outline the prominent roles for artificial intelligence (AI) solutions to provide motion correction, image registration, and reconstruction. We will review the role for AI in extracting anatomic data for hybrid MPI which is otherwise neglected. Lastly, we will discuss AI methods to integrate the wealth of data to improve disease diagnosis or risk stratification. EXPERT OPINION: There is growing evidence that AI will transform the performance of MPI by automating and improving on aspects of image acquisition and reconstruction. Physicians and researchers will need to understand the potential strengths of AI in order to benefit from the full clinical utility of MPI.",W135321714; W2032520632; W2048677675; W2088118463; W2088369476; W2091338891; W2118606707; W2121827537; W2127873596; W2156178549; W2334499091; W2345003174; W2611367247; W2765106267; W2789532252; W2790564346; W2894464394; W2901245852; W2905855569; W2921335396; W2946509953; W2947549751; W2962858109; W2964959245; W2971735020; W2980265608; W2989991226; W2996087518; W3012956770; W3019885855; W3034798037; W3038613716; W3089501259; W3092370246; W3093094186; W3125945929; W3126008863; W3134320777; W3139236016; W3144392514; W3157423176; W3160118309; W3165356463; W3175428929; W3177759260; W3178568195; W3180421545; W3180863822; W3186670325; W3207760730; W3212475206; W3212863100; W3215978285; W4213207180; W4220945215; W4220977779; W4221120819; W4225001162; W4229024388; W4281841850; W4282917204; W4283465653; W4284966265; W4289295773; W4293804742; W4294001699; W4295752815; W4295774775; W4296514530; W4296794342; W4301182844; W4301392237; W4302293118; W4303474922; W4305082717; W4306827332; W4308364833; W4308988932; W4309411036; W4310117627; W4313880113; W4322488802; W4322754683; W4323534775; W4323546622; W4324017548; W4362608384; W4365139917; W4365140000; W4367311891; W4367598055; W4376891758; W4383218413; W4384071683; W4385415452; W4386583706; W4387091693; W4387996367; W4390348160; W4390484694; W4390607161; W4391511766; W4392503039; W4393185616; W4393311713; W4393408403; W4394061301; W4394619856; W4396781314,4,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37670607,Digital dietetics and the era of artificial intelligence,10.1111/1747-0080.12841,,,,,,Margaret A Allman-Farinelli,W4386467000,"We are in the midst of yet another technological revolution with the growing use of artificial intelligence and augmented reality. The challenge for dietitians and nutritionists is to discover how dietetics practice can effectively engage with these technologies and those from the preceding revolution. The beginning of this century saw the launch of social media websites sharing information, such as Myspace, soon followed by Facebook, Reddit, Twitter and Pinterest and now joined by Instagram and Snapchat applications (apps) for image and video sharing. In 2007, the iPhone was launched by Apple Inc and was transformational in how we communicate with each other, and after the advent of apps, has changed our professional, educational, financial and leisure behaviours. In this issue of Nutrition & Dietetics, there are articles from dietitians researching and employing communication and information technologies in their research and practice.1-5 The potential of new information and communication technologies in healthcare has been increasingly realised. Digital health modalities allow for real-time two-way communications or asynchronous one-way exchanges via electronic health (eHealth) using computers, or mobile health (mHealth) using smartphones.6 Wearable sensors and videoconferencing are increasingly used, sensors combine with apps to self-monitor blood glucose concentrations, blood pressure and heart rate to name but a few. Three papers in the current issue concern social media.1-3 Blogs are one of the simplest ways to engage in social media and are essentially a website where information is communicated, regularly updated and opinions can be shared rather than the more usual static informational website. Mete and colleagues have researched the profile of a typical Australian adult who reads healthy eating blogs.1 They investigated what readers of healthy blogs look for in a blog and why other individuals do not read them. Of the 151 healthy eating blog sites identified by the 238 participants, 27% of them were written by health professionals and proved most popular with participants. The authors suggest that blogs are still a useful medium for dietitians to communicate and encouragingly some participants reported they changed the way they eat on recommendations of these blogs. However, only half the survey sample read healthy eating blogs. The usefulness of social media in dietetics practice goes beyond the blog as illustrated by Squires and colleagues.2 Being aware that dietitians are using Instagram to post food and nutrition information, they created a checklist to assess the ethics and professionalism displayed in the Instagram posts of 50 dietitians and dietetics students. Of interest was that a substantial percentage of dietitians failed to indicate the source of their information, so it was unclear whether it was evidence-based. Additionally, any conflicts of interest were generally not considered by the dietitians, so few were posted. These are important considerations as stated in the Dietitians Australia guide published almost a decade ago.7 Social media can be a powerful means to promote healthy diets to Australians at no cost to the people reading it. The public engages with social media and Instagram is immensely popular. Research with young adults has demonstrated that 96% consider Instagram a suitable medium to disseminate food and nutrition information with videos preferred to text and images for engagement and motivation.8 Some dietitians may view social media as a means of 'marketing' to reach potential clients, but the ethics of the profession must be observed including declaration of conflicts of real and perceived interest. Any commercial interests such as industry-funded conferences or gifts or any sponsors or consultancies should be declared. Those dietitians who work in research and clinical trials know the value of using social media in recruitment and intervention. A group of paediatric obesity researchers led by Lang share their insights from using social media to recruit participants to dietary trials and create a useful checklist to follow.3 They talk about the ethics of social media in research recruitment and factors such as inclusion of vulnerable groups. Different forms of social media have also been used in research to deliver interventions. For example, Facebook has been used to deliver information, motivational messages, tips and recipes and allow interaction among intervention participants.9 There are many other ways that social media can be used in nutrition research, but interestingly few dietitian and nutritionists engage in exploring the big data available. Billions of people express their opinions on a plethora of nutrition issues. While the data can only be generalised to the population who make the posts, the data do offer insights into what people are eating and thinking about food. In a review of studies using social media to study dietary behaviours, 71 studies were identified from 2003 (inception of My Space) to 2021, with most studies published from 2016 onwards.10 It was found that only two of the studies included a researcher qualified in nutrition. Topics varied from analyses of a small number of posts to using methods to study millions of posts. Attitudes to certain foods such as organic foods and gluten-free foods were a feature. Both dietitians and the food industry may be able to gauge trends in acceptance of foods from such discussions. Studies also included assessment of food consumption patterns with the number of posts included ranging from the low hundreds to 50 million. Those studies with exceptionally large datasets used forms of artificial intelligence with automated computing programs and natural language processing software (a subset of artificial intelligence, machine learning and linguistics to interpret human communications). The smaller studies usually involve thematic analysis after posts are scraped from the web. So social media may provide a complementary dataset to traditional dietary surveys when trying to study dietary trends and attitudes. However, currently the ethical framework for dietitians and other health professionals for using the data is undefined and research should be submitted to an ethics committee. Users of social media may agree to the conditions of use without appreciating how their data may be used and that they can be sourced by third parties. Like social media usage, eHealth and mHealth are becoming part of everyday dietetics practice. The COVID-19 pandemic was a digital disruptor and moved many dietitians to deliver their services using digital technologies. While the telephone (smart or conventional) was an affordable easy option, videoconferencing for virtual consultations also gained in popularity. Increasingly in countries such as Australia, United Kingdom and the United States, virtual care is being funded and recommended by health service funders. The benefits for patients largely revolve around accessibility so that those in remote regions, with physical disability, and those who work in full-time employment receive convenient consultations with health professionals. The Dietitians Australia Position Paper on Telehealth published in 2020 reported that this medium was as effective as in-person medical nutrition therapy and that the emerging evidence base in eHealth and mHealth was positive.6 mHealth includes both text messaging interventions, apps and videoconferencing using apps. The Position Paper suggests that in-person, telephone and videoconference consultations may be enhanced by mHealth. One example is a randomised controlled trial using phone counselling and text messages among an overweight young adult population.11 Modest weight loss was achieved and maintained and dietary behaviours improved with the intervention group eating more fruit and vegetables, less take-away food and drinking less sugar-sweetened beverages. A systematic review and meta-analysis of the use of apps as an adjunct to usual care showed greater weight loss (Hedges g = −0.28; 95% CI −0.47 to −0.09) occurred when using apps.12 Further research into the use of mHealth as a stand-alone method rather than as part of a multicomponent intervention for the delivery of medical nutrition therapy is needed. Diverse groups of people may benefit from dietitians developing electronic and mHealth services. Wright and colleagues have undertaken exploratory research examining bariatric patients' barriers to accessing dietetics services and their preferences for digital delivery using mixed methods research.4 This study reports that text messages and emails were preferred modalities for information. In everyday medical nutrition therapy practice, apps and text messages may potentially save time and add economic benefit. An important consideration before recommending digital nutrition services is to determine the digital literacy of the individual as well as the resources available to them. Apps may be used across the nutrition care process such as for collecting information on patients' dietary intakes before the interview, goal setting and monitoring and supplying knowledge and skills information.13 Text message reminders are an effective means to improve attendance at healthcare appointments as confirmed in a systematic review.14 Electronic medical records enable prompt audits of patient records; machine learning can be used to program alerts to recognise patients at risk. Park and colleagues used electronic medical records for their study of nutrition in major trauma patients and outcomes.5 Before the advent of the electronic record, this type of study involved many hours going through paper records for relevant information. Although not explored in this issue, it is the burdensome task of dietary assessment where researchers have experienced gains this past decade. The relationship between diet, health and disease is dependent on valid assessment of food and nutrient intakes and tools for measuring food and beverage intakes are proliferating. We have automated 24-h dietary recalls that can be self-administered en masse for population surveys and a multitude of apps have been developed to replace the traditional food record. The advantage of these tools is that they simultaneously analyse the nutrient composition of the individual's dietary intake bypassing the process of dietitians entering all foods and amounts consumed into the analysis package. Nutrition researchers have designed and validated 24-h recalls and apps for prospective recording. There is still some burden on the individual recorder to ensure all meals, snacks and beverages are recorded, and caution should be exercised to ensure the participant can effectively use these apps. Some commercial apps have no dietitian and nutritionist input, and the source of the food composition data is questionable if crowd sourcing and commercial websites are utilised. However, if the source of nutrient data is confirmed, the portion size estimation is included, and the app has high usability scores, the tools are useful. Dietitians have worked on using smartphone digital images to record food intake for many years.15 While the burden is removed from the individual who needs to record their food and beverages, there are several barriers to achieving estimates of nutrient intake. Translating images into food and beverage portions is burdensome for the dietitian: portion sizes can be hard to estimate, some foods like mixed dishes are hard to discern, additions of salt, sugar and oils added to cooking are invisible and it is critical that the image is captured at the correct angle. Some of these barriers have been overcome with the use of machine learning systems (a subset form of artificial intelligence that uses algorithms trained on data) that are able to identify the foods and segment foods for portion size estimation. Wearables and sensors can be a useful addition to understand food consumption, revealing the when, where and how food is consumed as well as 'what is consumed' to researchers.16 Wearable cameras are not reliant on the participant taking a digital image.17 Cameras can take continuous images, so all consumption is captured and not reliant on the participant remembering to take an image at all eating and drinking occasions. Wearable cameras can be linked to an accelerometer or other systems that detect eating and turn the camera on and off that has advantages for analysis and participant privacy. The time and length of eating and drinking occasions can be monitored and the many environments where food is consumed, social settings of eating, and the concurrent use of screen technologies is captured. Lastly, we should further the role artificial intelligence may play in our profession. Artificial intelligence is differentiated from machine learning, and the subset of machine learning called deep learning, because it is meant to mimic human cognition, which includes reasoning and decision-making. Chat generative pre-trained transformer (ChatGPT) is a hot topic in education, research and business. It is trained on an enormous body of text data and uses deep learning to generate text resembling that written by a real person. The ethics of using copyrighted materials, such as books, and the use of this artificial intelligence in writing copy for business, school essays and publications, such as newspaper and research works, warrant urgent attention. Time will tell whether artificial intelligence will be used for clinical decision-making instead of dietitians.18 The best artificial intelligence chatbot may recognise human emotions but cannot develop its own emotions that are so important when we counsel patients. As with all technologies, dietitians will need to explore the benefits, beware the detriments, and develop ethics around responsible uses in their future practice. The author reports no conflict of interest for this editorial. She has received grant funding from NHMRC, ARC and NSW Health for unrelated work.",W2463825201; W2538984988; W2770903552; W2896822707; W2989371412; W3037563332; W3154470738; W3204044862; W4205726956; W4225286292; W4292824112; W4306835747; W4315754123; W4366988742; W4375956157; W4377010942; W4379375666,9,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35729255,Could artificial intelligence hijack author contributions?,10.1038/d41586-022-01697-w,,,,,,Akira Akabayashi; Eisuke Nakazawa; Hiroyasu Ino,W4283216672,,,5,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34044997,"Big data, artificial intelligence, and the opioid crisis",10.1016/S2589-7500(21)00087-X,,,,,,The Lancet Digital Health,W4248160375,"COVID-19 has exacerbated the opioid crisis in the USA because of lockdowns, treatment centres closing, and pervasive triggers for addiction and relapse, like stress and social isolation. By August, 2020, more than 88 000 drug overdose deaths had occurred in the USA in the previous 12-month period, largely driven by synthetic opioids, such as fentanyl, making 2020 the worst single year in history for fatal overdoses. Additionally, some survivors of COVID-19 have been prescribed opioids for lingering symptoms, which could potentially lead to addiction and further fuel the opioid crisis.",,2,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
32179541,Artificial intelligence isn’t,10.1503/cmaj.74722,,,,,,David Burns,W3010747002,"Seeking to compare the reasoning of human and artificial intelligence (AI) in the context of medical diagnosis is an overly optimistic anthropomorphism. The term AI, as used to describe machine learning algorithms employed in this domain, is itself a misnomer. This is apparent when comparing modern",W2991213201; W4239558931,13,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
