42311616,Opportunities and obstacles in harnessing intraoperative data,10.3389/jaws.2026.16301,,,,,,A Alanon Hernandez; Jayson Marwaha,W7163157071,"Robotic surgery utilization has increased rapidly over the past decade, a trend that is well recognized across surgical specialties, with general surgery representing the largest and fastest-growing contributor. 1 This expansion has been reflected by the transition of several complex general surgical procedures-such as pancreatectomy, colorectal surgery, and esophagectomy-from laparoscopic to predominantly robotic approaches². Similarly, minimally invasive hernia repair has seen substantial growth, with robotic inguinal and ventral hernia repairs increasing more than 40-fold between 2012 and 2018 11,2 .A consequence of the rapid expansion of robotic surgery is the generation and capture of novel, high-dimensional data. The introduction of robotic platforms in the OR offers the potential to generate and capture rich intraoperative data streams, including modalities rarely or never captured previously, such as video, haptics, kinematics, audio, robotic usage metadata, and more. With the advent of this data, the previous ""black box"" of the intraoperative period has the potential to become more measurable and analyzable. High-fidelity and time-stamped data with synchronized audiovisual information collected over time can create large datasets that can ultimately be linked to clinical outcomes and identify best practices.The availability of this data provides new opportunities for improvements in patient care, education, quality improvement, innovations in intraoperative AI, and more. Current research on intraoperative AI applications is in early developmental stages, demonstrating how AI applications can enhance surgical precision through motion analysis as well as provide decision support through surgical phase recognition and anatomical landmark identification 34 , but this intraoperative data will surely prove to be instrumental in advancing the capabilities of these tools. Most existing intraoperative AI systems have the capability to provide simple assistance, with very few (3%) providing conditional decision-making 4,5 . However, as higher-quality, task-specific intraoperative data are collected and analyzed, these data may help catalyze advancement of efforts to enable robots to perform select autonomous surgical tasks 6 .3) Data for Quality Improvement Data generated and collected on robotic platforms may also aid in quality improvement efforts in the form of retrospective analyses to ensure best practices are being met. For example, intraoperative data has already been used to ensure the critical view of safety is being achieved during a cholecystectomy or using ambient operating room sensors to collect audiovisual data to ensure a surgical timeout is always being performed 7,8 . Metadata generated by how physicians interact with surgical technology contains valuable signals for understanding and improving healthcare delivery. For example, studying audit log data from how physicians use electronic health records has provided great insight into understanding how patient care is delivered 910 . Thus, studying metadata from how users are interacting with the robot could give tremendous insight into how to improve intraoperative processes.On the side of clinical research, the data generation of robotic surgery has created a rapid pace of evidence development. This has encouraged increased research in the form of retrospective studies and database reviews 11 . The abundance of automated data generation unique to robots creates large and easily accessible datasets that allow researchers to analyze observational data. However, there is a need for well-designed prospective and randomized studies to create higher-quality evidence of the impact of this data.In the context of education, data leveraged from robotic surgery can play a role in structuring surgical development, assessing skills, and monitoring performance improvement. Automated metrics derived from robotic tools can provide objective data on technical parameters, motion, and time to help trainees improve 12 . Data-driven insights from robotic surgeries can offer opportunities for standardized, objective skills assessment and feedback, supporting surgical education. 1314 .These are among some of the many opportunities robotic surgery has to offer. There remain obstacles that prevent its true potential from being unlocked. The potential of this data and the involvement of multiple stakeholders in generating this data raise the issue of how this data should be governed so that it can be used for all of the potential benefits listed above. While not comprehensive, some important elements of establishing a governance infrastructure for this data are standardization and interoperability. Presently, there is a lack of a standardized and universally adopted ontology for intraoperative data in robotic surgery. Several groups, including the Society of American Gastrointestinal and Endoscopic Surgeons (SAGES, as well as industry groups such as Intuitive Surgical, are working to advance the ontology and annotation of intraoperative events through the development of standardized frameworks for surgical phases, tasks, actions, and gestures 15 . Standardized frameworks that exist outside of surgery can serve as models. A standard used in radiology called Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine (DICOM) aims to ensure interoperability between different manufacturers' devices and set guidelines for managing, storing, and transmitting medical image data 16 . Data standards adapted to intraoperative data could similarly be beneficial.Additionally, data interoperability -the seamless transfer and aggregation of data across vendors, researchers, and healthcare systems -will be an important feature of this data to ensure it can be used productively. Interoperability is complementary to standardization: while standardization ensures data is in a comparable format across various vendors and institutions, interoperability ensures that there are resources to allow the data to be aggregated into larger datasets and used for more powerful analyses. As an example, the development of interoperability in the setting of EHR data may offer lessons for what this might look like for intraoperative data. There is a well-developed regulatory environment around the sharing of electronic health information (EHI), which is data commonly found in EHRs.The 21st Century Cures Act established rules around ""information blocking"" that prevent healthcare providers, health information technology developers, exchanges, and networks from interfering with authorized access to EHI 17 . The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) Final Rule implemented these provisions and specified that there must be standardized application programming interfaces (APIs) that enable data sharing 18 . Finally, interoperability standards, including Health Level Seven (HL7) and Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resource (FHIR) were established to facilitate the integration, exchange, sharing, and retrieval of EHI 19 .The rapid expansion of robotic surgery means that there has been an influx of new digital technology into the operating room that is capturing and generating surgical data that has never been done at this scale. There is great potential in using this data as it can be crucial to surgical skill improvement, patient care, and research efforts to improve surgical outcomes. A rate-limiting step to harnessing this data is to proactively ensure it is standardized and shareable. This is the first step to ensure a collaborative and cohesive environment that promotes innovation and inspires researchers and surgeons to optimize surgical care.",W2106366262; W2957091295; W2994017552; W3000214625; W3020833162; W3090321808; W3108694685; W4298088267; W4309651319; W4377940971; W4395668991; W4400561342; W4406560717; W4407491501; W4409086860; W4412124433; W4413450113,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41676787,Does a Human-drafted Letter Edited by Chat Generative Pre-Trained Transformer Escape Detection by Artificial Intelligence Detectors?,10.31662/jmaj.2025-0256,,,,,,Shigeki Matsubara,W4417181228,,,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41285363,"Artificial Intelligence Will Not Replace Imaging Specialists, For Now",10.1016/j.cjca.2025.11.031,,,,,,Daniel G. Kyrollos; Robert A. deKemp; Benjamin J.W. Chow,W4416549211,"Artificial intelligence in medicine attracts both excitement and fear, yet history reminds us to be cautious: in 2016 Geoffrey Hinton predicted that AI would replace radiologists within five years, a forecast now clearly unmet. Current medical AI excels at narrow, well-defined tasks but remains far from general intelligence capable of working autonomously. Progress is slowed by several challenges: limited and fragmented datasets, opaque model reasoning and unresolved legal and ethical questions.",W4220839569; W4408377893,0,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39764524,Preparing the AI-assisted animal scientist: faculty and student perspectives on enhancing animal science education with artificial intelligence,10.1093/af/vfae038,,,,,,Allison M Baumhover; Stephanie L Hansen,W4406077936,"Mixed messaging around AI’s appropriate use in academia and fears of job disruption has led to hesitation by some users, while positive hands-on experiences shifted views. When adopted prudently with transparency, AI shows promise for streamlining tutoring, testing assessment design, and collaborative idea development. The authors advocate a balanced approach through open dialogue, guiding responsible integration, and emphasizing AI as a partner to human expertise rather than a replacement to empower thoughtful utilization of these technologies. Since the rise of ChatGPT in late 2022, reactions to artificial intellegence (AI) within animal science, particularly in academia, have varied widely. In this article, we draw on our experiences as a professor and a first-year graduate student in animal science, along with existing evidence, to explore the challenges and opportunities AI presents. Offering a framework for ethically integrating these tools into animal science education, particularly through the lens of Bloom’s Taxonomy, which provides a standard for assessing learning across different cognitive levels. The introduction of AI in academia has raised widespread concern. There’s a legitimate fear that over-reliance on AI could stifle creativity and hinder genuine learning. However, the inevitability of AI in future careers cannot be ignored. From a student’s perspective, Ally initially hesitated to embrace AI due to concerns about academic integrity, yet she recognized the necessity of mastering these tools to remain competitive with her peers. A review by Kelly et al. (2023) supports this viewpoint, noting that perceived usefulness, performance expectancy, trust, and societal influences are significant predictors of AI usage. Additionally, a study by Tran et al. (2021) revealed that Gen Z is more confident in their AI abilities compared to millennials and Gen X, leading to greater acceptance among younger demographics. This generational divide underscores a potential disconnect between professors and students regarding AI use in academia, which could lead to mistrust. While many faculty colleagues were initially skeptical, Dr. Hansen approached AI with optimism, influenced by conversations with fellow fiction writers enthusiastic about its potential. She was eager to explore how AI could streamline administrative tasks and free up time for more engaging work. Ethan Mollick, a prominent advocate for AI, suggests that it takes approximately ten hours of using tools like ChatGPT to achieve a true level of comfort (Mollick, 2023). If faculty do not make this initial time commitment, they will miss out on AI’s benefits, leading to a growing divide between adopters and skeptics. This resistance could limit innovation and adaptation in an increasingly digital academic environment. To effectively implement AI in education, as suggested by Houman Harouni from the Harvard Graduate School of Education, educators must stop pretending AI does not exist and instead use it alongside their students (Ross, 2023). This approach helps students see AI as a tool, not a shortcut to bypass critical thinking. For faculty, modeling appropriate AI use is essential. Dr. Hansen, for instance, has been transparent about her AI use with graduate students, which has destigmatized the topic and encouraged them to share their experiences. AI can be a powerful tool when used thoughtfully in animal science education and can be utilized at various levels of Bloom’s Taxonomy (Figure 1). For example, Ally has used AI tools to create flashcards and practice quizzes from her notes. In tutoring settings, AI is particularly adept at generating analogies to explain complex biological processes, such as the metabolic interplay between glycolysis, the TCA cycle, and electron transport. With the right prompts, AI can challenge students to rewrite concepts in their own words, demonstrating a deeper level of understanding. The application of AI tools across the six levels of Bloom’s Taxonomy in animal science education. Each level is divided into two sections, highlighting specific uses of AI for students (S) and teachers (T). AI, however, is not limited to lower levels of learning, it can also be used to achieve higher levels of Bloom’s Taxonomy, from application to creation. For instance, in Dr. Hansen’s graduate-level vitamins and minerals course, students were tasked with writing a short grant proposal on mineral metabolism in their chosen species. Although many students had little experience in grant writing, AI guided them through the process, offering brainstorming assistance, structural guidance, and critical feedback. Students were required to submit their ChatGPT conversation links along with their proposals, with a clear expectation that the ideas and writing were their own. This approach introduced students to grant writing while focusing on the mineral topic, using AI as a supportive tool and not a way to get out of thinking for themselves. Technology, like AI, challenges educators to create assignments that cannot be completed with a simple input into ChatGPT but instead require students to digest, interpret, and apply what they have learned to real-world scenarios, thus reaching higher levels of cognitive understanding. As we conclude this article, it is important to emphasize that AI is not infallible. There is a propensity for AI tools to generate incorrect information, known as hallucinations, which requires the user to have a strong grasp of their concepts to avoid being misled. It is crucial to “don’t trust, and definitely verify” any AI-generated information to ensure quality is not compromised. Additionally, outlining what is ethically appropriate when using AI is paramount if it is to be integrated into the classroom. Ethics and morals may vary, but by setting clear boundaries on what constitutes acceptable AI use, students can begin to develop their own ethical framework. With open dialogue and clear guidelines, our community can thoughtfully explore AI applications while preparing students to use these tools ethically in the future. AI is here to stay, and it would be a disservice to our students’ education if we did not learn how to implement it. Allison M. Baumhover is a first-year master’s student at Iowa State University, working under the guidance of Dr. Stephanie Hansen. Her research focuses on trace mineral interventions to alleviate transportation stress in feedlot cattle. Born and raised in central Iowa, she is a life-long Cyclone and passionate about helping cattle producers improve their management practices. Outside of her academic work, Ally is always exploring new hobbies—whether it’s tennis, climbing, ceramics, or swimming. Stephanie L. Hansen is a Professor in the Department of Animal Science at Iowa State University. She studies feedlot cattle nutrition with a particular emphasis on trace mineral and vitamin metabolism. She also writes crime thrillers under the pen name S. L. Hansen. Having followed discussions about Generative AI in the author community for some time, she has gained valuable insights into its many applications. Corresponding author:[email protected] S.H. brainstormed with ChatGPT on approaches to the article, ultimately using her own idea of the two perspectives shared here. A.B. and S.H. met to generate an outline, then each wrote their own sections using AI tools to brainstorm, provide wording suggestions, and edit. S.H. worked with Claude (poe.com) to merge A.B. and S.H. drafts and to brainstorm figure ideas. S.H. tried to get ChatGPT to make a figure, but it was terrible, so she made it herself. A.B. and S.H. edited and have responsibility for the final version. This manuscript was invited for submission by the American Society of Animal Science. The views expressed in this publication are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the views or policies of the American Society of Animal Science, the journal, or the publisher. The authors declare that they have no conflicts of interest relevant to the content of this article.",W3137663272; W4311487064; W4411248028,3,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39082228,The environmental impact of AI in the lab: a double-edged sword?,10.1080/07366205.2024.2376459,,,,,,Annie Coulson,W4401184878,"Computational tools, particularly AI, are becoming more ubiquitous in scientific research; but what impact do they have on the environment?[Formula: see text].",W2216595528; W2329974904; W3201631622; W3216798413; W4313880353; W4367049415; W4376461016; W4382055832; W4388020336; W4389235688; W4393220192,8,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37640506,Unleashing the potential of AI: a deeper dive into GPT prompts for medical research,10.1136/bmjhci-2023-100857,,,,,,D. Garín,W4386272453,"© Author(s) (or their employer(s)) 2023. Reuse permitted under CC BYNC. No commercial reuse. See rights and permissions. Published by BMJ. I read the article by Haemmerli et al on the performance of ChatGPT3.5 in generating treatment recommendations for central nervous system (CNS) tumours, which were then evaluated by tumour board (TB) experts. While the study did illuminate promising aspects of the Artificial Intelligence (AI) model, the design of the prompt used to interact with ChatGPT warrants further consideration. In the study, the prompt employed was a brief patient history, followed by two questions, which appears to have limited the model’s performance. As a sophisticated large language model (LLM), GPT3.5 relies heavily on the context and specificity of the provided prompt. 2 Based on cited literature, an alternative prompt structure could have included context, specific intent, a question and an expected response format. Moreover, pretraining the LLM with examples of the expected answer significantly improves the quality of the answer. 3 Finally, the introduction of GPT4 in early March 2023 has shown considerable improvement in understanding and generating responses when compared with ChatGPT3.5. 5",W4317435136; W4377010595; W4383059305; W6850668563,3,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
