38271186,Response to: Limitations of Artificial Intelligence in Plastic Surgery,10.1093/asj/sjad386,,,,,,Jose F. Palacios; Nicholas Bastidas,W4391212303,"We thank Mr Dhawan and Dr Brooks for their response and are pleased to see that our article Man, or Machine? Artificial Intelligence Language Systems in Plastic Surgery has generated such interest.1,2 We believe the topics brought up by the authors merit further commentary. As the authors point out, plastic surgery is heavily invested in optimization of aesthetic outcomes. Several versions of “ideal” or “youthful” aesthetics have been described for structures from the nose to, recently, the male buttocks. However, we would proceed with incorporating artificial intelligence (AI) into aesthetic analysis cautiously. While aesthetic outcomes are important, they should be patient-centric, emphasizing subjective patient satisfaction and not reinforcing often ethnocentric ideals. We should remember to not only ask ourselves, “Can we use AI for something?” but also, “Should we use it?” And if so, “How exactly do we use it?”3 The role of AI is obvious in areas of medicine that require objective pattern recognition for tasks with high interoperator reliability; its role is much less clear in aesthetic outcome assessment.4 However, novel patient-centric applications of AI are likely just over the horizon. Furthermore, recommending explicit incorporation of AI into curricula at this point may be premature. AI is likely to see progressively increased utilization in daily practice. As tools are developed and ethical considerations become more prominent, these topics will inevitably be incorporated into both medical school and residency curricula. However, AI in plastic surgery and in medicine as a whole is still in its infancy. Many publications on the topic explore either modestly enhancing the capability of existing technology or speculating on future applications.5 Curricula incorporating AI topics is likely to struggle to keep up with advancements. AI ethics in medicine is also in its infancy. This is reflected by the variability of journal policies on AI language models in manuscript authorship, with some outlining nuanced guidelines and others opting for blanket policies.6 Several topics are still being actively debated, with novel topics yet to be discussed. The crux of incorporating AI into plastic surgery comes down to identifying specific areas within the field that are appropriate for technologic support. AI may not be beneficial in all circumstances, and forceful incorporation may instead be detrimental to both surgeons and patients. Discussions of AI applications within plastic surgery must be specific and actionable if they are to be helpful. Additionally, keeping up with innovations in medical AI technology will likely require dedicated effort for those who wish to stay at the cutting edge. The authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and publication of this article. The authors received no financial support for the research, authorship, and publication of this article.",W3000117122; W3101254140; W4318911937; W4381597744; W4386083791; W4389076114,0,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
