35919010,Comments on: Artificial intelligence in ophthalmology - Machines think!,10.4103/ijo.IJO_912_22,,,,,,Merina Kurian,W4289786919,"Dear Editor, It was interesting to read the article Artificial intelligence in ophthalmology—Machines think![1] The article outlines the exciting opportunities artificial intelligence presents for ophthalmology including the screening, diagnosis, and management of a range of ocular pathologies, from diabetic retinopathy to glaucoma.[1] The author also incorporates the governance and ethical issues of artificial intelligence as outlined by the World Health Organization, such as “ensuring inclusiveness and equity”.[12] However, equity and inclusiveness are persistent challenges in ophthalmology. Access to and the delivery of ophthalmic services are not universal, despite the widespread impact of visual impairment.[3] The socioeconomic and gender-based disparity has also been associated with the global burden of disease conditions such as age-related macular degeneration and diabetic retinopathy.[45] Additionally, several low-to-middle income countries need to further support the training of ophthalmic professionals to meet population demands for services.[3] Artificial intelligence can be used alongside telemedicine eye services.[1] Even so, we must be careful to ensure that the use of artificial intelligence does not further exacerbate the disparities, which exist in the provision of ophthalmic care, particularly in resource-limited settings. Financial support and sponsorship Nil. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest.",W3033427424; W3130938027; W3170263180; W4220666699; W4223418127; W4225724957,0,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
