37152126,"Editorial: Machine learning and artificial intelligence for smart agriculture, volume II",10.3389/fpls.2023.1166209,,,,,,Shanwen Zhang; Chuanlei Zhang; Dong Sun Park; Sook Yoon,W4366598257,"Currently, AI is being widely used in various agricultural scenarios, including intelligent perception, real-time field 12 monitoring, intelligent early warning, disease and pest detection, and intelligent decision-making for crop 13 production environments. With the help of AI, farmers can now detect whether there are any diseases and pests, 14 whether they need to use pesticides, and whether their plant protection practices are effective. This special edition 15 focuses on several issues that still require further study and discussion, such as agricultural unmanned aerial 16 vehicles, crop type mapping, crop phenotypic analysis, and the identification of crop diseases and pests in 17 sustainable and intelligent phytoprotection. 18 2",W2912874092; W2955639914; W2966657623; W3120101602; W3143922534; W3158568413; W3159378418; W4200571805,14,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35802382,Rethinking Algorithm Performance Metrics for Artificial Intelligence in Diagnostic Medicine,10.1001/jama.2022.10561,,,,,,Matthew A. Reyna; Elaine O. Nsoesie; Gari D. Clifford,W4284964177,"The promise of artificial intelligence (AI) to improve and reduce inequities in access, quality, and appropriateness of high-quality diagnosis remains largely unfulfilled. Vast clinical data sets, extensive computational capacity, and highly developed and accessible machinelearning tools have resulted in numerous publications that describe high-performing algorithmic approaches for a variety of diagnostic tasks. However, such approaches remain largely unadopted in clinical practice. This discrepancy between promise and practice— the AI chasm—has many causes. Some reasons are endemic to the larger field of AI, including a lack of generalizability and reproducibility for the published algorithms. Other reasons are more specific to clinical AI, such as a lack of gender, racial, and ethnic diversity in clinical data sets and insufficient evaluation of the algorithms in clinical settings. The disconnect between the metrics for algorithm performance and the realities of a clinician’s workflow and decision-making process is a fundamental but often overlooked issue. The inclusion of clinical context in AI performance metrics for optimizing and evaluating clinical algorithms could make AI tools more clinically relevant and readily adopted (Box).",W2893366129; W2995282027; W3098699929; W3162447981; W4200133306; W4285044750,49,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
33940691,Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Neurosurgery: More than Machinery.,10.1016/j.wneu.2021.02.057,,,,,,Ishaan Ashwini Tewarie; Alexander F.C. Hulsbergen; William B. Gormley; Wilco C. Peul; Marike L.D. Broekman,W3158599782,,W2600022899; W2761529114; W2787816121; W2792248972; W2803590515; W2804285896; W2887274340; W2900530244; W2958059103; W2962412324; W2977436523; W2981676251; W2981869278; W2999203623; W3032207303; W3070313384; W6735060455,7,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
29757747,The Human Touch: Practical and Ethical Implications of Putting AI and Robotics to Work for Patients,10.1109/MPUL.2018.2814238,,,,,,Jim P. Banks,W2802635027,"We live in a time when science fiction can quickly become science fact. Within a generation, the Internet has matured from a technological marvel to a utility, and mobile telephones have redefined how we communicate. Health care, as an industry, is quick to embrace technology, so it is no surprise that the application of programmable robotic systems that can carry out actions automatically and artificial intelligence (AI), e.g., machines that learn, solve problems, and respond to their environment, is being keenly explored.",,23,2018,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
27898975,Adapting to Artificial Intelligence,10.1001/jama.2016.17438,,,,,,Saurabh Jha; Eric J. Topol,W2558050786,,W1929024416; W2172567064; W2174661749; W2514628397,630,2016,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39925011,Is more data always better? On alternative policies to mitigate bias in Artificial Intelligence health systems,10.1111/bioe.13398,,,,,,Guillermo Lazcoz Moratinos; Íñigo de Miguel Beriaín,W4407349175,"The development and implementation of Artificial Intelligence (AI) health systems represent a great power that comes with great responsibility. Their capacity to improve and transform healthcare involves inevitable risks. A major risk in this regard is the propagation of bias throughout the life cycle of the AI system, leading to harmful or discriminatory outcomes. This paper argues that the European medical device regulations may prove inadequate to address this-not only technical but also social challenge. With the advent of new regulatory remedies, it seems that the European policymakers also want to reinforce the current medical device legal framework. In this paper, we analyse different policies to mitigate bias in AI health systems included in the Artificial Intelligence Act and in the proposed European Health Data Space. As we shall see, the different remedies based on processing sensitive data for such purpose devised by the European policymakers may have very different effects both on privacy and on protection against discrimination. We find the focus on mitigation during the pre-commercialisation stages rather weak, and believe that bias control once the system has been implemented in the real world would have merited greater ambition.",,4,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39250158,Laying a Foundation for the Use of Artificial Intelligence in Diagnosis.,10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.31907,,,,,,Andrew D. Auerbach,W4402357048,"The study by Zimolzlak et al 1 in this issue of JAMA Network Open is one of what will soon be a flood of research that focuses on the role of artificial intelligence (AI) in identifying diagnostic process problems and diagnostic errors (also called diagnostic opportunities), with the overall goal of catalyzing diagnostic improvement. 2 At its core, research of diagnostic processes and outcomes is a field of measurement: measurement of timeliness, accuracy, equity, and effectiveness. In the case of diagnosis, this can sometimes be very straightforward, as in the case of unexpected findings at time of autopsy. 3 In others, diagnostic errors are at the core of a clinical practice, but the line between diagnostic faults and closely related issues, such as overtreatment, is less clear. 4 Methods can be driven entirely by administrative data 5 with challenges in terms of face validity, or manual methods that are accurate but very time consuming and difficult to spread. 6 Finally, some combination of diagnosis data and events can be used to identify cases where diagnostic problems may have taken place, an approach called electronic triggers (e-triggers) 7 . As a result, we have few scalable, time efficient, and accurate ways to identify where an error took place and in turn, to support efforts to reduce these same errors.",W2068084886; W2895457576; W4200109803; W4384524772; W4390673899; W4393150791; W4402357060,7,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39017482,Theoretical AI Harms Are a Distraction,10.1038/scientificamerican0224-69,,,,,,Alex Hanna; Emily M. Bender,W4396816062,,,6,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38863917,Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Scientific Publications,10.15605/jafes.039.01.01,,,,,,Elizabeth Paz-Pacheco,W4399007668,"Twenty-six years earlier in their famous chess rematch, an IBM Supercomputer called Deep Blue defeated then-world chess champion Garry Kasparov: it was the first-ever chess match won by a machine, a much celebrated milestone in the field of Artificial Intelligence. Just last year, the World Association of Medical Editors released the “WAME Recommendations on Chatbots and Generative Artificial Intelligence in Relation to Scholarly Publications,” a recognition of not just the expanding applications of AI in scholarly publishing but more so of the accompanying emergence of concerns on authenticity and accuracy. In recognition of this relevant topic, our Vice Editor in Chief, Dr. Cecile Jimeno, provided a well-attended and interesting talk during the last ASEAN Federation of Endocrine Society Convention in Thailand on the “Emerging Issues on the Use of Artificial Intelligence for Scientific Publications.”",W4389937130,6,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38606893,"Artificial intelligence transforms research, but the integrity norms do not change",10.1111/dmcn.15912,,,,,,Bert Seghers; Oldřich Tůma,W4394763378,"Artificial intelligence transforms research, but the integrity norms do not changeArtificial intelligence creates opportunities for better science, from idea creation and automated insight harvesting to fluently written articles and more accessible science communication.It may revolutionize the way we do research.In a decade from now, our daily work might be unrecognizably changed in ways we cannot imagine today.However, the principles for research integrity do not change with the advent of artificial intelligence.When ChatGPT was introduced in late 2022, the scientific community became concerned about growing risks of non-authenticity of published work.Journals and learned societies responded with codes of conduct to regulate the use of artificial intelligence in research.The more detailed they were, the faster they were outdated and replaced.Indeed, it makes little sense to overregulate artificial intelligence, and certainly not in research.When the European Federation of Academies of Sciences and Humanities (ALLEA) updated the European Code of Conduct for Research Integrity in 2023 (https:// allea.org/ wpconte nt/ uploa ds/ 2023/ 06/ Europ ean-Code-of-Condu ct-Revis ed-Editi on-2023.pdf), a significant change was requiring disclosure of artificial intelligence use.This so-called ALLEA Code added as unacceptable practice, 'Hiding the use of artificial intelligence or automated tools in the creation of content or drafting of publications.'Researchers must disclose precisely how artificial intelligence was used, which tool was employed, and for which parts of the research or writing.That makes it a robust, future-proof norm: research integrity permits artificial intelligence use, but it is misleading to present your work as if you were not assisted by artificial intelligence.Authorship norms and criteria were well-established (https:// www.icmje.org/ recom menda tions/ browse/ rolesand-respo nsibi lities/ defin ing-the-role-of-autho rs-and-contr ibuto rs.html) before artificial intelligence.The possibilities of generative artificial intelligence have not changed them, but rather emphasized that the essence of authorship is responsibility and accountability.Because only humans can be held accountable for the content of a paper, ChatGPT and other non-humans cannot be an author (https:// wame.org/ page3.php",,3,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
