42161475,Artificial Intelligence and Technology in Cardiac Disease,10.1016/j.cjca.2026.02.008,,,,,,Christopher L F Sun; Pascal Thériault-Lauzier,W7162012515,"Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly reshaping clinical and operational cardiology by improving diagnostic precision, risk assessment, and early deterioration detection1 through cardiac imaging, electrocardiography, and electronic health record (EHR) analysis. Building on this progress, the field is shifting from specific single-task tools to generalized models and end-to-end systems that can reuse knowledge across tasks, reason over text and images, and coordinate clinical workflows. This work highlights 3 AI paradigms, foundation models, large language models and vision language models, and agentic AI systems that are central to this transition toward generalized cardiology AI by outlining bedside relevance, early evidence, risks, and safeguards.",W4396494945; W4401943935; W4409020930; W4415259594,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41429220,Artificial Intelligence Will Replace Imaging Specialists.,10.1016/j.cjca.2025.12.029,,,,,,Akash Fichadiya; Bethlehem Mengesha; D. Paterson; Robert J.H. Miller,W4417520295,"There has been tremendous advancement of artificial intelligence (AI) in all aspects of medicine, including the field of cardiac imaging. Cardiac imaging is well suited for artificial intelligence (AI) solutions because it follows a defined workflow with clear technical challenges at each step. AI could potentially optimize diagnostic quality during image acquisition and reconstruction, facilitate automated segmentation, or assist with reporting to improve disease diagnosis and risk stratification. We will describe specific applications in multimodality cardiac imaging and how AI will either replace roles traditionally held by imaging specialists or transform practice of imaging specialists.",W3177759260; W4303474922,0,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
