38699579,Use of artificial intelligence to track platelet-rich plasma treatment outcomes in females with nonscarring alopecia: A case series,10.1016/j.jdcr.2024.02.037,,,,,,Ora Raymond; Javed A. Shaik; Katelyn J. Rypka; Ronda S. Farah; Gretchen Bellefeuille; Maria Kramarczuk Hordinsky,W4393238389,"Platelet-rich plasma (PRP), a minimally invasive procedure, has shown promising results in the treatment of androgenetic alopecia (AGA).1,2 During this procedure, patients’ platelets are injected into the scalp.1,2 Over the course of 3 PRP treatments (typically given once per month for 3 consecutive months) improved hair growth has been reported.1,3,4 However, objectively assessing PRP efficacy is challenging. Current methods to assess alopecia treatment efficacy include global photographic comparison, manual trichoscopy, physician assessment, and patient self-assessment.",W2119646539; W2783646062; W2999124437; W3034147795; W3041019649; W3082994739; W3120831533,6,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
