41592547,Building the Future of Radiology Through Artificial Intelligence: Uzbekistan National Efforts and Innovations,10.3348/kjr.2025.1550,,,,,,Marat Khodjibekov; Yulduz Khodjibekova; Lalita Yunusova,W7124244217,"Artificial intelligence (AI) is profoundly reshaping the field of radiology across Asia and Oceania, contributing to enhanced diagnostic precision, optimization of workflow processes, and improved accessibility to imaging services in metropolitan and in underserved regions.Globally, the integration of AI into radiological practice continues to accelerate, propelled by advances in deep learning algorithms, the expansion of large-scale imaging datasets, and the establishment of standardized frameworks for clinical validation and reimbursement.In 2023, the Ministry of Innovative Development of the Uzbekistan implemented 511 innovative projects worth over 176 billion soums (USD 14 million).The primary areas that received funding were: medicine, the agricultural sector, architecture, metallurgy, and energy.A key priority project, approved by the Cabinet of Ministers in July 2025, is focussed on the development of image analysis in radiology (AI software for computed tomography [CT] and X-ray).At the Republican Specialized Scientific and Practical",W4307638147,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41474366,Abstract writing in the modern era of artificial intelligence,10.1093/cvr/cvaf253,,,,,,Kenneth Chan; Pok‐Tin Tang,W7117872956,"Abstracts are ubiquitous, and a key component of modern scientific research. In the 18th century, papers presented at meetings of the Royal Society would be summarized, initially for record-keeping (published from 1831 as the Proceedings of the Royal Society).1 Later, in the 19th century, scientific societies began publishing collections of summaries of newly published research.1 Early scientific abstracts were cheaper to print than full manuscripts, enabling wider publication, translation, and access to the contents of scientific meetings. Often written by professional staff such as society secretaries or abstractors, they sometimes replaced full manuscripts due to limited library services. In 1892, the Royal Society began requiring author-composed abstracts to reduce the burden on their secretarial staff—a practice not widely adopted until the mid-20th century.1 Nowadays, abstracts help researchers to rapidly communicate the rationale, findings, and potential impact of their work. An unclear abstract could potentially sink even the strongest work. This article explores how the modern scientific abstract has evolved into different formats, elements of a high-quality abstract, and the impact of the dawn of artificial intelligence (AI).",W2954917766; W4225891786; W4400587327; W4411948293; W4413844004,0,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
