33896487,Author Reply: Artificial Intelligence in Sports Medicine,10.1016/j.arthro.2021.03.013,,,,,,Nikolaos K. Paschos,W3157540725,"I would like to thank the Editor for the opportunity to respond to the letter by Ramkumar et al.1Ramkumar P.N. Karnuta J. Nwachukwu B.U. Williams R.J. Regarding “Editorial Commentary: Artificial intelligence in sports medicine diagnosis needs to improve”.Arthroscopy. 2021; 37: 1365-1367Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (4) Google Scholar that concerns my editorial commentary2Paschos N.K. Editorial Commentary: Artificial intelligence in sports medicine diagnosis needs to improve.Arthroscopy. 2021; 37: 782-783Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (4) Google Scholar about the systematic review entitled “Diagnostic Performance of Artificial Intelligence for Detection of Anterior Cruciate Ligament and Meniscus Tears: A Systematic Review”3Kunze K.N. Rossi D.M. White G.M. et al.Diagnostic performance of artificial intelligence for detection of anterior cruciate ligament and meniscus tears: A systematic review.Arthroscopy. 2021; 37: 771-781Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (5) Google Scholar so as to further promote discussion of artificial intelligence (AI) in sports medicine. One of the points raised was that my editorial commentary “amplified the message of the systematic review by distilling the value of AI to whether or not AI was ready to take over a part of the diagnostic process” and cautioned that we need additional data before making this decision. Another point made by Ramkumar et al.1Ramkumar P.N. Karnuta J. Nwachukwu B.U. Williams R.J. Regarding “Editorial Commentary: Artificial intelligence in sports medicine diagnosis needs to improve”.Arthroscopy. 2021; 37: 1365-1367Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (4) Google Scholar was that “the studies in the systematic review did not actually evaluate AI itself and instead they evaluated the quality and quantity of MRI [magnetic resonance] images inputted” and that certain limitations exist in the attempted comparisons between experts and computers in the different studies. I believe that the latter should be addressed by the authors of the respective studies. The purpose of the systematic review was “to determine the diagnostic efficacy of AI methods for detecting ACL [anterior cruciate ligament] and meniscus tears and to compare the efficacy to human clinical experts.” This is a specific clinical question, which is the diagnosis of ACL and meniscal tears—currently done by experts. When we attempt to compare the diagnostic efficiency between AI and experts, the notion of incorporating AI to be part of the diagnostic process is obvious. AI is not a competitor but an assistant, and as I stated in my editorial commentary, “we need to be ready to embrace AI use, but we also ought to be critical of its implementation and seek transparency in every step of the process. We cannot afford to see AI as an antagonistic element in our practices but rather as a valuable assistant that could significantly improve diagnostic accuracy, once it meets qualitative validation.” As stated by Ramkumar et al.,1Ramkumar P.N. Karnuta J. Nwachukwu B.U. Williams R.J. Regarding “Editorial Commentary: Artificial intelligence in sports medicine diagnosis needs to improve”.Arthroscopy. 2021; 37: 1365-1367Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (4) Google Scholar “few will argue the importance of scientific rigor and remaining critical of new process adoption,” so I believe that we all agree on improvement of our scientific methods4Rossi M.J. Brand J.C. Lubowitz J.H. Tools to improve scientific research.Arthroscopy. 2018; 34: 3113-3114Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (9) Google Scholar and on the need for robust and high-quality data.5Paschos N.K. Editorial Commentary: Meta-analysis shows that platelet-rich plasma is safe and effective for knee osteoarthritis: Future randomized controlled trials must be of high quality—Not high quantity.Arthroscopy. 2021; 37: 326-327Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (1) Google Scholar In 1921, the term “robot” was introduced in a theatrical play originating from the word “robota,” which in Czech means “forced laborer.”6https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/robota#Czech. Accessed March 20, 2021.Google Scholar The fascination of humans as well as the associated—justified or not—fear and anxiety toward machines taking over has existed for a long time. One hundred years later, the utilization of automatic technologies has simply improved our quality of life and I hope that we are more mature to embrace new technologies without misconceptions. More important, we ought to be ready to openly discuss our rapidly evolving present. AI and its role is one of these topics, and we need to explore this without positive or negative biases. After all, diagnostic accuracy in detecting ACL and meniscal tears is physicians’ responsibility toward patients. It is our duty to work harder to ensure that the incorporation of AI in our clinical practice is flawless and to ensure transparency and constructive criticism in evaluating the performance of AI by continually adding high-quality research. Download .pdf (.08 MB) Help with pdf files ICMJE author disclosure forms",W2902352837; W3087523897; W3113620651; W3128861789; W3159139833,5,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
