38206157,Objective Facial Assessment With Artificial Intelligence: Introducing the Facial Aesthetic Index and Facial Youthfulness Index.,10.36849/JDD.7080,,,,,,Sonja Sattler; Konstantin Frank; Martina Kerscher; Sebastian Cotofana; Tatjana Pavicic; Berthold Rzany; Peter Peng; Rainer Pooth,W4390793231,,,10,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37557096,Allergic to ChatGPT? Introducing a Desensitization Protocol to Embrace Artificial Intelligence,10.1159/000531785,,,,,,Pamir Cerci,W4385716413,"Dear Editor, The “ChatGPT allergy” concept, while seemingly absurd, reflects the unease some individuals experience in response to rapid technological advancements, akin to allergic reactions. Tachycardia and chest tightness can manifest when confronted with novel technology, signaling technological apprehension. As Arthur C. Clarke noted, advanced technology can seem like “magic,” inciting anxiety for some. In this context, we encounter artificial intelligence (AI) and generative pretrained transformers (GPTs). AI aims to emulate human cognitive processes, and GPTs, an advanced form of AI, excel at generating human-like text. These technologies, born of human creativity, enhance productivity and efficiency. It is vital to acknowledge the ever-evolving AI landscape. Current technologies like ChatGPT 4 will soon yield successors like GPT-5, ushering in the AGI era. This progression could pose challenges for those resistant to current technology, emphasizing the need for adaptability. The swift evolution of AI and its potential implications have prompted industry leaders like OpenAI’s CEO, Sam Altman, to advocate for proactive regulation, given the severe consequences ofmissteps in AI technology. This call for caution is further supported by over 1,100 signatories, including Elon Musk and Steve Wozniak, who urged AI laboratories to pause for 6 months to address the societal risks posed by large-scale AI systems. AI and GPT technologies, particularly ChatGPT, released by OpenAI in 2022, have shown potential in allergy and immunology. They can detect primary immunodeficiency disorders, improve diagnostic accuracy, facilitate clinical practice, and optimize practice management. ChatGPT also contributes to healthcare education and research by improving academic writing and data analysis. ChatGPT, a promising AI tool, can potentially transform academic writing, data analysis, and workflow in allergy and immunology [1–4]. However, its limitations are evident, such as in a study where it inaccurately proposed nutritional advice for individuals with specific food allergies [5]. This underscores the necessity of human oversight in AI utilization. As we progress toward advanced AI models like GPT-5, wemustmitigate risks by enhancing safety, accuracy, and ethical use. Despite their potential, these technologies cannot replace human expertise, which remains indispensable in allergy and immunology [6, 7]. We propose a 12-step desensitization program (Table 1) inspired by drug desensitization practices [8] to alleviate ChatGPT-related apprehension. This",W3023258423; W3089369348; W3110254058; W4220952043; W4327946446; W4376223903; W4379278559; W4379877813; W4380290447,0,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37528589,Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Otology: What Will the Future Be?,10.5152/iao.2023.170723,,,,,,O. Nuri Özgïrgïn,W4385458466,Artificial,W2155075970; W2913945298; W3033882614; W4284961177,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37460378,Artificial intelligence in sleep and art.,10.1016/j.sleh.2023.07.005,,,,,,Meir H. Kryger,W4384438343,"Artificial intelligence (AI) seems everywhere. What is it? “AI refers to the capability of computer systems to perform tasks conventionally considered to require human intelligence, such as speech recognition, decision-making, and visual recognition of patterns and objects.” In the realm of visual art, the program DALL.E 2 (https://openai.com/dall-e-2/) uses as input natural language commands to create new art, or modify existing art. The images generated do not have “an artist.” If there is not an artist, is it art? AI has penetrated many aspects of life from the operation of motor vehicles, medical decision making and even surgery, to manufacturing, and myriad other applications. In PubMed in 2022 alone there were more than 15,000 articles with the keywords “Artificial Intelligence.” Fig. 3 shows the dramatic increase since 2018 in the articles with keywords “Artificial intelligence” and “sleep.” One might have the impression that this is something new in medicine and especially sleep medicine. This is simply not correct. AI has been used in many different ways, and the terms for its use have varied over the last four decades. Early systems were “heuristic” (rulebased) and later “neural networks,” and finally “AI.” Sleep has always been a data-intensive field and computers have been used to acquire and attempt to analyze data and replicate human interaction with the data. This trend started in the 1970s, first to define waveforms, then stage sleep, then to analyze breathing patterns. As the sleep field and computers and cloud computing evolved, much more complex issues could be addressed. For example, large datasets can be evaluated to phenotype data from hundreds to thousands of patients. With the development of miniaturized sensors and the adoption of wearable devices, data from millions of people can be analyzed. Where AI will take medicine is still a mystery. Where AI will take art is even a bigger mystery. Sleep Health: Journal of the National Sleep Foundation 9 (2023) 387–388",W2072288110; W2396647704; W2409191094; W3202381754; W4212846881; W4220995359; W4283073788; W4285404558; W4292411285; W6712228252; W6842312010,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35777464,"Editorial: Artificial Intelligence, machine learning and the changing landscape of molecular biology",10.1016/j.jmb.2022.167712,,,,,,James Zou; Hongzhe Li; Sylvia Plevritis,W4283700281,,W4223558335; W4223640474; W4225016428; W4229073727; W4281658915; W4282958926; W4282965996; W4283657261,1,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35244030,Artificial Intelligence for Nursing Practice and Management,10.1097/CIN.0000000000000871,,,,,,Suzan Ahmad; Melinda Jenkins,W4214890997,"KEY POINTS With the advancement of available clinical and health data as well as the growth of big data in different healthcare disciplines, artificial intelligence is becoming a prevalent emerging technology to predict disease risks, disease diagnoses, courses of illness, and treatments. A simplified meaning of artificial intelligence, machine learning, and deep learning methods is illustrated for future nursing informatics research. Current use ofmachine learning and deep learning in nursing education is described and research and recommendations for future use are presented. ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE Artificial intelligence (AI) has been defined as an area of study in computer science concerned with the ability to make computers do things that would require intelligence if done by humans. Artificial intelligence is a new-old science. Artificial intelligence concepts were presented in the 1950s; however, due to the scarcity of electronic data, at that time, its use for further discovery was still immature. Nowadays, with the growth of electronic data, AI is growing in importance in many disciplines. In healthcare and nursing science, with widespread implementation of electronic health records and electronic documentation, big data in terms of large volume, variety, velocity, and veracity of data aremore available. Large warehouses of structured and unstructured nursing data will enhance the use of AI technology to create more meanings that can be used in research to improve nursing practice and management. Artificial intelligence is the ability to use machine learning to imitate humans' cognitive abilities by using numerous statistical and algorithmic calculations in understanding, problem solving, and decision making. Artificial intelligence is a broad concept under which are machine learning and deep learning (Figure 1). Machine learning (Table 1) has three subfields; each uses different assumptions and mathematical frameworks for how data are ingested and how learning occurs within the algorithm. The subfields of machine learning are (1) supervised learning; (2) unsupervised learning; and (3) reinforcement learning (semisupervised learning). Supervised learning requires labeled data sets, which is the process of attaching meaning to different types of digital data such as text, audio files, images, videos and more. This environment includes a known data domain and known response (labeled) set. Therefore, a supervised learning model and an algorithm deal with an environment where the given",W1039522147; W1958478380; W1967542410; W1982398500; W2032321113; W2064939794; W2070654153; W2147505388; W2300440214; W2335120868; W2411330383; W2417664095; W2540149843; W2584734105; W2591252554; W2597227151; W2738113705; W2769726739; W2781788077; W2791525516; W2859535992; W2890616856; W2981296841; W3006219531; W3033755023; W3103211661,13,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
33217757,Commentary on: Facelift Surgery Turns Back the Clock: Artificial Intelligence and Patient Satisfaction Quantitate Value of Procedure Type and Specific Techniques,10.1093/asj/sjaa262,,,,,,Janek S. Januszkiewicz,W3101254140,"It is doubtful any other branch of surgery surpasses facial aesthetic surgery for its capacity to promote new techniques and procedures with only modest scientific evidence in support. Fortunately we all stand on the “shoulders of giants” and the apprenticed-based surgical training model has served admirably as a mechanism for evolving facelift surgery over the past century to the point where a great variety of diverse surgical pathways can, nonetheless, yield pleasing and consistent results. I welcome the opportunity to discuss the article “Facelift Surgery Turns Back the Clock: Artificial Intelligence and Patient Satisfaction Quantitate Value of Procedure Type and Specific Techniques.” 1 The authors have attempted to use modern computational science to address the challenge of objectively evaluating outcomes of facelift surgery, and provide some basis for comparative assessment of different techniques. In our modern era it does not require expert review or a validated patient satisfaction survey to realize that contemporary facial aesthetic surgery, when done well, allows patients to look younger, healthier, and (usually) more attractive. Quantifying this change is another matter. As the authors point out, we all struggle to accurately compare the various techniques and procedures available, particularly when there are so many variables at play, and when we the observers are prey to conscious and subconscious bias. The facial aging process is indeed complex but the human psyche is another quantum altogether. What, in truth, are we trying to measure? Can artificial intelligence (AI) do it better? It could be argued that dispassionate unbiased analysis is impossible to achieve with human observers. All observers, whether trained or untrained, carry bias.2 Surgeons blinded to surgical technique may feel they can be objective about the relative improvements achieved when carrying out comparative studies, but 2-dimensional (2D) photography presents a limited data set of information to the observer. Videography can supplement this but there is no substitute for close physical observation of the animated face to assess a facelift outcome. When observing face-to-face we cannot be entirely removed from the person or the personality we are assessing. We are all emotional creatures influenced by microexpressions, body language, and our own foundational guidelines of what we consider aesthetically valuable or valueless. When it comes to estimating facial age human observers are generally accurate to within a mean error of 6 years.3 Studies of human facial age estimation have also shown that there is a bias towards middle age. We assimilate information from preceding faces viewed, which then modulates our perception of the face currently in front of us. Younger faces are perceived to be older than they are, and older faces to be younger. These adaptation biases account for most of the variance in age estimation.4 To confound this further, the apparent age of a person can be very different from her/his biological age, and be influenced by race and gender. Quantitative analysis of 3D surface scanning has confirmed that the rate of facial aging change is relatively constant (and similar) in both sexes until around age 50, but soon after menopause there is a sharp change in trajectory for female aging.5 Men will continue to age at a steady pace into their mid-sixties, whereas for women the rate of aging increases significantly (up to 3 times faster) just after menopause, peaking in the mid-to-late fifties before settling back. Aging is not a level playing field. In this study the authors focused on the degree to which different methods of facelift and fat grafting might make a patient look younger than before. Of course “younger” may not necessarily mean “better” if there are bizarre distortions of animation and volume, or unwelcome tension and an imbalance of features that otherwise corrupt the outcome. Misdirected vectors of superficial muscular aponeurotic system (SMAS) repositioning alter the tensional forces on zygomaticus major and other mimetic muscles, which can contribute to distortions of facial expression.6 These disturbances are less likely to be apparent in neutral expression 2D images and are unlikely to be measured in an AI-based analysis. How successful is AI in measuring facial age and therefore quantifying perceived reductions in facial age after surgery? To answer this it is important to understand the methodology and limitations of machine learning AI in facial recognition and age estimation. AI has come a long way since its origins in the 1960s when the US Department of Defense began training computers to mimic human reasoning. Early street mapping projects by the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency were the precursors to Google Maps. Machine learning describes the computer algorithms that were developed to improve automatically through experience by constructing mathematical models built on sample data or “training.” These methods have given way to neural networks and deep learning in which a collection of connected units (artificial neurons) interact in a manner vaguely inspired by the architecture of the biological brain. The algorithms are stacked in a hierarchy of increasing complexity and abstraction. Deep learning is thus a subfield of machine learning that uses neural networks; however, this process of AI is too complex for humans to understand, hence the inner workings of these models are often referred to as being a “black box” that we cannot look into. There is unquestionably risk associated with handing over more and more decision-making to a machine when we as overseers cannot prevent or detect shortcomings in the algorithms inside that “black box.” The fear with AI is not so much malevolence, but rather misdirected competence. Many researchers, scientists, and leaders, including Hawking, Musk, Gates, Chomsky, and Tegmark, have expressed concern in the media and via open letters of the dangers when the goals of a superintelligent AI system fail to align with the goals of human society (https://futureoflife.org/background/benefits-risks-of-artificial-intelligence/). Of course, the benefits to society will be transformative if we can avoid the pitfalls. These deep-learning algorithms have improved machine learning approaches and allowed biometric face recognition software to become more useful. In some settings they have improved the accuracy of facial age scores from 60% to above 90%. Current convolutional neural networks are becoming larger and more complex and in controlled settings can approach age estimation accuracy of 97%,7 but there are still significant limitations and reservations. The neural networks rely on learning from large data sets of facial images such as MORPH or IMBD-WIKI, which contain thousands of annotated images. (A word of caution: the developers of IMDB-WIKI use images and age labels of actors and celebrities scraped from the internet. These include stills from movies unverified for the accuracy of age information and with no allowance for facial makeup, previous “digital” alterations, or previous “surgical” alterations.) The convolutional neural network model is “trained” on these data sets to improve its accuracy by looking for combinations and patterns and then learning from those data. Based on this process, the formula begins to change (independent of human input) and learn what to look for and how to make that calculation better and quicker. The authors of this study analyzed their patient images with 4 different proprietary convolutional neural network models, averaging the results to generate a mean estimated age. They reported near-perfect correlation in the preoperative imagery of measured age to biological age accuracy score of 100.4 (100 being an exact match). Unfortunately the mean accuracy scores for the individual neural networks ranged between 91.5 and 106.3 and the combined error margins were ±13 (which equates to ±8 years for a 59-year-old). A year after the skin-only facelift surgery, AI estimated patients to be 2.95 years younger but without statistical significance. After SMAS procedures, AI estimated patients to be 5.35 to 5.85 years younger, which was a significant improvement over the skin-only procedures. It is not clear why the authors chose between skin-only and SMAS-based techniques when planning treatment, and they acknowledge this study is impeded by selection bias. Fat grafting amplified the age reduction benefit by 2.1 years. The scale of improvements reported does correlate with results of other human observer studies but the AI-estimated age reductions after facelift fall well inside the age-estimation margins of error for current neural network models. The authors have not reported what role makeup can play in confusing the ability of AI to judge facial age, but given that textural changes are an important part of the surface image analysis, it is likely some patients in this study photographed in full makeup will have distorted the AI mean scores to some degree. The authors also used FACE-Q patient-reported outcomes to demonstrate that their patient population was satisfied with their facial appearance, quality of life, and the decision to undergo surgery. The FACE-Q is an attempt to quantify outcome measures that are otherwise unknowable to the external observer. The ultimate measure of any success in facial aesthetic surgery will always be patient satisfaction; however, human patients will be no less subjective in their interpretation and assessment of outcomes than their human surgeons. Patients who “like” their surgeon, and patients who have realistic expectations, will report more favorably than those who do not. Unfortunately, there were no preoperative baseline FACE-Q measures with which to compare, we do not know what interventions or procedures any patients in the study group had previously undergone that might otherwise reduce their apparent age by appearance, and there was no correlation between FACE-Q scores and the facelift procedure subtypes, despite the different levels of age reduction reported between skin-only (2.95 years) and SMAS-based techniques (5.35-5.85 years). With only 1 year of follow-up it is not possible to draw any meaningful conclusions from this study, including the merits of different SMAS techniques. If the authors can follow these patients for a further 5 years we may learn whether SMAS plication and SMASectomy stand up to time equally well. In the absence of verifiable science or knowledge, humans have typically filled the vacuum with mystical thinking. We believe what we see with our own eyes and thus the world can appear flat. However, when we measure accurately and objectively the true picture reveals itself and we start to understand where our eyes deceived us. Reports extoling the benefits of a new, minimally invasive technique, injectable, or stem-cell therapy can seem like a case of “the emperor’s new clothes,” with little discernible difference between the before and after images. A truly independent quantitative measure of facial age reduction and enhanced attractiveness would be a welcome tool for most plastic surgeons. Standardization of outcome analysis would help improve the homogeneity and accuracy of reporting, aid in comparison between individuals or institutions, and reduce reporting bias. Ultimately some variation of machine learning modeling could be used not only to assess facial age, but also to guide clinical decision-making and personalized treatment strategies8 according to the type of facial aging that has occurred. Can contemporary AI technology and the use of convolutional neural network algorithms reliably estimate apparent facial age after an aesthetic facial procedure? This study suggests the answer is a very qualified “yes.” It is less clear whether AI analysis of a 2D static image will ever successfully apply a quantitative value to enhanced youthful facial beauty. Nevertheless the authors are to be sincerely applauded for taking the first steps in using modern image analysis by AI to shine a more objective light on the field of facial aesthetic surgery. Moore’s law of a doubling of transistors on a circuit every two years has held for 55 years now and quantum computing and AI may augment this exponential trend in computational power. On that basis we can anticipate huge leaps in facial image analysis and increasingly sophisticated new tools that encompass facial movement and expression—ultimately, perhaps, providing us with a system to quantify youthful beauty. The author declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and publication of this article. The author received no financial support for the research, authorship, and publication of this article.",W2126263520; W2790071538; W2896707536; W2937882605; W2951808774; W2997089963; W3000626837; W3102977445; W6785692178,2,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
32055869,Innovative use of artificial intelligence in urogynecology,10.1007/s00192-020-04243-2,,,,,,Jouhayna Bentaleb; Maryse Larouche,W3006265162,"Development of artificial intelligence in urogynecology will benefit greatly from future developments in telemedicine and complex management algorithms, to help patients and providers to navigate through available options and predict response to treatment for women with various pelvic floor disorders.",W2021774979; W2526220767; W2822216045; W2900708016; W2937495001; W2970747439,15,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
31373643,Artificial Intelligence and Training Physicians to Perform Technical Procedures,10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2019.8375,,,,,,George Shorten,W2965826336,"Winkler-Schwartz et al have set out to determine if some combination of machine learning algorithms can differentiate participants according to their stage of practice (ie, neurosurgeon, fellow, senior or junior resident, or medical student) based on their performance of a complex simulated neurosurgical task. A total of 250 simulated surgical resections performed by 50 participants were studied using a prospective, observational case series design. The best-performing algorithm (K-nearest neighbor) had 90% accuracy for prediction and used 6 machine-selected metrics. Three of the 4 algorithms used in the study misclassified a medical student as a neurosurgeon.",W1892775430; W2075057776; W2792298780; W2966447025; W4238206825,7,2019,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
30721962,Artificial Intelligence for Breast Cancer Imaging: The New Frontier?,10.1093/jnci/djy223,,,,,,Christoph I. Lee; Joann G. Elmore,W2913088693,"There is great excitement around artificial intelligence (AI), the use of computers to mimic human cognitive functions, and its promise of automating time-consuming and repetitive tasks in medicine. One prime target for AI is the analyses of visual input data. For example, high-powered computers using AI algorithms already rival ophthalmologists in identifying diabetic retinopathy on fundus screening images (1). Now, screening mammography is taking center stage as an obvious target where 40 million women in the United States alone undergo the exam annually, and radiologists have traditionally been less than perfect in their interpretive performance (2, 3). Implied in these efforts is the provocative notion that certain tasks that are currently performed by highly specialized physicians can be completely replaced by super computers that run AI systems. In this issue of the Journal of the National Cancer Institute, Rodriguez-Ruiz and colleagues (4) efficiently used existing imaging datasets from nine prior studies to compare the interpretations of their commercial AI system with interpretations of radiologists. In all, the study team compared the performance of their AI system to radiologists’ performance on 2652 digital mammography screening exams. Their AI system generated a probability of malignancy score between 1 and 10, whereas radiologists provided an assessment based on the Breast Imaging and Reporting Data System (BI-RADS) assessment scale that ranges from 0 to 6. The area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) for the AI system was 0.840 compared with an AUC of 0.814 for the radiologists. Based on these findings, the authors conclude that the AI system was as accurate in screening mammography interpretation as the radiologist. This study is a promising example of the emerging era of AI-based interpretation of medical image data. Rodriguez-Ruiz and colleagues (4) reported that their AI algorithm was previously trained and tested using 9000 true positive and 180 000 true negative mammograms not used in the current study for validation. In this analysis, the authors use a diverse set of screening digital mammograms obtained from seven countries and multiple imaging equipment vendors, with ground truth for the presence of cancer based on histopathologic data and clinical follow-up. While these results are compelling, multiple additional steps need to be taken prior to widespread adoption of commercial AI systems for automated mammography screening in routine practice. First, Rodriguez-Ruiz and colleagues (4) used existing imaging sets from prior studies that compared mammography to another imaging technology (eg, digital breast tomosynthesis). To demonstrate differences in sensitivity and specificity between two screening technologies, these cohorts were enriched with a greater proportion of cancer-positive mammograms than seen in routine screening practice. The enhanced frequency of abnormal findings among these images could trigger greater radiologist scrutiny (context bias), resulting in higher recall rates of the radiologists with resultant lower observed accuracy. Indeed, the AUC of 0.814 among radiologists is considerably lower than the AUC of more than 0.90 observed in actual U.S. community practice (5). Thus, to truly demonstrate efficacy of an AI algorithm, larger validation datasets are needed that are more representative of a screening population. Second, incremental improvement in the AUC is not directly translatable to improved patient outcomes in the clinical setting. In the case of this commercial AI system, the output is an estimated probability of malignancy on a scale of 1–10. However, it is unclear what threshold probability of malignancy value would trigger further diagnostic workup if the AI system was used in clinical practice. Currently, subjective interpretation by radiologists using the standardized BI-RADS is categorized as 0–6 in a noncontinuous fashion (with BI-RADS 3, 4, and 5 requiring further diagnostic workup). It is not clear how to compare a 10-point linear scale from an AI system with our current noncontinuous BI-RADS scale. For instance, a radiologist’s suspicion of more than 2% but less than 95% malignancy is given a BI-RADS 4 assessment, whereas a radiologist’s suspicion of more than 95% malignancy is given a BI-RADS 5 assessment. It is uncertain what proportion of exams that a commercial AI system would flag as having more than 2% malignancy, requiring additional diagnostic workup under our current clinical practice thresholds. If the vast majority of screening exams are given a probability of a malignancy score of more than 1 on a 10-point scale (suggesting >2% change of malignancy), then the technology would lead to an excessively large proportion of women called back for additional imaging (compared to just 10% of women currently), rendering the AI system useless under accepted practice thresholds. Third, it is uncertain if patients and physicians would accept medical imaging devoid of any human involvement and interpretation (6). In the absence of a radiologist reviewing these mammography images, who would take ultimate responsibility for breast cancers missed by an imperfectly performing AI algorithm? Radiologists will still likely be tasked with reviewing abnormalities noted by the commercial AI algorithm on the screening exams, ordering and interpreting the subsequent diagnostic imaging, and then performing any final image-guided tissue biopsies to confirm malignancy. Exactly where and how AI algorithms will support the practice of interpreting radiologists within their current workflow remains unknown. These nuances will be important because missed breast cancers on screening mammography remain the most litigious situation for medical malpractice lawsuits (7). For now, AI holds incredible promise for rapidly and reproducibly interpreting vast amounts of medical image data. However, like other emerging technologies, AI systems for automated breast cancer detection require robust evaluation for clinical effectiveness before broad adoption. We learned from the experience of computer-aided detection in mammography that adopting promising new technologies too quickly could be a costly mistake; computer-aided detection was accepted and reimbursed as an adjunct to digital mammography in the early 2000s based on hype but little evidence and later found to lead to more false positives without improved cancer detection (8). As we enter this exciting and rapidly evolving new frontier, it will be important that AI systems for breast cancer screening be validated on multiple, diverse imaging datasets representative of screening populations. Moreover, the optimal interface between radiologists and AI systems must be determined, with ascertainment of broader stakeholder tolerance for inevitable missed cancers and false positive workups. Affiliations of authors: Department of Radiology, University of Washington School of Medicine, Seattle, WA (CIL); Department of Health Services, University of Washington School of Public Health, Seattle, WA (CIL); Hutchinson Institute for Cancer Outcomes Research, Seattle, WA (CIL); Department of Medicine, David Geffen School of Medicine at UCLA, Los Angeles, CA (JGE). The authors report no conflicts of interest related to this article. Dr Lee reports grant funding from GE Healthcare unrelated to this work.",W1976376218; W2010120422; W2028177593; W2078839662; W2557738935; W2611638831; W2755007334; W2918598741; W4239212557,14,2018,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
30567676,Artificial intelligence and capsule endoscopy: Is the truly “smart” capsule nearly here?,10.1016/j.gie.2018.08.017,,,,,,Michael F. Byrne; Fergal K. Donnellan,W2905420451,"One wonders whether Gavriel Iddan truly realized what an impact his invention of wireless capsule endoscopy (CE) would have in the world of GI endoscopy.1 Since its official release in 2001, it has revolutionized the management of small-bowel diseases, including GI bleeding, Crohn’s disease, abnormal radiologic imaging, polyposis syndromes, and celiac disease. Furthermore, the advent of balloon enteroscopy to “chase” the findings of small-bowel CE has brought a very powerful solution to occult GI bleeding.",W1650175677; W2009349908; W2320847508; W2529627479; W2765527079; W2766166720; W2780770155; W2797069348; W2809596283; W2858686322; W6745672129; W6747002025,27,2018,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
29325181,„Das Thema künstliche Intelligenz ist in der Radiologie angekommen“,10.1055/s-0043-125045,,,,,,,W4255377442,"Zum zweiten Mal nach 2017 findet im Februar 2018 der Kongress Emerging Technologies in Medicine – ETIM statt. Initiator des Zukunftskongresses ist Prof. Dr. Michael Forsting, Direktor der Universitätsradiologie Essen. Im Interview skizziert er die Themen der kommenden Tagung und welche Bedeutung sie für die Radiologie haben.",,0,2018,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
29052631,Learning to play Go from scratch,10.1038/550336a,,,,,,Satinder Singh; Andy Okun; Andrew Jackson,W2766846498,,W2056740053; W2131600418; W2153039919; W2257979135; W2766447205; W2787259794; W2911296969; W4321428174,63,2017,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
