30119128,The Fourth Industrial Revolution Tackles Plastic Surgeons,10.1097/PRS.0000000000004914,,,,,,Arturo S. Prado,W2886739978,"Sir: The first industrial revolution started in 1784 with mechanical production; the second, in 1870 with mass production; the third, in 1969 with computer science and automation; and today, we are in the middle of the fourth industrial revolution with cyber-physical intelligent systems, which include different key drivers, such as cloud computing, the Internet, simulation, augmented and virtual reality, holographic projections, intelligent systems integration, autonomous and supervised robots, cybersecurity, additive manufacturing (three-dimensional printing), and big data. Artificial intelligence is a general term that includes various platforms and technologies aimed at enabling, through electronic computer systems, the transformation of tasks that normally require human intelligence, such as visual perception, hearing, decision-making, speech, object recognition and translation between languages, among others. Through algorithms, artificial intelligence can give machines the ability to reason and to achieve problem-solving, object recognition, and finally decision-making.1 At the origin of artificial intelligence, there are multiple fields, including philosophy, psychology, linguistics, statistics, and robotics. However, advances in computer science, such as improvements in speed and power, have allowed performing analysis of big data.2 Metaphorically, a self-driven Google car, generates 4000 GB of information per day, and that also goes to big data; thus, this term cannot be defined only in terms of quantity, because what numerically for a group of researchers is big data, for another group, can be simply a set of traditional data. The world is living in the midst of an exponential data explosion, and plastic surgeons will have to look to associate with analytic and data scientists because they will always be better prepared to deal with the five-Vs paradigm of big data: volume, veracity, variety (complexity), velocity (real-time streaming), and value. A 1-minute high-definition plastic surgery video contains far more data than found in a high-resolution computed tomographic image, and that is why plastic surgeons are in a unique position to help drive these innovations instead of waiting passively for technology to be useful. We should input early in the design of artificial intelligence algorithms to improve accountability and interpretability of big data analyses, because artificial intelligence causal inference cannot yet determine causal relationships in data at a level necessary for clinical implementation, nor can it provide an automated clinical interpretation of its analyses. Although big data can have rich variables, it is poor in providing the appropriate clinical context with which to interpret the data. Therefore, future plastic surgeons should critically evaluate the predictions generated by artificial intelligence and interpret the data in clinically meaningful ways. Artificial intelligence has the potential to revolutionize the way in which future plastic surgery will be taught and will keep giving the patient the highest quality care. DISCLOSURE The author has no financial interest to declare in relation to the content of this article. Arturo S. Prado, M.D.School of MedicinePostgraduate SchoolPlastic SurgeryUniversity of ChileSantiago, Chile",,6,2018,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
