41932501,Artificial intelligence uncovers latent signals of portal hypertension in routine CT imaging,10.1016/j.jhep.2026.03.032,,,,,,Tal Zeevi; Catherine Mezzacappa,W7147458404,,W1877563293; W2922822790; W3025022288; W3180754516; W4220694100; W4281645619; W4319872581; W4407135816; W4410206247; W4414400950,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41701078,Effectiveness of artificial intelligence chatbots in providing sexual health information: an exploratory study,10.1093/jsxmed/qdag028,,,,,,Himel Mondal; Saumya Ranjan Mishra; Jaswant Jangra; Adesh Kumar Agrawal; Santanu Nath; Sujit Sarkhel,W7129564509,"Generative artificial intelligence (AI) chatbots powered by large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated their potential in healthcare by offering relevant, user-specific information.1 Their versatility has been validated across domains like education and clinical decision support. In the domain of sexual health, accurate and stigma-free information is crucial for physical and psychological well-being. Misinformation or lack of access to credible information often contributes to anxiety, unhealthy behaviors, and delayed healthcare seeking. In many regions of world, cultural taboos further hinder open conversations about sexual health, making it difficult for individuals, especially youth to seek clarification from professionals.2 This emphasizes the need for anonymous, accessible sources of information. AI chatbots can fill this gap by offering personalized, stigma-free responses in a conversational style.3 This study aimed to assess the effectiveness of several such chatbots in addressing sexual health queries, evaluating their performance across key attributes such as accuracy, relevance, fluency, and coherence. The results of this analysis can inform their role in sexual health education, particularly in conservative settings.",W4379260371; W4401503123; W4408504382; W4411184968,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41494671,Access for Artificial Intelligence in Radiology: A Hong Kong Perspective,10.3348/kjr.2025.1655,,,,,,Billy Ming-Hei Lai,W7117982717,"Without doubt, when utilized appropriately, artificial intelligence (AI) can help radiologists and benefit patients.AI can assist the radiologist in many ways, in both diagnostic and interventional radiology, helping to prioritize reporting, facilitate clinical management, concentrate expertise, focus limited manpower resources to needy areas and, at times, can also aid to increase confidence for the reporting radiologist.In Hong Kong, AI has become an increasingly integral part of the radiology workflow, especially in the public system, which serves more than 90% of the patients.There are a number of factors which influence accessibility and utilization of AI, such as, compatibility with existing practices and platforms, adaptability to user needs, presence of appropriate infrastructure, and cost.Like many places worldwide, there are separate existing platforms utilized by clinicians and radiologists for dayto-day workflow.In Hong Kong, the Clinical Management System (CMS) is used mainly by clinicians for everyday operations, such as clinical note taking and prescription. The Radiology Information System (RIS) and Picture",,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
