36107621,How Artificial Intelligence Aspires to Change the Diagnostic and Treatment Paradigm in Eyes With Age-Related Macular Degeneration,10.3928/23258160-20220817-01,,,,,,Ashley Sohn; Howard F. Fine; Dimosthenis Mantopoulos,W4295900634,,W2192471031; W2589074029; W2621101817; W2734118236; W2766696009; W2767404384; W2768217608; W2772059204; W2775252515; W2787314811; W2796809202; W2797401432; W2886281300; W2893458693; W2896202701; W2904843285; W2950848962; W2956665584; W2969181508; W3013530016; W3018398338; W3022960093; W3023998086; W3033178972; W3046351077; W3082449889; W3083782439; W3089071011; W3089097858; W3110049172; W3113554311; W3115064642; W3126399635; W3155153710; W3214691923; W4211020962; W4247214019,0,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35567548,Artificial intelligence in cardiovascular medicine in China,10.1093/eurheartj/ehac002,,,,,,Yaling Han,W4206968718,"Artificial intelligence (AI) and digital health technologies have become of increasing interest in cardiovascular medicine due to their unique ability to improve the quality of patient care and leverage growing data to facilitate personalized and precision medicine.1,2 The potential of AI in cardiovascular medicine is tremendous; however, the practical application of these technologies has not been fully integrated into the current clinical workflow. Chinese cardiovascular physicians have generally attempted and made preliminary achievements in AI-assisted diagnosis, treatment, and clinical research of cardiovascular diseases to drive the deep integration of AI and digital health technologies in the cardiovascular field for patient benefit.3 This was recently reported in the Chinese Journal of Cardiology (CJC) with a contribution entitled ‘Key data elements for clinical management and outcomes of patients with coronary artery disease: definitions from Coronary Artery Disease and Creative Antithrombotic Clinical Research Collaboration (CardiaCare)’ by Prof. Yaling Han and colleagues from the General Hospital of Northern Theater Command.4 The CardiaCare writing committee members reviewed data elements and definitions from published guidelines, clinical trials, databases, and standardized documents, then determined the data elements and standardized definitions, which should be included in CardiaCare, aiming to facilitate the exchange of disparate data sources, enhance abilities to support multicenter research, and subsequently ensure the use of databases according to standardized process and criteria. The CardiaCare standardized set comprised 864 data elements from admission to the post-hospital follow-up visit. There were eight tables in the documents (Table 1), including demographic and admission information (23 elements), medical history and risk factors (102 elements), clinical presentations and diagnosis (22 elements), diagnostic and laboratory tests (111 elements), interventional diagnosis and treatment (118 elements), pharmacological therapy (213 elements), clinical outcomes (161 elements), and special subpopulations (114 elements: 87 elements for transcatheter valve replacement and 27 elements with cardiac rehabilitation). In brief, the CardiaCare standardized data elements set will provide support for real-world clinical research in consecutive data collection and database mining. Definitions of key data elements from CardiaCare Definitions of key data elements from CardiaCare AI is not only faster but also able to pick up findings that escape the human eye. In the process of combining AI technology with clinical practice, special attention should be paid to data security, including the security and privacy of patient information, clinical data transmission, data processing, and other aspects.5 In addition, it is necessary to regularly evaluate data integrity and accuracy and make sure physicians compare the results of AI calculation with clinical practice to ensure and continuously optimize the accuracy of iterative algorithms and improve the accuracy of AI-assisted clinical decision-making.6 Another recent publication was: ‘Application of remote “Internet+” interactive mode in the management of patients with hypertension during normalized epidemic prevention and control of COVID-19’ by Prof. Feng Cao and colleagues from the Chinese People’s Liberation Army General Hospital.7 In this randomized controlled study, which took place from October 2019 to December 2020, a total of 394 patients with hypertension were randomly divided into an experimental and a control group. The experimental group adopted remote ‘Internet+’ interaction mode to carry out remote blood pressure intervention and the control group received traditional blood pressure control. A total of 394 patients with hypertension were included in this study. After 6 months of intervention, the systolic and diastolic blood pressure of the two groups were both lower than the baseline blood pressure before intervention (both P < 0.05), the systolic blood pressure [(125.7 ± 11.7) mmHg vs. (132.6 ± 12.9) mmHg, P < 0.001] and diastolic blood pressure [(72.4 ± 10.7) mmHg vs. (79.8 ± 11.6) mmHg, P < 0.001] in the experimental group were lower than those in the control group, as shown in Table 2. The blood pressure reduction speed of the experimental group was faster than that of the control group [(18.63 ± 1.59) mmHg/day vs. (13.26 ± 2.85) mmHg/day, P < 0.001], and the time to reach the target blood pressure in the experimental group was shorter than that in the control group [(23.69 ± 2.93) day vs. (47.12 ± 5.81) day, P < 0.001]. Compared with the control group, the blood pressure measurement times [(0.98 ± 0.13) times/day vs. (0.20 ± 0.40) times/day, P < 0.05] and the number of communications with physicians [(0.97 ± 0.16) times/week vs. (0.12 ± 0.32) times/week, P < 0.05] were significantly higher in the experimental group. Correlation analysis showed that the speed of blood pressure reduction was positively correlated with the number of blood pressure measurements and the number of communications with physicians. The proportion of standardized medication [93.91% (185/197) vs. 51.78% (102/197), P < 0.001], timely measurement [97.46% (192/197) vs. 47.21% (93/197), P < 0.001], and high-degree disease awareness [94.42% (186/197) vs. 49.24% (97/197), P < 0.001] were significantly higher in the experimental group than those in the control group. So, the remote ‘Internet+’ interactive management strategy can effectively improve patients’ blood pressure control. Comparison of blood pressure between two groups of patients with hypertension before and after intervention Comparison of blood pressure between two groups of patients with hypertension before and after intervention We believe that AI and digital health technologies will not replace physicians, but it is important that physicians should know how to use them sufficiently to generate their hypotheses, perform big data analytics, and optimize applications in clinical practice to bring on the era of precision cardiovascular medicine. Fast-growing AI and digital health technologies will inevitably bring revolutionary development in the field of cardiovascular disease, especially in the progress of cardiovascular clinical research, which will be worth promoting and adopting. Conflict of interest: none declared.",W2920679031; W3084231313; W3125603166; W3216211209; W6803473070; W6803613247; W6804323812; W6804588435,2,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34775711,[Improve the quality of clinical cardiovascular research in China with the help of artificial intelligence].,10.3760/cma.j.cn112148-20210826-00737,,,,,,Yali Han,W3213697005,随着人工智能技术的迅速发展，心血管领域迎来了革命性突破的契机，但目前该技术的实际应用尚未完全融入临床工作流程中。本期内容分享了我国心血管医生在人工智能辅助心血管疾病诊治和临床研究方面的良好尝试和初步成效，以期推动人工智能与心血管领域的深度融合，造福广大心血管疾病患者。.,,0,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
