37935386,Artificial intelligence‐based self‐feedback on medical counselling performance,10.1111/medu.15279,,,,,,Jee Young Hwang,W4388479022,"Artificial intelligence (AI) has become common in medical education and patient care.It is essential, therefore, to address the emergence of these new technologies, both to optimise training and ready learners for a care provision environment in which patients have easier and arguably more accurate access to medical information.To do so, students need opportunities to experience the benefits of AI when interacting with patients who require medical consultation. | WHAT WAS TRIED?A telemedicine-based medical counselling simulation was conducted in which a standardised patient (SP) presented specific medical issues followed by AI-based self-feedback.Forty-four fourth-year medical students participated.The SP scenario involved a 62-year-old female patient who was diagnosed",,4,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
