34727154,Response to: Additional Thoughts on Artificial Intelligence Evaluation of Facelift Surgery,10.1093/asj/sjab381,,,,,,James P. Bradley; Steven Lu; Alexander R. Gibstein; Kevin Chen,W3211111609,"We appreciate the authors’ comments and critiques1 of our paper on the use of artificial intelligence evaluation for facelift surgery.2 We too recognize that the accuracy of the neural network algorithms will continuously improve over time. We predict there will be more applications for this technology in the future for physicians, and possibly for patients. We also appreciate the authors’ suggestion that neural networks be trained on facelift patients; however, we believe the goal of any facelift patient is to appear unoperated on. Therefore, we feel that being evaluated by a neural network trained on millions of (mostly) unoperated images is more appropriate. We agree with the authors that 3-dimensional (3D) imaging would allow for different, and potentially more accurate, assessment of apparent age and other aesthetic metrics. The key would be developing a large, tagged data set of 3D images on which to train the neural networks. In other words, 3D images would have to be connected with demographic data for each image. Neural networks are indeed inherently limited by the nature, contents, and breadth of the training data. The authors astutely highlight the consequences. Yet, the power of neural networks comes from the training set being larger than anything a regular person can experience or incorporate. For objective assessment of facelift results, appearance of age reduction is important. However, we readily concede that an objective assessment of beauty may be more challenging. Beauty, as a key for patient satisfaction, is a quality that changes over time, and varies with cultures and individuals. For this quality, it is difficult to imagine a computer algorithm fully replacing the human observer. The authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and publication of this article. The authors received no financial support for the research, authorship, and publication of this article.",W3102977445; W3205917537; W6803068811,0,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34628177,Artificial intelligence and predictive analytics in nursing education.,10.1016/j.nepr.2021.103224,,,,,,Siobhan O'Connor,W3204923662,,W1574901103; W1990830572; W2064939794; W2146798017; W2770717476; W2789391194; W2888126660; W2937037822; W2971301364; W2995805754; W3108291954; W3113048821; W3125643946; W3132346797; W3152594952; W3172048750; W4405056171; W6797161134; W6874875886,25,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
