35712908,"Will digitization, big data and artificial intelligence– and deep learning–based algorithm govern the practice of medicine?",10.1111/jdv.18223,,,,,,C. L. Goh,W4283008379,"Artificial intelligence (AI) broadly refers to any human-like behaviour displayed by a machine or system. AI systems are powered by algorithms, using techniques such as machine learning and deep learning. Machine learning refers to the process by which computers develop pattern recognition, or the ability to continuously learn from and make predictions based on data. Deep learning is a subset of machine learning that is superior. Deep learning models process a large amount of data—big data. AI is now part of our daily lives. We have AI solutions for quality control, video analytics, in manufacturing and healthcare solutions.1, 2 In this issue of JEADV, F Flament et al.3 reported in their findings using AI analysis of over half a million European and Chinese women on their differences in the phenotypic appearance of photoageing between the 2 groups. Their study cohort was a staggering 465 687 Caucasians from Europe/Russia and 79 026 Chinese. The authors used smartphone applications to capture facial features and analyse the data collected from the study cohort. It would have been difficult to conduct such a large-scale study without image digitization of medical records and collation of big data and using computer algorithm applications to differentiate subtle differences in phenotypic characteristics between the study groups. Their findings of the AI analysis were not surprising to Asian clinicians, as it has been observed that Asians tend to manifest photoageing with pigmentary disorders, whereas the Caucasian tends to manifest with more wrinkles and vascular anomalies, but little pigmentary changes. The current AI analysis confirmed the previous clinical observation.4 Does this mean that we now have another avenue to more objectively measure changes in the clinical presentation in dermatology sciences? Will we utilize more AI tools in future epidemiology study to help dermatologists to plan and put resources in the right place and through improved diagnostic accuracy? None declared. Data sharing not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analysed during the current study",W2408008918; W3023997891; W4220668300; W4224299761,7,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
