37698049,Augmented liver pathology: artificial intelligence and the assessment of hepatocellular neoplasms,10.1111/his.15020,,,,,,Alastair D. Burt,W4386624437,"The emergence of machine learning (ML)- and deep learning (DL)-generated algorithms and their application to digitised whole slide images (WSI) in contemporary diagnostics has been described as the ‘third revolution in pathology’.1 Such approaches use advanced computer technologies that draw upon huge data sets and have the capacity to accurately, automatically and reproducibly identify tissue features and patterns as well as trained histopathologists (if not better). Moreover, with DL it may be possible to identify features that are invaluable for a precise diagnosis and for the prediction of response to therapy and prognosis, but which are beyond the eyes of the pathologist.2 In future, artificial intelligence (AI) will also undoubtedly play a role in unravelling the complexities of multiple data sets from, for example, electronic health records, genomics, ‘multi-omics’ and radiomics to provide integrated, meaningful outputs that ultimately enhance precision care. While the world of radiomics is arguably ahead of pathology in the application of AI, during the past 5 years there has been an exponential growth in the number of publications describing AI technologies applied to WSI in histopathology. However, we are yet to see a corresponding rise in the translation of this into routine clinical practice. Some of this may relate to regulatory constraints.3 To date, only two applications have been approved in the United States by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for clinical use (identification of prostate carcinoma in needle biopsies and cervical cancer screening), while in Europe approximately 20 applications have now received CE mark approval. Nonetheless, it is likely that over the next decade the number of approved clinical applications will dramatically increase; these should significantly improve patient outcomes, but will also undoubtedly impact upon KPIs in laboratories, the pathology workforce, process automation and workflow management.3 In the field of liver pathology, much of the focus to date of AI has been upon addressing perceived shortcomings in the histological assessment of biopsies for clinical trials in non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (or the currently preferred term, ‘metabolic associated steatotic liver disease’). Some have argued that the failure to date to generate an effective, licenced therapeutic intervention for this common disease is due to inadequacies in the systems used by histopathologists for semiquantitative scoring but also, importantly, the well-documented suboptimal inter- and intraobserver consistencies in their assessment. While the real reason for trial failures may lie more with inadequate power in many studies, such concerns regarding trial biopsy interpretation have generated considerable interest (and activity) in the development of AI solutions; these are now gaining considerable traction.4 In addition, however, there have been some recent publications that have focused upon the possible use of ML and DL algorithms in the clinical assessment of hepatic tumours and tumour-like lesions.2 Hepatocellular nodular lesions are often found in the context of established cirrhosis. This comprises a spectrum from (i) benign nodules (such as focal nodular hyperplasia, adenomas and regenerative nodules) to (ii) low and high dysplastic nodules and (iii) malignant tumours (early and progressed hepatocellular carcinoma, HCC).5 The accurate distinction between some of these can be challenging for the histopathologist even in resection specimens and explant tissue, but is particularly difficult in needle biopsy specimens despite immunohistochemical algorithms that can help with the interpretation. Cheng et al.5 developed a DL system to differentiate between different hepatocellular nodular lesions. Using surgical excision and biopsy specimens from six centres, they applied four deep neural networks. The predictive efficiency of their ‘optimal model’ was verified by a comparison with that of a group of nine pathologists. When they used their model on a test cohort, they found that it provided an agreement rate higher than that of the study pathologists at both a patch and whole-slide level. They suggested that this could improve diagnostic efficiency and quality particularly in centres where subspecialist opinion may not be available. Furthermore, they demonstrated utility in ‘tricky’ cases lacking a definitive diagnosis following three independent reviews: augmented pathology helped establish a consensus. Although the scale of their study is impressive (213 280 patches from 1115 WSIs of 728 patients), in real terms the numbers are still small and, as the authors acknowledged, it requires multicentre validation. There is preliminary evidence that an ML-based approach may also be useful in the subtyping of hepatocellular adenomas. The study by Liu et al.6 of haematoxylin and eosin-stained sections from resected adenomas showed their algorithm to have at least a moderate level of performance in distinguishing between HNF1α-inactivated adenomas (90% accuracy), inflammatory (80%) and unclassified (85%) adenomas. They suggested that this approach may be of use in clinical settings that lack access to immunohistochemical and molecular assays. Perhaps one of the most exciting areas of development for AI on WSI in the assessment of human malignancies more generally, however, is with the prediction of the response to therapy, disease-free survival (DFS) and overall survival (OS). Abundant proof of principle has been documented for a variety of malignant extrahepatic tumours, but progress has been slower with HCC.2 This may be explained by the (hitherto) dismal outcome with this tumour, but recent interest has increased in the light of (i) earlier detection of HCC, (ii) better surgical resection techniques, (iii) increased use of transplantation for HCC and the (iv) development of next-generation drugs including immunotherapy and combination protocols.7 Zeng et al.,8 for example, developed an AI model that was able to identify the activation of several immune gene signatures in WSI of HCC that may hold promise in predicting response to immunomodulatory agents. For the prediction of post-treatment survival, one approach has been to apply AI to improve the detection of an already known key prognostic factor in HCC, that of microvascular invasion (MVI). This pathological process is commonly scattered among peritumoral tissue and relies upon extensive sampling for accurate detection. Chen et al.9 demonstrated that a DL-based model could be used to predict the probable MVI status of an HCC with an area under the curve of 0.904; this may become an additional tool for the pathologist in the evaluation of malignant liver tumours. A different approach to predicting outcome was that taken by Saillard et al.,10 who used two DL algorithms to build models to predict survival following surgical resection for HCC using WSI. One algorithm (‘SCHMOWDER’) used an attention mechanism on tumour areas that had been annotated by a pathologist, while the other (‘CHOWDER’) required no human expertise. The survival prediction of their models was 0.78 and 0.75, both outperforming a composite predictive score which combined all currently documented clinical and pathological prognostic factors. A further interesting attempt at predicting outcome in HCC using AI is published in this issue of Histopathology.11 Patil et al. turn to an old favourite of hepatopathologists, the reticulin stain. Early observations indicated that the normal hepatic reticulin framework, while preserved in benign hepatocytic lesions, is frequently altered in HCC with the spacing of reticulin fibres and a reduction or loss of reticulin, particularly in poorly differentiated tumours. Reticulin staining has therefore long been a mainstay of routine liver tumour assessment. The authors hypothesised that the total amount of tumour-associated reticulin might be of prognostic significance. They developed a supervised AI model using an established cloud-base DL platform to test their hypothesis in a cohort of 101 consecutive HCC cases from their centre. Overall, using multivariate analysis (which included clinical and other pathological variables), a reduction (> 50%) in the ‘reticulin proportionate area’ was found to be an independent predictor of DFS and OS and the only independent predictor of metastasis. The authors recognise technical challenges in the recognition of reticulin by such AI models, including histological/staining artefacts such as aberrant tumour cytoplasmic positivity. Their system appears to be able to exclude such areas based upon morphology and context rather than pixel colour. They also recognise that histochemical stains for matrix proteins are susceptible to other technical issues, including variations in tissue fixation, processing, sectioning and staining protocols. Indeed, the impact of inconsistent staining for collagen in liver tissue between, and within, laboratories on the image analysis of fibrosis has been highlighted by others.12 Patil et al.11 describe a technique for internal normalisation to allow for reticulin staining variation within their own centre, but this does not address the issue of interlaboratory variations. While their results are certainly promising, as with translation into clinical practice of other AI solutions in pathology, implementation will face challenges. Others have highlighted the need to avoid ‘overfitting’, whereby a good performance on training data does not generalise well on external data sets.2 For AI to be in widespread routine clinical practice by 2030, there will need to be a greater focus upon the standardisation of pre-analytical, analytical and post-analytical elements of ‘augmented pathology’ and the mining of extensive data sets from many centres to ensure generalisability.",W2895484887; W3007100994; W3098164696; W3157377277; W4210897409; W4213208716; W4220993918; W4226330357; W4282041953; W4313594376; W4366998607; W4382631991,2,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37578315,Artificial Intelligence Chatbots: addressing the stochastic parrots in medical science,10.1097/MNM.0000000000001739,,,,,,Anshul Sharma; Rakesh Kumar; Sobhan Vinjamuri,W4385799335,"aDepartment of Nuclear Medicine, Homi Bhabha Cancer Hospital and Research Centre (Tata Memorial Centre), New Chandigarh bDepartment of Nuclear Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India cDepartment of Nuclear Medicine, Royal Liverpool and Broadgreen University Hospitals, NHS Trust, Liverpool, UK Received 30 June 2023 Accepted 22 July 2023. Correspondence to Dr. Rakesh Kumar, MD, PhD, Professor and Head, Diagnostic Nuclear Medicine, Department of Nuclear Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi 110029, India, Tel: +91 11 26594472; e-mail: [email protected]",W2937454300; W4313451803; W4315784554; W4317853296; W4319662928; W4361000349; W4361289889; W4376613087,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37473781,Embracing the Unprecedented Pace of Change: Artificial Intelligence's Impact on Dentistry and Beyond,10.1055/s-0043-1770913,,,,,,Hakan Türkkahraman,W4384925018,"Introduction Human beings have long exhibited a natural fear of the unknown. We tend to initially ignore novel changes, only to gradually adapt to them as they become familiar. However, the recent emergence of generative artificial intelligence (AI) applications has propelled us into an era of technological advancement comparable to the invention of electricity. The remarkable speed of these developments has caught many off guard, surpassing our expectations. It is not the change itself that instills fear but rather the breathtaking pace at which it unfolds. The challenge lies in ensuring that regulations keep pace with technological advancements to guide the responsible use of AI. In this guest editorial, we explore the transformative potential of AI in dentistry and emphasize the need to embrace change while maintaining ethical frameworks. Publication History Article published online: 20 July 2023 © 2023. The Author(s). This is an open access article published by Thieme under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, permitting unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction so long as the original work is properly cited. (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/) Thieme Medical and Scientific Publishers Pvt. Ltd. A-12, 2nd Floor, Sector 2, Noida-201301 UP, India",W3039618620; W3206094211; W3210915111; W4283791207; W4292833616; W4293181926; W4296713073; W4307033742; W4322495274; W4367051932; W4367189262; W4376603350,7,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37469045,The Emerging Role of Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare,10.47391/JPMA.23-48,,,,,,Farooq Azam Rathore; Muhammad Ahmad Rathore,W4381952038,"Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly being used in the field of healthcare to improve the efficiency and accuracy of medical diagnoses, treatment plans, and decisionmaking.1 It has the potential to transform the way healthcare is delivered and improve patient outcomes. There are many examples of the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare. One way that AI is being used in healthcare is by developing machine learning algorithms that can analyze vast amounts of patient data and identify patterns and trends that may not be immediately apparent to humans.2 This can be particularly useful in identifying early signs of diseases or conditions, allowing for earlier diagnosis, treatment, prognosis evaluation, and more. Another application of AI in healthcare is through the use of natural language processing (NLP). NLP systems can analyze electronic medical records (EMR) and extract important information, allowing healthcare providers to access and interpret patient data conveniently.3 This can help healthcare providers access and interpret patient data more easily, leading to more informed decision-making and better patient care. AI can also be used to assist with tasks such as image analysis, allowing for more accurate analysis and efficient diagnosis of medical images such as CT scans or X-rays.4 In addition, AI can be used to help automate routine tasks, freeing up healthcare providers to focus on more complex and higher-level tasks that require human expertise.5 Machine learning algorithms can analyze large amounts of patient data and identify patterns and trends that may not be immediately apparent to humans.6 This can be useful in identifying early signs of diseases or conditions, leading to earlier diagnosis and treatment. AI-powered chatbots or virtual assistants can help with tasks such as appointment scheduling and medication reminders.7 Systems that can assist with the automation of routine tasks, freeing up healthcare providers to focus on more complex and higher-level tasks that require human expertise. Predictive analytics systems can forecast patient outcomes and help healthcare providers make informed decisions about treatment plans.8 Personalized medicine systems can help tailor treatment plans to individual patients based on their specific needs and characteristics. These are just a few examples of the many ways in which AI is being used in healthcare. It is important to note that AI in healthcare is still in its early stages, and the potential applications of the technology are likely to expand significantly in the coming years.",W2082612446; W2120751691; W2756289758; W2895763047; W2897620540; W2953532875; W2973190536; W2977373448; W3030621456; W3038004684; W3086286002; W4288078032; W6959011865,32,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
