35086158,Comments on: “Endoscopic prediction of submucosal invasion in Barrett’s cancer with the use of artificial intelligence: a pilot study”,10.1055/a-1707-2265,,,,,,Zetao Liu; Linjie Guo,W4210559777,"See also: Endoscopic prediction of submucosal invasion in Barrett’s cancer with the use of artificial intelligence: a pilot study Endoscopy 2021; 53(09): 878-883 DOI: 10.1055/a-1311-8570 Ebigbo et al. recently trained an artificial intelligence (AI) system on the basis of deep artificial neural networks to differentiate between mucosal (T1a) and submucosal (T1b) Barrett’s cancer on white-light images, with sensitivity of 77 %, specificity of 64 %, and accuracy of 71 % [ 1 ]. Compared with five highly experienced endoscopists, there was no statistically significant difference. We congratulate the authors as the work has good application prospects and innovation, but it has certain limitations. The indications for endoscopic therapy for T1b sm1 esophageal adenocarcinoma are controversial. Submucosal adenocarcinoma was once considered unsuitable for endoscopic therapy, but more recent published guidelines consider endoscopic therapy as an alternative to surgery for T1b sm1 tumors under certain conditions [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ]. Whether tumors can be cured by endoscopic therapy depends not only on the depth of infiltration, but also on the lymphatic or vascular infiltration and degree of differentiation. According to such criteria for judgment, the final outcome of patients may also be different. Therefore, it is insufficient to differentiate between mucosal (T1a) and submucosal (T1b), as this alone cannot directly guide the choice of treatment strategy. This may limit application of the AI system. Secondly, the sample size of 230 lesion images is insufficient to train an AI system and the quality of images may influence the performance of the system. In endoscopy practice, endoscopists can judge a lesion through use of virtual chromoendoscopy and by observing dynamic characteristic such as the movement of the esophageal wall. Therefore, this study does not reflect the ideal situation in real-life settings. In addition, no normal images were included in the testing of the AI system and no P values were reported for the comparison of the Al system versus endoscopists. Although this study has some shortcomings, this is the first step toward developing an AI system to aid in the prediction of submucosal invasion of Barrett’s cancer. Publication History Article published online: 27 January 2022 © 2022. Thieme. All rights reserved. Georg Thieme Verlag KG Rüdigerstraße 14, 70469 Stuttgart, Germany",W2125681184; W2187093113; W2581415326; W3100528900,3,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34965240,Reply: Using Artificial Intelligence to Measure Facial Expression following Facial Reanimation Surgery,10.1097/PRS.0000000000008757,,,,,,Thanapoom Boonipat; Malke Asaad; Jason Lin; Graeme E. Glass; Samir Mardini; Mitchell Stotland,W4200021253,"We thank Dr. Dusseldorp and Dr. Hadlock for their response. We agree that the findings that were published previously are similar to ours in terms of the increased happiness expression postoperatively for patients with facial palsy.1 The difference with the machine learning algorithm that we implemented was that there were more cardinal facial expressions that the software was able to detect, which is arguably more representative of real-life expressions. The FaceReader software that we utilized was validated against 10,000 images from expert assessments. The software analyzes different action units of the face and computes the percentage of emotion by combining multiple action units, which is reflective of the complex nature behind facial expressions. In addition, FaceReader has been compared to the Facial Action Coding System and reported to have high validity, especially for smile analysis, and has been validated by multiple groups.2 We agree with the utility of using a simplistic approach of defining an emotionality quotient, but we wanted to take a step further to analyze more cardinal facial expressions. Given that a smile can be perceived as more than just happiness, we wanted to take a look from a more detailed aspect at the other emotions that could be mixed into the smile. We hoped to illustrate the utility of this software and therefore started with a smaller sample. Our method also allows for an automated and simple method of analysis that does not require any manual processing. We intend to investigate a larger analysis on different types of facial reanimation patients later. We thank the authors for their response and agree that using machine learning algorithms can improve on objectifiable outcomes for facial reanimation procedures. DISCLOSURE None of the authors has a financial interest to declare in relation to the content of this article. Thanapoom Boonipat, M.D.Malke Asaad, M.D.Jason Lin, B.S.Division of Plastic SurgeryDepartment of SurgeryMayo ClinicRochester, Minn. Graeme E. Glass, M.D.Division of Plastic SurgeryDepartment of SurgerySidra MedicineDepartment of SurgeryWeill Cornell Medicine–QatarDoha, Qatar Samir Mardini, M.D.Division of Plastic SurgeryDepartment of SurgeryMayo ClinicRochester, Minn. Mitchell Stotland, M.D.Division of Plastic SurgeryDepartment of SurgerySidra MedicineDepartment of SurgeryWeill Cornell Medicine–QatarDoha, Qatar",W2966049994; W2980594093,1,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34775714,[Artificial intelligence application in the field of cardiovascular imaging].,10.3760/cma.j.cn112148-20210730-00639,,,,,,J X Wang; Shihua Zhao,W3213414332,近年来，人工智能（AI）在心血管疾病影像学领域的研究方兴未艾，涉及图像分析、疾病诊断和预后评估等。在心血管超声、CT、MRI、核医学等无创影像领域，高质量的AI研究层出不穷，近两年发表的成果数量超越以往的总和，这无疑将会对心血管疾病的诊疗模式、医疗格局和资源分配产生深远影响。鉴于此，该文就AI影像在心血管疾病中的最新研究进展进行综述，以飨读者。.,,0,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
