33336488,Artificial intelligence for Australian and New Zealand surgeons: is it time for us to get more involved?,10.1111/ans.16262,,,,,,Ching‐Siang Cheng,W3115268505,"Artificial intelligence (AI) embraces us every day the moment we encounter any technology, from Google search to traffic cameras. Since the raucous Turing machine capable of cognition in the 1950s, AI took off in health care sometime in the 1970s, proliferating as commercial benefits began to manifest. The summers and winters of AI coincided with the waxing and waning of interest and funding. Today, AI in health care appears to be approaching its summer and is already active in data management and medical records, in drug development, telehealth and medical diagnostics. The ongoing coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic has also stimulated the growth of digital health applications. Our generation of surgeons in Australia and New Zealand are poised to become the early adopters of AI in surgical treatment.",W1553038132; W2169818249; W2576404523; W2581082771; W2623273412; W2810143521; W2888648021; W2909984744; W2996280595; W2998175747; W3024691543; W6685025714,1,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
