34432029,Commentary: Reporting Guidelines for Studies on Artificial Intelligence: What Neurosurgeons Should Know,10.1093/neuros/nyab331,,,,,,Nathan A. Shlobin; David Moher,W3194934048,"Increasingly, artificial intelligence (AI) has been used within neurosurgery to rapidly, comprehensively analyze larger quantities of data than can be analyzed by humans.1 Though AI investigations are largely preliminary given unproven real-life efficacy, AI algorithms may be applied to tasks in patient care, including enhancing accuracy and speed of diagnosis, augmenting clinical decision-making, providing intraoperative support, and developing accurate models for prognostication, as well as to health policy.1-3 As studies using AI become more prevalent within neurosurgical literature,1-3 it is important to ensure studies are methodologically rigorous and reported appropriately to allow for generalizability, validity, and applicability to clinical practice.4 We detail the role of reporting guidelines and discuss specific reporting guidelines for AI. Transparent, accurate, and detailed reporting...",W1550847922; W1779022892; W2002463857; W2050096609; W2144032281; W2342064264; W2414283705; W2739695028; W2741989018; W2763556273; W2768899399; W2909237159; W2989744286; W3083992516; W3122114034; W3136960503; W3173393692; W3180959755; W3202966610,1,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
