38352165,Editorial: Artificial intelligence and bioinformatics applications for omics and multi-omics studies,10.3389/fgene.2024.1371473,,,,,,Angelo M. Facchiano; Dominik Heider; Margherita Mutarelli,W4391358994,Artificial intelligence and bioinformatics applications for omics and multi- omics studies,W3002247544; W3016227741,4,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37923836,Letter to the Editor on “Comparative performance of fully-automated and semi-automated artificial intelligence methods for the detection of clinically significant prostate cancer on MRI: a systematic review”,10.1186/s13244-023-01520-8,,,,,,Alessandro Bevilacqua; Margherita Mottola,W4388284341,"We have read the article entitled “Comparative performance of fully-automated and semi-automated artificial intelligence methods for the detection of clinically significant prostate cancer on MRI: a systematic review” by Sushentsev et al. [1], recently published in Insights into Imaging , which also mentions our recent publication entitled “The primacy of high B-value 3 T-DWI radiom-ics in the prediction of clinically significant prostate cancer” [2]. In their comparative review, the Authors address several state-of-art research studies employing Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) and exploiting deep learning and machine learning methods for predicting clinically significant prostate cancer (csPCa). Accordingly, our work is cited because we compare the predictive performance achieved with b2000 Diffusion-Weighted Imaging (DWI b2000 ) and Apparent Diffusion Coefficient (ADC) MRI sequences to classify csPCa and non-csPCa (ncsPCa), finally stating the primacy of DWI b2000 , that provides by far the best results.",W3156007814; W4220782222,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37173156,Can AI language models replace human participants?,10.1016/j.tics.2023.04.008,,,,,,Danica Dillion; Niket Tandon; Yuling Gu; Kurt Gray,W4376117416,"Recent work suggests that language models such as GPT can make human-like judgments across a number of domains. We explore whether and when language models might replace human participants in psychological science. We review nascent research, provide a theoretical model, and outline caveats of using AI as a participant.",W2070850637; W3017217867; W3034344071; W3198287945; W4220993274; W4224235806; W4281776173; W4292947474; W4318919287; W4320499441; W4321455981; W4361866126; W4363624465; W6842080761; W6848496620; W6851152545; W6880308988,388,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
