37202987,"Population stratification strategies in artificial intelligence–based glaucoma monitoring, “corneal anthropology” to bridge gap between genetics and clinics?",10.4103/IJO.IJO_3061_22,,,,,,Rajan Sharma; Ashok Kumar Sharma,W4377044118,"Dear Editor, Artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to predict glaucoma progression by assessment of structural and functional changes. AI-based optic nerve head (ONH) evaluation has been shown to have high accuracy as a glaucoma prediction tool.[1] Glaucomatous optic nerve damage is characterized by an increased vertical C:D ratio (VCDR), and correcting VCDR to vertical disk diameter (VDD) enhances relevance to glaucoma.[2] A population-specific, large assembled database of VCDR, VDD, and images of the neuroretinal rim and average nerve fiber layer thickness from a optical coherence tomography (OCT) paired with a fundus photo are used in studies for glaucoma monitoring using the deep learning (DL) algorithm.[1,3] Studies have reported significant differences in optic disk parameters between different ethnic groups.[4] There are various strategies for stratification of the population in glaucoma studies and management. These may be self-reported race/ethnicity or genetically inferred ancestry (GIA). Most studies on Primary open angle glaucoma (POAG) use self-reported ethnic and racial identities, which may be biased and inaccurate.[5] In countries with “multi-ethnic” societies like the USA (USA), genome-wide association studies (GWAS) use stratified population data as defined by the national US census.[6] Fang et al.[6] also observed that most GWAS use self-identified race/ethnicity (SIRE) for population stratification and adjust GIA within SIRE. They proposed a new supervised learning algorithm to define categorical stratification in multi-ethnic GWAS, which they termed harmonized ancestry and race/ethnicity (HARE).[6] HARE is an algorithm which aims to improve interpretation in multi-ethnic GWAS studies. It combines information on both race and ethnicity and genetic ancestry.[6] GIA aims at identifying an individual’s population of origin based on natural population group. This is a much more accurate and reliable estimation of the patient’s ancestral origin than SIRE.[7] Ancestry proportion varies greatly between individuals and should not be confused with race or ethnicity and may overlap between different population subgroups.[6,8] For example, African-Americans from one part of the USA were found to have around 20% European ancestry.[8] The difference in the ancestry proportion of an individual has a significant effect on disease prevalence and severity. Studies have reported specific mitochondrial haplogroups as the risk factors in POAG.[9] Ancestry has been found to be a risk factor for POAG progression by using the quantitative assessment of ancestry as an independent variable.[10] The study found higher African ancestry to be a risk factor for POAG in self-identified African-Americans and concluded that ancestry analysis can be used as a possible population screening tool for glaucoma assessment.[10] Han et al.[11] used a large collection of images and compared various genetically defined ancestry groups. Results showed African ancestry to have a higher risk for glaucoma and larger VDD than Asian ancestry. Also, VDCR was found to be larger in Asians and Africans than in Europeans. Earlier studies showed Africans to have a higher VCDR than whites.[12] GWAS have been done to identify the genetic loci accounting for intraocular pressure (IOP) and central corneal thickness (CCT) variability as well.[13] Up to 14.2% of CCT variation can be explained on the basis of multi-ethnic GWAS. CCT is a highly inheritable trait with a coefficient of up to 0.95. It needs to be studied for its variation between various population groups. Genes like COL8A2, COL5A1, COL1A1, and COL1A2 encoding for collagen in stroma, ZNF469 and AVGR8 encoding for transcription factors, and AKAP13 and IBTK for signal transduction affecting corneal development influence CCT variation between populations of different origins.[14] CCT variation between racial/ethnic-based stratified population groups is well reported. For instance, African races and African-Americans have significantly thinner CCT than whites, apart from variation between other races as well.[15,16] Racial subgroup-specific baseline CCT is a must in multiracial heterogenous society, and CCT, being highly heritable, may be a “substantial mediator” of relationship between race and glaucoma.[15] Thinner CCT, being an independent predictor of glaucoma (POAG) progression, affects IOP by underestimation on applanation tonometry, and this influence of CCT on the accuracy of applanation tonometry in glaucoma and ocular hypertension has been recently re-emphasized by Brandt et al.[17] Baseline CCT data from specific subpopulations may have implications for AI-based algorithms used to treat glaucoma and other corneal applications such as applanation tonometry. Also, CCT may influence the structural/mechanical susceptibility of the optic nerve head to IOP changes in glaucoma.[15,18] We have earlier highlighted the importance of local population-based baseline CCT identification and subsequent correction of IOP under a new term “corneal anthropology.”[19] Another effect of CCT is through corneal biomechanics, and racial differences in measurements of corneal hysteresis (CH) have been recorded, Chinese, for instance, having lower CH than Indians.[20] There should be population-specific data collection of CCT and optic disk parameters (VCDR, VDD) and further research on their correlation. Consensus on population stratification strategies (racial/ethnic categorization or GIA) and their application in glaucoma clinics is still lacking. More research is needed on establishing a database of race/ancestry-based baseline CCT, VCDR, and VDD, which will further help in refining datasets used by AI-based algorithms for predicting/monitoring glaucoma [Fig. 1]. An ophthalmologist managing glaucoma, especially in the developing world, may not know much about GWAS or AI-based algorithms and their application. Better knowledge sharing and coordination is needed between anthropologists (identifying racial background) and genetic scientists (doing GIA/GWAS), so that more easy applications of AI-based algorithms (shift from “Black box” toward “explainable AI)[1] are available to ophthalmologists in glaucoma clinics.Figure 1: Glaucoma service flowchart showing possible role of data collection for specific racial/ethnic/ancestry-based groups (modified from Mursch-Edlmayr et al.)[ 1 ]Financial support and sponsorship Nil. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest.",W1990931081; W1995312072; W2009844809; W2013276680; W2066192418; W2096376670; W2106538068; W2126377392; W2129316818; W2139762445; W2607831630; W2889832720; W2890035583; W2897507310; W2976869024; W3035510112; W3096061254; W3131822128; W3159870562; W3164496322; W4237930359,0,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
