36409207,In defence of the machines: How artificial intelligence may help to improve dermatologic outcomes and diminish barriers and disparities in care,10.1111/ajd.13956,,,,,,Shayan Cheraghlou,W4309512825,"Advances in computing hardware such as graphics processing units and progress in deep learning (DL) techniques have led to rapid recent expansion in the use of DL across industries. Despite promising success, DL models are not without limitations. An important point of contention has been the potential of algorithms to codify and perpetuate societal disparities. In dermatology, where the most common use of DL algorithms has been generation of diagnoses from clinical images, it has been argued that training data sets for these models lack images of darker skin types, leading to poorer performance for these patients.1 However, provided that adequate attention is given to preventing these differences and correcting them if they arise, artificial intelligence (AI) also has the potential to significantly diminish both barriers and disparities in dermatologic care. First, it is important to recognize current limitations in the care of patients with skin of colour (SOC). Recent work has highlighted the persistent underrepresentation of darker skin types in the clinical images in textbooks used to train dermatology residents.2 For trainees who do not see many patients with SOC in their resident clinics, this may diminish their ability to accurately diagnose and treat these patients as they start their practice. Indeed, a survey of practicing dermatologists and dermatology residents in the United States revealed that 47% felt that their medical training was inadequate in training them on diseases in SOC.3 While changes to educational resources will be helpful in training future generations of dermatologists, it will be decades before a sizeable portion of the workforce has undergone such curricula. In contrast, with the recognition of the scarcity of images of SOC in a model's training set, images can be added, and the model retrained and updated in weeks rather than decades. Furthermore, integration of DL models into clinical decisionmaking tools may ameliorate existing disparities in dermatologic care. For example, Black Medicare beneficiaries are 70% less likely than White beneficiaries to receive biologic treatment for moderatetosevere psoriasis, controlling for other characteristics.4 An algorithmic approach using diseasespecific features optimized for clinical outcomes is likely to both improve response among all patients and to diminish racial disparities in treatment selection by guiding the selection of the optimal biologic therapy using only diseasespecific factors as model inputs. Additionally, widespread availability of accurate dermatologic diagnostic tools would be especially helpful for communities with difficulties in accessing dermatologic care. Dermatologists are not uniformly distributed and accessible, with minority and rural communities having much lower densities of practicing dermatologists.5 While access to these tools is certainly not equivalent to access to a dermatologist, they may be used as an adjunct to increase diagnostic accuracy in primary care visits and expedite referrals to dermatology for urgent conditions. Ultimately, dermatologic practice stands to be significantly impacted by AI. If dermatologists actively engage with these technologies, optimize their integration within our clinical workflow and identify shortcomings, DL models are likely to expand the reach of the field while allowing for better patient outcomes and decreased barriers and disparities in care.",W941262555; W3019100240; W3022776589; W3181273692,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35908985,Artificial Intelligence in Pneumology: Diagnostic and Prognostic Utilities.,10.1016/j.arbres.2022.07.007,,,,,,José Luis Izquierdo; Clara Lillian Oeste; Ignacio Hernández Medrano,W4286251273,,W1985521297; W2014725879; W2046416745; W3084565377; W3093928028; W3096610536; W3119252617; W3119947644; W3213912342; W4210422744; W4407052208,6,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35365318,"Although depth prediction of colorectal cancer with artificial intelligence is clinically relevant, standardization of histopathologic diagnosis should also be taken care of",10.1016/j.gie.2022.02.008,,,,,,Yutaka Saito; Masayoshi Yamada; Yuichi Mori,W4221051694,"The article by Lu et al1Lu Z. Xu Y. Yao L. et al.Real-time automated diagnosis of colorectal cancer invasion depth using a deep learning model with multimodal data (with video).Gastrointest Endosc. 2022; 95: 1186-1194.e3Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (2) Google Scholar introduces a novel deep learning algorithm (Endo-CRC), which is able to estimate the invasion depth of colorectal cancer (CRC) and therefore provide information about whether a lesion can be removed endoscopically. This is the first artificial intelligence (AI) model to analyze both white-light and image-enhanced endoscopy (IEE)2Tamai N. Saito Y. Sakamoto T. et al.Visualization of laterally spreading colorectal tumors by using image-enhanced endoscopy.Gastroenterol Res Pract. 2012; 2012: 638391Crossref Scopus (8) Google Scholar at the same time to assess the invasive nature of early-stage CRC. The authors retrospectively assessed the developed AI tool by using 3 different test data: internal still images, internal video data, and external still images taken from the hospitals that did not provide any endoscopic images for the development of the AI model. In the evaluation of the still images, Endo-CRC worked quite well, which was comparable to the endoscopists’ performance. Furthermore, Endo-CRC achieved 100% accuracy during the assessment of the video-based data, with acceptable processing speed. In the field of endoscopy, especially colonoscopy, many AI tools have been developed and are commercially available, including computer-aided detection and characterization of polyps.3Yamada M. Saito Y. Imaoka H. et al.Development of a real-time endoscopic image diagnosis support system using deep learning technology in colonoscopy.Sci Rep. 2019; 9: 14465Crossref PubMed Scopus (102) Google Scholar, 4Yamada M. Saito Y. Yamada S. et al.Detection of flat colorectal neoplasia by artificial intelligence: a systematic review.Best Pract Res Clin Gastroenterol. 2021; 52-53: 101745Crossref Scopus (8) Google Scholar, 5Tamai N. Saito Y. Sakamoto T. et al.Effectiveness of computer-aided diagnosis of colorectal lesions using novel software for magnifying narrow-band imaging: a pilot study.Endosc Int Open. 2017; 5: E690-E694Crossref PubMed Google Scholar, 6Kamba S. Tamai N. Saitoh I. et al.Reducing adenoma miss rate of colonoscopy assisted by artificial intelligence: a multicenter randomized controlled trial.J Gastroenterol. 2021; 56: 746-757Crossref PubMed Scopus (14) Google Scholar However, endoscopic assessment of the preoperative risk of lymph node metastasis (LNM) has not been thoroughly investigated in these AI-related research studies. The clinical guidelines clearly state that endoscopic treatment should be limited to submucosally invasive CRC, which has no or minimum risk of LNM.7Hashiguchi Y. Muro K. Saito Y. et al.Japanese Society for Cancer of the Colon and Rectum (JSCCR) guidelines 2019 for the treatment of colorectal cancer.Int J Clin Oncol. 2020; 25: 1-42Crossref PubMed Scopus (652) Google Scholar To follow these guidelines and maximize the benefit to patients, preoperative prediction of the risk of LNM is mandatory. Specifically, the risk factors for LNM include submucosal (SM) deep invasion, lymphovascular invasion, worse histologic grade (eg, poorly differentiated carcinoma), and extended budding grade. Among these factors, only SM invasion depth can be estimated before treatment; therefore, assessment of SM invasion depth before treatment is clinically important.7Hashiguchi Y. Muro K. Saito Y. et al.Japanese Society for Cancer of the Colon and Rectum (JSCCR) guidelines 2019 for the treatment of colorectal cancer.Int J Clin Oncol. 2020; 25: 1-42Crossref PubMed Scopus (652) Google Scholar Although endoscopic prediction of the SM invasion is challenging, several attempts have been done mainly by Japanese research groups with the aid of advanced endoscopic technologies. Previous studies have shown that the diagnostic accuracy of the depth of invasion can be increased by ≥5% with the addition of optical magnification in comparison with a conventional endoscopic diagnosis of nonmagnified white-light imaging.8Kudo S. Kashida H. Nakajima T. et al.Endoscopic diagnosis and treatment of early colorectal cancer.World J Surg. 1997; 21: 694-701Crossref PubMed Scopus (236) Google Scholar,9Ikehara H. Saito Y. Matsuda T. et al.Diagnosis of depth of invasion for early colorectal cancer using magnifying colonoscopy.J Gastroenterol Hepatol. 2010; 25: 905-912Crossref PubMed Scopus (52) Google Scholar We have also reported a diagnostic accuracy of 85% sensitivity and 99% specificity by using the invasive pattern, and we implement it to diagnose the depth of cancer in daily clinical practice.10Matsuda T. Fujii T. Saito Y. et al.Efficacy of the invasive/non-invasive pattern by magnifying chromoendoscopy to estimate the depth of invasion of early colorectal neoplasms.Am J Gastroenterol. 2008; 103: 2700-2706Crossref PubMed Scopus (259) Google Scholar Recently, a 500-fold magnification colonoscope has been also developed. Kudo et al11Kudo S.E. Mori Y. Wakamura K. et al.Endocytoscopy can provide additional diagnostic ability to magnifying chromoendoscopy for colorectal neoplasms.J Gastroenterol Hepatol. 2014; 29: 83-90Crossref PubMed Scopus (28) Google Scholar reported that it provides an additional benefit in predicting invasion depth over optical magnification. However, it is well known that optical diagnosis of invasive cancer requires considerable experience and expertise. This has motivated several research groups to develop AI tools that can standardize this complicated process. Although no such AI tools have been on the market, several companies are working on this very important clinical challenge. Fujifilm Corp (Tokyo, Japan) has released CAD-EYE,12Neumann H. Kreft A. Sivanathan V. et al.Evaluation of novel LCI CAD EYE system for real time detection of colon polyps.PLoS One. 2021; 16e0255955Crossref Scopus (6) Google Scholar which can provide binary classifications between neoplasms and nonneoplasms, and the company plans to release depth diagnosis with magnified blue-light imaging as a next step.13Sakamoto T, Nakashima H, Nakamura K, et al. Performance of computer-aided detection and diagnosis of colorectal polyps compares to that of experienced endoscopists. Dig Dis Sci. Epub 2021 Aug 17.Google Scholar NEC Corp (Tokyo, Japan) is also working on new AI software that can differentiate invasive cancer with magnified IEE in addition to the binary classifier (neoplasm vs nonneoplasm).3Yamada M. Saito Y. Imaoka H. et al.Development of a real-time endoscopic image diagnosis support system using deep learning technology in colonoscopy.Sci Rep. 2019; 9: 14465Crossref PubMed Scopus (102) Google Scholar Both products are currently under the regulatory clearance process. The new AI algorithm reported in the article by Lu et al1Lu Z. Xu Y. Yao L. et al.Real-time automated diagnosis of colorectal cancer invasion depth using a deep learning model with multimodal data (with video).Gastrointest Endosc. 2022; 95: 1186-1194.e3Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (2) Google Scholar is distinct because it did not use optical zoom but instead used 2 modalities, white-light imaging and IEE, for the cancer depth diagnosis. In view of the limited availability of magnifying endoscopes in the West, nonuse of magnification would be the strength of the AI tool. In addition, inasmuch as the developed software was able to provide a prediction based on video data, its performance could be further improved with thorough air exchange and by taking several images from different angles and distances. However, the excellent performance of the introduced model may not be generalizable because of the small sample size in the study.1Lu Z. Xu Y. Yao L. et al.Real-time automated diagnosis of colorectal cancer invasion depth using a deep learning model with multimodal data (with video).Gastrointest Endosc. 2022; 95: 1186-1194.e3Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (2) Google Scholar Actually, the authors included only 23 slightly invasive SM cancers and 29 deeply invasive SM cancers in the study,1Lu Z. Xu Y. Yao L. et al.Real-time automated diagnosis of colorectal cancer invasion depth using a deep learning model with multimodal data (with video).Gastrointest Endosc. 2022; 95: 1186-1194.e3Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (2) Google Scholar which appears too weak to enable generalization of the conclusion. We look forward to the next version with more cases used for both machine learning and validation. Another critical point to be noted is the unreliability of the measurement of the SM invasion depth. Usually, 1000-μm or deeper invasion into the submucosal layer is regarded as deeply invasive cancer. According to the Japanese guidelines, if a mucosal muscularis mucosae can be identified, invasion depth should be measured from the muscularis mucosae to the edge of the cancer invasion; if not, it should be measured from the top of the tumor surface.14Kitajima K. Fujimori T. Fujii S. et al.Correlations between lymph node metastasis and depth of submucosal invasion in submucosal invasive colorectal carcinoma: a Japanese collaborative study.J Gastroenterol. 2004; 39: 534-543Crossref PubMed Scopus (489) Google Scholar,15Tanaka S. Kashida H. Saito Y. et al.Japan Gastroenterological Endoscopy Society guidelines for colorectal endoscopic submucosal dissection/endoscopic mucosal resection.Dig Endosc. 2020; 32: 219-239Crossref PubMed Scopus (115) Google Scholar However, these complicated criteria introduce much concern about the reliability of the measured invasion depth. Actually, there is considerable interobserver variability between pathologists in the measurement of this invasion depth.14Kitajima K. Fujimori T. Fujii S. et al.Correlations between lymph node metastasis and depth of submucosal invasion in submucosal invasive colorectal carcinoma: a Japanese collaborative study.J Gastroenterol. 2004; 39: 534-543Crossref PubMed Scopus (489) Google Scholar,15Tanaka S. Kashida H. Saito Y. et al.Japan Gastroenterological Endoscopy Society guidelines for colorectal endoscopic submucosal dissection/endoscopic mucosal resection.Dig Endosc. 2020; 32: 219-239Crossref PubMed Scopus (115) Google Scholar This will be a big hurdle to the creation of standardized AI for depth prediction because the criterion standard for machine learning (ie, depth of invasion) is variable and thus is unreliable. Apart from the unreliability of the ground truth, another issue is coming up in the management of submucosally invasive cancer. Recently, it has been reported that the risk of LNM is quite low (only a few percentage points) when predictors other than invasion depth are negative,15Tanaka S. Kashida H. Saito Y. et al.Japan Gastroenterological Endoscopy Society guidelines for colorectal endoscopic submucosal dissection/endoscopic mucosal resection.Dig Endosc. 2020; 32: 219-239Crossref PubMed Scopus (115) Google Scholar and thus the invasive depth of 1000 μm may be expanded or eliminated in the future guidelines. Furthermore, the accuracy of prediction of invasion depth varies according to tumor morphology, such as polypoid growth or nonpolypoid growth.16Aizawa D, Sugino T, Oishi T, et al. The essential problem of over-measuring the depth of submucosal invasion in pT1 colorectal cancer. Virchows Arch. Epub 2021 Nov 5.Google Scholar Subclassification of laterally spreading tumors such as granular type (LST-G) and nongranular-type (LST-NG) also affects the accuracy of the invasion depth prediction.17Uraoka T. Saito Y. Matsuda T. et al.Endoscopic indications for endoscopic mucosal resection of laterally spreading tumours in the colorectum.Gut. 2006; 55: 1592-1597Crossref PubMed Scopus (342) Google Scholar,18Yamada M. Saito Y. Sakamoto T. et al.Endoscopic predictors of deep submucosal invasion in colorectal laterally spreading tumors.Endoscopy. 2016; 48: 456-464Crossref PubMed Scopus (53) Google Scholar Actually, LST-NG is difficult to diagnose because of the presence of multifocal SM infiltration without a type V pit pattern on the surface.17Uraoka T. Saito Y. Matsuda T. et al.Endoscopic indications for endoscopic mucosal resection of laterally spreading tumours in the colorectum.Gut. 2006; 55: 1592-1597Crossref PubMed Scopus (342) Google Scholar,18Yamada M. Saito Y. Sakamoto T. et al.Endoscopic predictors of deep submucosal invasion in colorectal laterally spreading tumors.Endoscopy. 2016; 48: 456-464Crossref PubMed Scopus (53) Google Scholar When we consider the complexity and heterogeneity of colorectal neoplasms, more numbers of cases will be needed for the machine learning and validation processes of reliable AI tools in the future. We also believe that multimodal pathologic diagnosis and metastasis prediction19Kudo S.E. Ichimasa K. Villard B. et al.Artificial intelligence system to determine risk of T1 colorectal cancer metastasis to lymph node.Gastroenterology. 2021; 160: 1075-1084 e2Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (49) Google Scholar will be necessary in the future, with pathologic images incorporated into the analysis.20Takamatsu M. Yamamoto N. Kawachi H. et al.Prediction of early colorectal cancer metastasis by machine learning using digital slide images.Comput Methods Programs Biomed. 2019; 178: 155-161Crossref PubMed Scopus (25) Google Scholar With this advance, text-based, variable pathologic diagnoses will not be required. In the diagnosis of cancer depth, we should be most careful to avoid unnecessary surgery. Even if deep invasion is found after endoscopic removal, recent advances in endoscopic submucosal dissection21Saito Y. Abe S. Inoue H. et al.How to perform a high-quality endoscopic submucosal dissection.Gastroenterology. 2021; 161: 405-410Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (7) Google Scholar,22Saito Y. Uraoka T. Yamaguchi Y. et al.A prospective, multicenter study of 1111 colorectal endoscopic submucosal dissections (with video).Gastrointest Endosc. 2010; 72: 1217-1225Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (537) Google Scholar have shown that there is no adverse effect on the patient prognosis if margin-free resection is completed with negative margins.23Takamaru H. Saito Y. Sekiguchi M. et al.Endoscopic resection before surgery does not affect the recurrence rate in patients with high-risk T1 colorectal cancer.Clin Transl Gastroenterol. 2021; 12e00336Crossref Scopus (6) Google Scholar It is important, therefore, not to misdiagnose a shallow lesion as a deep one. From this point of view, the diagnostic specificity to differentiate deep invasion should be close to 100% for actual use in practice. In this regard, there will be room for improvement in the presented AI device. The article by Lu et al1Lu Z. Xu Y. Yao L. et al.Real-time automated diagnosis of colorectal cancer invasion depth using a deep learning model with multimodal data (with video).Gastrointest Endosc. 2022; 95: 1186-1194.e3Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF Scopus (2) Google Scholar gives us a huge expectation toward the improved optical diagnosis of CRC with the aid of AI. Future research should be focused on the collection of more numbers and more varieties of learning material to generalize the study results. We should also rethink how to assess the reliability of the pathologic diagnoses, which are believed to be the criterion standard of everything. Dr Mori is a consultant for Olympus Corp and has an ownership interest in Cybernet System Corp. The other authors disclosed no financial relationships. Real-time automated diagnosis of colorectal cancer invasion depth using a deep learning model with multimodal data (with video)Gastrointestinal EndoscopyVol. 95Issue 6PreviewThe optical diagnosis of colorectal cancer (CRC) invasion depth with white light (WL) and image-enhanced endoscopy (IEE) remains challenging. We aimed to construct and validate a 2-modal deep learning–based system, incorporated with both WL and IEE images (named Endo-CRC) in estimating the invasion depth of CRC. Full-Text PDF",W1613991498; W2009124022; W2029487819; W2038065536; W2042790937; W2047344407; W2144545582; W2155579604; W2312150800; W2742672216; W2953033020; W2979904523; W2996957344; W3088023123; W3156142187; W3156783185; W3166456620; W3178851191; W3193539667; W3195394248; W3210131000; W4200192474; W4211133582,1,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35177258,Can artificial intelligence be your angel to diagnose early gastric cancer in real clinical practice?,10.1016/j.gie.2021.12.042,,,,,,Seiichiro Abe; Yutaka Tomizawa; Yutaka Saito,W4213166130,,W1963756790; W1985517414; W1999409447; W2078376767; W2082998411; W2119813479; W2260439334; W2284859774; W2406205248; W2591332913; W2607024525; W2691799169; W2901534201; W2978940166; W3015708637; W3024896321; W3042541362; W3089235807; W3094309999; W3100293787; W4200129153; W6640987521,1,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34652003,Contribution of the Japan Gastroenterological Endoscopy Society to promote computer‐aided diagnosis/detection system development using artificial intelligence technology,10.1111/den.14146,,,,,,Hiromu Kutsumi,W3207126487,"This paper outlines what the Japan Gastroenterological Endoscopy Society (JGES) has done in terms of regulatory science for the development and spreading of gastrointestinal endoscopy and related medical devices. Since 2015, JGES had established an industry–government–academia social gathering and held regular meetings for the purpose of promoting research and development and commercialization of gastrointestinal endoscopes and related devices. At this social gathering, industry, regulatory agencies, and academia were able to understand their respective positions and share their problems. This became one of the contributions to the development of gastrointestinal endoscopic medicine. This social gathering completed in 2018, and then JGES focused on the issues of endoscopic device development using artificial intelligence (AI). In recent years, social implementation of AI technology has progressed, AI research has been activated in medical care, and many JGES members have participated in such research. By 2018 more than 13 Japanese companies were developing AI software to support endoscopic diagnosis. Utilization of AI in the medical field is highly expected, but on the other hand, there have been the following problems: consent and personal information protection in collecting medical information, evaluation of device performance, quality control, compensation for misjudgment. Therefore, JGES established the “AI Promotion Review Committee” in October 2018, to provide certain guidelines for the development of AI endoscopes and related devices and to promote appropriate development, so that they would not be distributed in a disorderly manner. Immediately after the establishment of committee, discussions were held with the advisors from Pharmaceuticals and Medical Devices Agency (PMDA) and the Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare, and a notification was issued to members on June 1, 2019. This notice provided regulatory information. The accuracy of endoscopic diagnosis depends on the skill of the endoscopist, and there is difference in diagnostic ability between expert endoscopists and non-expert endoscopists. There are two differences. One is the difference in lesion identification. The other is the difference in the qualitative diagnosis of the lesion, such as the differentiation of benign and malignant tumors. By using computer-aided diagnosis/detection (CAD) which has learned the answers of experts using AI technology, it is expected that the diagnostic ability of non-experts will be improved. In addition, experts can be also expected to prevent oversight of lesions and assist in qualitative diagnosis. These will lead to the provision of appropriate medical care and contribution to patients, and can be expected to be effective in the medical economy. As a project commissioned by the Japan Agency for Medical Research and Development (AMED), JGES collected images including diagnostic data and built a database in cooperation with the National Institute of Informatics for 2 years from 2017 (https://www.jges.net/news/2018/05/18/33064). From 2019, the JGES performed the following research, “Development of information and communications technology infrastructure linked to integrated database for prototype AI device development in the field of gastrointestinal endoscopy” and “Formulation of guidelines for empirical research for public cloud of AI device development infrastructure and its problem extraction” (https://www.jges.net/medical/newslist/clinical_research). The following research derived from above projects are still ongoing: “Research on AI-CAD accuracy improvement of gastric cancer”, “Disease classification by AI-CAD for inflammatory bowel disease”, “Morphological classification of duodenal papilla by AI-CAD” and “AI-CAD to prevent oversight”. In addition, for the purpose of accelerating the development of AI-CAD, JGES is working on the automation of structuring and parameterizing pathological diagnosis description by AI language analysis. Furthermore, for the purpose of continuous development of AI research and out-licensing to companies, “Research on ethical aspects for data collection and data utilization” and “Research on datasets for AI device development and AI medical device evaluation” have also started. In the gastrointestinal field, on December 6, 2018, colonoscopic computer-aided diagnosis (CADx) software “EndoBRAIN” co-developed by Professor Shinei Kudo's group of Showa University Northern Yokohama Hospital, Professor Kensaku Mori of Nagoya University Informatics, and Cybernet Systems Co Ltd (Tokyo, Japan) was approved and marketed by Olympus Co., Ltd. (Tokyo, Japan) for the first time in the world. “EndoBRAIN” was developed by learning about 69,000 endoscopic images based on the AI machine learning method (support vector machine). It has been shown to distinguish between neoplastic and non-neoplastic colorectal polyps with 96.9% sensitivity, 100% specificity, and 98% accuracy. These values were all significantly higher than those of the endoscopy trainees and was comparable to the accuracy rate of specialists.1 Since then, they released computer-aided detection (CADe) software “EndoBRAIN-EYE” and CADx software which added diagnostic capabilities for invasive cancer to EndoBRAIN “EndoBRAIN-Plus”. In October 2020, “CAD EYE” developed by Fujifilm, which combines CADe and CADx, was approved. Subsequently, in November 2020, the CADx “WISE VISION Endoscopy” co-developed by the National Cancer Center and NEC Corporation was approved. All of these are intended for colorectal tumors. So far, AI-CAD for inflammatory bowel disease, gastric lesions, and biliary-pancreatic lesions has not been approved in Japan. However, the “WISE VISION Endoscopy” has developed a technology to detect tumors in Barrett's esophagus and has obtained the CE mark, which is the European safety standard, for the first time in the world. For gastric cancer, Tada et al. are actively working on the development of AI-CAD.2, 3 In addition, research and development of AI-CAD for not only diagnosis of gastrointestinal tract by endoscopy and also diagnosis of pancreas cancer by endoscopic ultrasound are also underway by Japanese endoscopists.4 AI technology is becoming more and more common in our daily lives. Incorporating these technologies into medical care is natural and necessary. Also, it is expected to contribute to the progress of medical care. In the field of gastrointestinal endoscopy, AI-CAD was first put into practical use as a diagnostic aid for neoplastic diseases of the colon. In the near future, AI-CAD will be widespread, not only in neoplastic lesion but also in inflammatory disease, and not only for colon but also for esophagus, stomach, duodenum, small intestine, and pancreatobiliary disease.5-7 The evolution of these AI devices requires a large amount of accurately annotated teacher data. And how quickly and in large quantities these teacher data can be collected will determine the speed of development. To do so, it is necessary to build a data bank that complies with the regulations regarding the protection of personal information. It is also important to recognize regulatory approval from the early stages of development. In order to be approved by the regulatory affairs, it is necessary to prove the effectiveness and safety of AI-CAD devices as scientific evidence. Approval is the first step in the spread of AI-CAD. The next step is to list up insurance. If the use of AI-CAD is covered by insurance, it will be adopted by many hospitals and will become widespread in the medical field. In order to be covered by insurance, it is necessary to present the impact of AI-CAD on the medical economy as scientific evidence. It is convinced that the widespread use of AI-CAD in the medical field will lead to the best medical care for patients and the improvement of quality of life in the endoscopist's workplace. However, if the quality of those AI-CAD is not guaranteed, it will cause confusion in the medical field. JGES is a group of experts in the field of gastrointestinal endoscopy. All of us JGES members will need to build an annotated image database that anyone can use freely in order to develop a better AI-CAD. Also, it is also expected to build a data infrastructure that can prove the economic benefits of using AI-CAD. Author declares no conflict of interest for this article. None.",W2903886348; W2909293781; W2973140425; W3024896321; W3042541362; W3085806799; W3087297434,1,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34482571,Artificial intelligence meets clinical skills teaching,10.1111/medu.14636,,,,,,Robin Stone; Riley Reel; Laura Farrell,W3197459981,"care needs. Students read the proposed bill and background information; they then chose their own pathway to pursue advocacy for the bill. For example, a student could choose between contacting the state medical society for advocacy advice or consulting with other physicians for guidance. At another decision point, a student could choose between ‘attending’ a legislative health committee meeting (by listening to an archived recording), or arranging a simulated meeting with their state representative to discuss support of the bill. All students completed a position ‘one-pager’ with relevant points and an ‘ask’. Students followed the bill-to-law process to signing by the state governor. Final steps included creation of individualized SMART goals for ongoing learning and competency-based skills development.",W2058373465,3,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
