33128548,Artificial intelligence modeling of vascular calcification,10.1093/abbs/gmaa106,,,,,,Mingjun Gu; Lin Zhou; Xing Wang; Chao Shi; Xiangqi Li,W3095174869,"Vascular calcification is a pathological process during which deposits of calcium mineral in vessel walls are progressively formed, leading to tissue stiffening, plaque rupture, and heart failure [1]. The mortality rate is 3% among non-calcification individuals, while it reaches 28% in patients with high levels of vascular calcification [2]. Despite rapid advances in the understanding of this health problem, medical treatments targeting vascular calcification are still lacking. There are no approved therapeutic drugs available except the costly invasive transcatheter procedures and surgeries that do not fully address the wide spectrum of these conditions [3]. Unfortunately, current studies of vascular calcification are far behind the actual needs, probably because vascular calcification, especially microcalcification, is difficult to diagnose and treat. Traditionally, vascular calcification was believed to be a passive and degenerative process of human aging. Now, evidence is piling up that vascular calcification is an active process that provides a potential opportunity for effective therapeutic intervention. Almost all the most dreadful human diseases, such as diabetes, cancer, cardiovascular disease, stroke, chronic kidney failure, metabolic diseases, and infectious diseases, are associated with vascular calcification [1,3,4]. Meanwhile, most biological processes are involved in calcific diseases, including transcriptional regulation, mitochondrial dysfunction, endoplasmic reticulum stress, oxidative stress, inflammation, apoptosis, calcium and phosphate signaling, matrix remodeling, cellular senescence, and so on [3,5,6]. These molecular mechanisms form a complex regulatory network that makes it difficult to get to the bottom of things. In addition, the combination of molecular imaging with big data of multi-omics based on cellular, animal, and clinical levels further complicates our understanding of calcification in the pathology of diseases. So, the study of vascular calcification calls for the involvement of new technology in addressing these extremely complicated events of vascular calcification. Artificial intelligence (AI), also known as machine intelligence, is a computational mimicking of human intelligence [7]. It aims to imbue software with the power to analyze the target environment using either predetermined rules and search algorithms, or pattern recognizing machine learning models to make decisions. In other words, it attempts to mimic biological intelligence to act with varying degrees of autonomy, thereby reducing manual human intervention. The rise of AI is revolutionizing the development of science and technology [8,9]. Recently, Schwalbe and Wahl [10] presented four categories relevant to global health about AI-driven health interventions: diagnosis of disease, risk assessment of patient morbidity or mortality, prediction and surveillance of disease outbreak, and health policy and planning. They called on the global health community to work quickly to establish guidelines for the development, test, and use of a user-driven research agenda to facilitate equitable and ethical use. Mehta et al. [11] suggested that three key challenges need to be addressed for AI to fulfill its promise to improve global health, i.e. the reliability and availability of data; the application of AI tools in the health systems; and the lack of regulations that oversee and manage the rapidly changing technology. These visions provide us with very good inspiration to study vascular calcification. To overcome the difficulties in diagnosis and treatment of vascular calcification-associated diseases and unravel the extremely complicated events of vascular calcification, AI has its unique advantage to provide useful information for our understanding of vascular calcification, which forms the key basis for the treatment of diseases associated with vascular calcification. Nevertheless, current involvement of AI in the research of vascular calcification is in its infancy, and AI is most frequently applied in improving the efficiency of image recognition [3,12]. Thus, here we discuss macro AI models that can be used to promote the research and application of vascular calcification. Four key types of AI models need to be developed to investigate vascular calcification (Fig. 1): (1) AI models for the discovery of molecular markers of calcification in patients by analyzing transcriptome, proteomics and metabolomics from extracellular vesicles, extracellular matrixes, pro-inflammatory cytokines, and ion contents; (2) AI models for the identification of therapeutic targets of calcification by integrating big data of multi-omics from animal and clinical information; (3) AI models for the exploration of correlations between calcification levels and different stages of diseases by assembling data information and images from computed tomography, density-dependent color scanning electron microscopy, near-infrared fluorescence, transmission electron microscopy, and alizarin red staining or Von Kossa staining; and (4) AI models for the screening of therapeutic drugs by analyzing big data of molecular mechanisms from cellular and animal disease models and big data from clinical investigations, and for the design of drugs by computer simulations. The key issues that can only be resolved deeply by AI in vascular calcification are the relationships among the contents of calcium and phosphorus, information of images, and expression levels of calcification-related genes. Therefore, these AI models, either two dimensions or three dimensions, will greatly accelerate the research and application of vascular calcification. AI models for vascular calcification Four types of AI models are needed. Integration of experimental disease models with clinical data benefits disclosing of molecular mechanisms and biomarkers of diseases. Integration of imaging of vascular calcification with multi-omics data benefits discovering ideal drug targets. Integration of advanced imaging techniques and disease progresses benefits the diagnosis of diseases. Integration of software tools with big data benefits drug design and production. AI, artificial intelligence; DS, disease stage; CT, computed tomography; DDC-SEM, density-dependent color scanning electron microscopy; NIRF, near-infrared fluorescence; TEM, transmission electron microscopy; CS, chemical staining, that is, calizarin red staining or Von Kossa staining. The image on the right of the middle panel is staining of calcification in human vascular smooth muscle cells by alizarin red. AI models for vascular calcification Four types of AI models are needed. Integration of experimental disease models with clinical data benefits disclosing of molecular mechanisms and biomarkers of diseases. Integration of imaging of vascular calcification with multi-omics data benefits discovering ideal drug targets. Integration of advanced imaging techniques and disease progresses benefits the diagnosis of diseases. Integration of software tools with big data benefits drug design and production. AI, artificial intelligence; DS, disease stage; CT, computed tomography; DDC-SEM, density-dependent color scanning electron microscopy; NIRF, near-infrared fluorescence; TEM, transmission electron microscopy; CS, chemical staining, that is, calizarin red staining or Von Kossa staining. The image on the right of the middle panel is staining of calcification in human vascular smooth muscle cells by alizarin red. Collectively, the ideas of Schwalbe and Wahl [10] and Mehta et al. [11] are very inspiring for us to study vascular calcification. Given the increasing prevalence and high morbidity and mortality of vascular calcification, the lack of specific drugs to treat vascular calcification, the complex events that trigger vascular calcification, the low case reports on vascular calcification, the technical limitations in calcification imaging, and the delayed applications of AI in the field of vascular calcification, accelerating the development and application of AI technologies in the field of vascular calcification is urgently needed. The last but not least, the AI models we discussed here will provide a required framework for future investigations in vascular calcification. This work was supported by the grants from Key Discipline Construction Project of Pudong Health and Family Planning Commission of Shanghai (No. PWZxk2017-07), Key Specialty Construction Project of Pudong Health and Family Planning Commission of Shanghai (No. PWZzk2017-29), Outstanding Leaders Training Program of Pudong Health and Family Planning Commission of Shanghai (No. PWRl2018-02), and Pudong New Area Science and Technology Commission (Nos. PKJ2019-Y21 and PKJ2018-Y08).",W1549919702; W2105074740; W2130227007; W2150762378; W2611954277; W2803760365; W2904507351; W2976743137; W2992586577; W2996280595; W3008472299; W3023997891,0,2020,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
