38881226,ChatGPT – Integrity: Artificial Intelligence Misuse in Medical Writing,10.1111/bju.16438,,,,,,Tayla Fay; David Homewood; Darcy Fay; Owen Niall,W4399753225,"The arrival of arti ﬁ cial intelligence (AI) in medical writing challenges traditional beliefs around scienti ﬁ c responsibility, integrity, and authorship responsibility. Evidence-based medicine, the cornerstone of modern healthcare, is de ﬁ ned by making healthcare decisions through critical appraisal of peer-reviewed published scienti ﬁ c literature. One increasingly used novel AI language tool is Chat Generative Pre-trained Transformer (ChatGPT). ChatGPT was initiated on 30 November 2022 by the OpenAI research laboratory. The large language model (LLM) employs the deep learning technique to generate text in a humanistic way. The program produces unique responses to each prompt and claims not plagiarise text. Whilst ChatGPT draws its responses from large databases, it is still susceptible to producing biased answers. Amidst the growing excitement over ChatGPT ’ s potential in medical writing, we raise two central questions regarding its place in healthcare literature:",W4295472519; W4318263917; W4318671267; W4321446367; W4322154537; W4323050332,2,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
