42091391,"Comment on ""Artificial intelligence algorithms effectively classify 38 movements in infants born full-term and preterm recorded in the laboratory and at home"".",10.1016/j.jfma.2026.04.079,,,,,,Poornima Ramaraju; K. Nirmala; Vinjamuri Venkata Kamesh,W7160401955,,W4415685397,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
41915771,Authors’ Reply: Why Medical Education Without Artificial Intelligence Still Matters: A Neuroscience-Informed Perspective,10.2196/95205,,,,,,Juan S Izquierdo-Condoy; Marlon Arias-Intriago; L. Corrales; Esteban Ortiz-Prado,W7147303355,"Matters: A Neuroscience-Informed Perspective"" [1].We believe this letter makes a valuable contribution to the debate on artificial intelligence (AI) in medical education by emphasizing the cognitive consequences of training clinicians in environments where these tools are available yet not always reliable or accessible in everyday practice.From our perspective, the author's argument expands on a concern we had already raised previously: if AI, particularly generative AI, can compromise critical thinking and cognitive autonomy, this erosion may also translate into a reduced capacity to sustain clinical reasoning when the tool fails or is unavailable [2].In our article, ""Artificial Intelligence in Medical Education: Transformative Potential, Current Applications, and Future Implications"" [3], we identified uncritical dependence on algorithmic outputs as one of the main limitations of incorporating AI into medical training.",W4400858405; W4404862231; W4414436578; W4417258000; W7129759239; W7135369829,0,2026,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
40980805,Editorial: Advances in artificial intelligence-enhanced electrocardiography: a pathway towards improved diagnosis and patient care,10.3389/fphys.2025.1687592,,,,,,Vijay Singh Chauhan; Rémi Dubois; Domenico L. Gatti; Jichao Zhao,W4414027213,"proposing a camera-based approach to ECG electrode localization. Using 2D and 3D computer vision algorithms, the authors demonstrate sub-centimeter accuracy in reconstructing electrode positions on the human torso. The study's reliance on off-the-shelf cameras and open-source algorithms makes it a scalable solution for BSPM expansion. Kim et al. [7] focus on scanned or imaged ECG paper strips, which are still widely used in many healthcare settings. Their two-stage deep learning system combines a Faster R-CNN for detecting ST-segment elevation with an ensemble model for infarction territory classification. This paper underscores a growing priority in AI research: meeting clinicians where they are. By enabling analysis of ECG images (rather than raw digital signals), the model is inherently compatible with a wide array of existing workflows, including those in resource-constrained environments, and is particularly attractive for frontline decision support. Neural network performance depends critically on the quality and relevance of input data. Ramirez et al. [8] explore this issue by applying a mutual information analysis to ECG leads, identifying redundant information across the 12 standard leads and testing various reduced-lead configurations. Their results reveal that a well-selected 6-lead subset, and vectorcardiographic transformations, can match or exceed full 12-lead performance, and preserve classification accuracy while reducing computational load. This has significant implications for wearable devices and mobile health, where data acquisition may be limited. By optimizing for both informativeness and parsimony, this work advances the efficiency and scalability of AI-driven ECG diagnostics. QRS detection is a fundamental building block in any ECG analysis pipeline. Zhao et al. [9] contribute a compact, yet highly accurate DNN model based on feature pyramid networks and dual-channel input. The model's minimal size (∼27k parameters) and fast inference make it ideal for edge computing applications, such as smartwatches, fitness trackers, or implantable devices. Zhao et al.'s work reinforces the emerging consensus that the future of AI-enhanced ECG lies in small, explainable, and highly optimized models tailored to specific tasks within broader clinical systems. Extending the reach of AI-enhanced ECG into maternal-fetal medicine, Wahbah et al. [10] present a bi-directional LSTM-based framework for extracting fetal ECG (fECG) signals from abdominal recordings. Their model achieves high accuracy and demonstrates resilience even during stages where the fetal signal is physiologically obscured. As fetal and neonatal ECGs pose unique signal processing challenges, this study opens new avenues for AI-assisted perinatal care, remote monitoring, and early detection of congenital abnormalities. Looking Forward: A Field Poised for Impact The contributions to this Research Topic highlight a discipline on the cusp of transformation. From novel signal processing and intelligent hardware to regulatory-aware, interpretable algorithms, the field of AI-enhanced ECG analysis is advancing rapidly toward real-world impact. However, critical challenges remain. Generalizability across diverse populations, integration with electronic health records (EHRs), and validation in prospective trials are essential next steps. Ethical considerations, especially around algorithmic bias, data privacy, and clinical accountability, must be integrated into development from the outset. We are beginning to see new frontiers: multimodal integration (combining ECG with imaging, labs, or genomics), personalized risk prediction, and AI-guided therapeutic interventions. As Editors of this Research Topic, we are inspired by the diversity, creativity, and clinical awareness shown by the authors in this issue. The articles not only advance the science of AI in ECG analysis but also illuminate the path to meaningful clinical translation. Together, their work illustrates a maturing ecosystem of tools, methods, and philosophies ready to shape the next era of cardiovascular care.",W4405184595; W4407868488,0,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
40678844,Commentary on “Portable Ultrasound Bladder Volume Measurement Over Entire Volume Range Using a Deep Learning Artificial Intelligence Model in a Selected Cohort: A Proof of Principle Study”,10.1002/nau.70102,,,,,,Saraswati Sah; Renu Sah,W4412491373,The authors have nothing to report.,W4405528228; W4406357506; W4410480213,0,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39839420,"Editorial: Artificial intelligence, machine learning, and data-mining techniques to increase cost-effectiveness in healthcare",10.3389/fpubh.2024.1525628,,,,,,P. Wilner Jeanty; Marie‐Rachelle Narcisse; Romain Crastes dit Sourd,W4406131060,"The first paper by Zhengda Pei, Ningping Xiao, and Pei Yang, entitled ""Cost-Effectiveness Analysis of Tumor Treating Fields for Lung Cancer Treatment in China,"" uses a Markov simulation model with a theoretical population of 276 patients metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (mNSCLC) over a 15-year time horizon to evaluate the cost-effectiveness of Tumor Treating Fields (TTFields) combined with standard-of-care for treating this cancer. The economic evaluation, including total costs, life-years, quality-adjusted life years (QALYs), and incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER) values, reveals that the high costs of TTFields do not justify the modest survival benefits under current pricing models. This study aligns well with our research topic by applying a cost-effectiveness model providing insights into the economics of healthcare treatments. Future studies could combine simulation methods with AI techniques to optimize treatment decision models and dynamically update cost-effectiveness analyses as treatment parameters or patient demographics change. Additionally, research could use patient characteristics with mNSCLC to personalize these analyses using ML, AI, or DL analytics.The next article by Wenjie Liu, Gengwei Huo, and Peng Chen evaluates the cost-effectiveness of brigatinib and lorlatinib for treating ALK-positive non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), comparing the costs and outcomes of using brigatinib followed by lorlatinib before chemotherapy versus after. The study uses past trial data to recompose individual patient-level data and estimate survival models, with cost figures derived from publicly available data and agent administration expenses. They authors employ Markov models to simulate disease progression, deriving transition probabilities between states, each with associated costs and quality-adjusted life years (QALYs). Their findings show that using brigatinib as a first-line treatment followed by lorlatinib is more cost-effective than using brigatinib as a second-line option. This approach improves QALYs and health outcomes while optimizing healthcare costs. The study supports a treatment strategy prioritizing first-line brigatinib to enhance the costeffectiveness of NSCLC care.In the third paper titled ""Demand prediction of medical services in home and community-based services for older adults in China using machine learning"", Yucheng Huang and colleagues use cross-sectional data from the 2018 Longitudinal Healthy Longevity Survey of 15,312 Chinese adults aged 65+ to predict demand for medical services in home and community-based services (HCBS) based on Anderson's behavioral model of health services utilization. Results from five predictive machine-learning methods (logistic regression, logistic regression with LASSO regularization, Support Vector Machine, Random Forest, and Extreme Gradient Boosting) unveiled that enabling factors, need factors, and behavioral factors were significant predictors of HCBS use. The authors argue that a robust conceptual model combined with rigorous machine learning methods could help better allocate limited medical resources to this segment of China's population with increasing demand for healthcare services. Future studies could employ machine learning or deep-learning methods based on longitudinal data to make causal inferences and integrate other dimensions of Andersen's Model of Health Services Use, such as social support, an enabling factor, to predict healthcare demand.Entitled ""Hospital efficiency in the Eastern Mediterranean region: A systematic review and metaanalysis"", the fourth article by Hamid Ravaghi and colleagues analyzes hospital efficiency in the Eastern Mediterranean, focusing on management's impact on healthcare quality and costs. Using a systematic review and meta-analysis of 37 studies, the authors evaluate factors like resource allocation, technology use, and hospital size through data envelopment analysis. They find significant heterogeneity across countries. Oman, for instance, has the highest mean technical efficiency among high-income nations and Bahrain the lowest. Among low-and middle-income nations, Iraq and Iran rank the highest, with Pakistan the lowest. Key factors affecting efficiency include internal structure, regional differences, and decision-maker participation in sustainability assessments. The authors recommend developing outpatient care, reducing supplier-induced demand, and strengthening hospital management and governance to improve efficiency and resilience against crises like COVID-19.In the fifth article entitled ""The direct and indirect effects of length of hospital stay on the costs of inpatients with stroke in Ningxia, China,"" Ming Su and colleagues analyze data from 129,444 ischemic stroke patients and 15,525 hemorrhagic stroke patients. Using quantile regression and structural equation models, they assess how length of hospital stay (LOHS) impacts hospitalization costs, considering indirect social factors. The study compares ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke patients, examining variables like age, hospital level, admission and discharge details, payment method, LOHS, and surgery. Findings show LOHS as the largest contributor to inpatient costs, with other factors such as the Charlson Comorbidity Index, payment type, surgery, hospital level, and discharge method also having significant direct and indirect effects.Finally, the systematic review of 144 papers by Ali Imani, Roghayeh Alibabayee, Mina Golestani and Koustuv Dalal identifies key indicators affecting hospital efficiency, categorized into input, process, and output indicators. Input indicators include hospital capacity, structure, and costs; process indicators cover quality-oriented processes and educational activities; and output indicators focus on activity-related and quality-related outcomes. The study emphasizes the importance of both quantitative and qualitative measures for a comprehensive analysis of hospital efficiency, providing a structured framework for healthcare providers and administrators to enhance patient care and optimize cost. Future research can build on this framework to develop predictive models for real-time decision-making and cost management, especially in response to dynamic hospital demands and potential pandemics.The articles collectively highlight innovative applications of data science including machine and statistical learning techniques aiming at enhancing patient care and optimizing resource allocation in clinical and non-clinical settings. They provide actionable insights for healthcare providers and hospital administrators. They revolve around cost-effectiveness and efficiency and recommendations for improvements.",,1,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39764520,Precision animal husbandry: using artificial intelligence for camera traps to optimize animal production and management decision support systems,10.1093/af/vfae026,,,,,,Amira Burns; Hailey Wilmer; Ryan S. Miller; Patrick E. Clark; Joshua Bret Taylor,W4406077916,"• Artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to save producers and researchers money and time in deploying camera trap arrays for animal monitoring. • Accessible tools for animal detection and classification, individual animal id, and behavior, movement estimation are available to a wide range of practitioners. • AI tools can support adaptive management strategies. • AI tools are currently adaptive to user input, allowing engaged users to influence development. The future implications of these technologies in assisting adaptive and precision management in complex agroecosystems will depend on continued collaboration among managers and scientists. Agroecosystems are often managed as multifunctional landscapes for food production, conservation, recreation, and social goals. Resource managers and livestock producers make crucial decisions dependent on spatiotemporal information of the presence or abundance of certain species, including predators, invasive species, or potential disease vectors, but this information is frequently lacking for complex ecosystems. Camera traps are a low-cost, non-invasive, and widespread approach to collecting animal monitoring data. The sheer volume of images generated by a camera trap array presents a resource challenge for converting these raw data into quantifiable, accessible decision aids, e.g., species distribution maps, or early warning systems to detect disease or invasive species. Artificial intelligence (AI) technologies can improve the capacity of scientists, managers, and ranchers to assess, monitor, and adapt management practices to these dynamic conditions and disparate objectives, increasing productivity and profitability while minimizing resource inputs. This paper explores the intersection of camera trap monitoring and AI-based image processing, known as computer vision, as an example application of AI to adaptive agroecological management. The chosen tools are accessible to a wide range of practitioners without necessitating computational expertise and have potential to expand to wider use. We propose a logic feedback model (Figure 1) that incorporates AI tools for camera traps to quantify and assist with decision making, using a scientific framework to inform contextual management strategies. Logic feedback model where computer vision tools for camera trap images form the core of adaptive management, research, and planning strategies. A good experimental design is essential to camera arrays regardless of size or project context, from meeting production objectives to answering research questions. A systematic approach engenders more comprehensive information-gathering to address motivating questions or information needs and allows one to answer additional questions that were not conceived at the initial planning stage. Design parameters vary depending on species of interest and planning goals; nascent computational tools such as R packages camtrapmonitoring (https://github.com/robitalec/camtrapmonitoring) and cameratrapgridR (https://github.com/CameraTrapDetectoR/cameratrapgridR/) can help create methodological camera trap deployment arrays according to user-specified criteria. While AI is not directly applied during planning, any insights gleaned from using AI on a camera array would inform future planning stages as part of iterative adaptive management or research strategies. Computer vision models are currently being deployed on-device by custom providers or in a cloud environment to reduce false triggers, classify animals by species, send notifications in real time, and even identify individual animals. The Conservation X Labs Sentinel camera (https://sentinel.conservationxlabs.com/) uses customized object detection models and adapted hardware to provide real-time notification capabilities in a variety of settings, including livestock monitoring, with full technological support. The NanoDetector (https://nanodetector.ai), developed by the University of Idaho with the Wildlife and Humans in Shared Landscapes Project (https://whisl.org) also deploys customized AI models on-device to detect and classify animals and notify users in real time using open-source software and standard commercial hardware. Recreational camera manufacturers including Bushnell, Moultrie, and Spypoint advertise species recognition for common hunting targets; we are unable to verify the methodology or accuracy of these offerings, but they indicate a growing interest in computer vision for camera trap images. These examples indicate a future widespread availability of real-time or on-device AI camera trap technologies. Due to the size and volume of image files generated and potential incompatibilities with remote processing and transfer, current practice usually necessitates processing camera trap data upon retrieval; many computer vision tools are specifically designed for this application. Select examples stand out for their accessibility, privacy, or application to animal production. Our tool CameraTrapDetectoR (Tabak et al., 2022; https://github.com/CameraTrapDetectoR) deploys object detection deep learning models at the taxonomic species, family, and class levels on multiple platforms to serve users with varying levels of technical experience. Models are trained on an agriculture-focused dataset by a USDA development team, with a mission of retaining data privacy while providing free, generalized, one-stop detection, and classification AI models. Pytorch Wildlife (https://github.com/microsoft/CameraTraps) is supported by millions of training images and is highly accurate at animal detection but necessitates subsequent computer vision tools to automate classification. Wildlife Insights (https://www.wildlifeinsights.org) has a comfortable user interface and species-level classification but requires users to share their data to a cloud source and charges a fee above an image threshold or team size. The automation of image processing via computer vision acts as a gateway to establishing larger camera trap arrays with more cameras that can be deployed over longer time periods. The ability to process more data across larger areas at faster speeds will lead to more comprehensive data and better-informed analyses and decisions. Computer vision tools are also innovating analysis of classified camera trap images. Two types of AI models have broad implications for both managers and researchers: individual animal id and pose estimation. AI-assisted estimates of density, group behavior, and presence/absence can already be used to track management outcomes and inform adaptive management in animal systems. We anticipate future development of more affordable, accessible, and accurate AI tools for individual animal identification to estimate population size and monitor ecological events such as wildlife-livestock interactions, foraging behavior, and the effect of environmental factors. Managers could more closely monitor individuals’ behavior, and researchers will be able to use these tools to replace mark-recapture methods and other expensive, intensive interventions to estimate populations and monitor the spread of invasive species. The concept has been proven in an open-source format by SealNet (https://github.com/zbirenbaum/SealFaceRecognition), a facial recognition software for harbor seals that can be run on an AWS-supported Graphical User Interface (GUI) and by BearID (https://github.com/bearid/bearid-dlib), an open-source workflow to recognize individual bears that can be reproduced on other datasets. Both projects operate on closed datasets where all individuals are known and previously identified; however, their success indicates progress towards open systems with updated methodologies where previously unknown individuals can be identified and labelled. AI-supported pose estimation and movement tracking tools are essential for quantifying animal behavior, e.g. early disease detection or quantifying desirable behaviors, and can be used to inform management decisions. The DeepLabCut (Mathis et al., 2018), LabGym (Hu et al., 2023), and SLEAP (Pereira et al., 2022) tools guide users to label a small number of training images, then fine-tune a custom deep-learning model to quantify and track behaviors across an entire dataset. Additional tools to track animal behavior include DeepEthogram (https://github.com/jbohnslav/deepethogram) and SimBA (https://github.com/sgoldenlab/simba). Species interactions are of particular interest in camera trap arrays, and SLEAP offers the unique capability to detect and track multiple species’ behaviors in the same frame. These tools can measure animal physiology or traits such as the standardization of Body Composition Score, quantify behaviors and features that optimize reproductive output, or detect and quantify interaction between individuals. Results from these tools can also feed back into image processing models to optimize their training data and improve performance; for example, the CameraTrapDetectoR project is using SLEAP and DeepLabCut to improve training data by annotating animal orientation, thus creating a more effective training sample with fewer images that is stratified across different poses and behaviors. AI-based tools are essential to expanding the information reach and processing capacity of large data for systems-based, trans-disciplinary learning that provide better decision frameworks for all stakeholders on a multi-use landscape. For example, a collaborative stakeholder group can combine resources to deploy a single robust, comprehensive camera array on a landscape that would enable all contributors to answer different questions, build trust and learning, improve efficiency and service-delivery outcomes, and close the gap among organizations with different missions using the same landscape. A collaborative approach would further reinforce ethical and responsible use of AI-based tools. The fast-paced, flexible nature of computer vision provides a current opportunity to become an engaged user in these open-source tools and directly influence development through participant feedback. These tools can support a system of precision animal production that bolsters existing collaborative, adaptive management practices and enhances our ability to respond to dynamic biological and social conditions. Amira Burns is a fellow with the USDA Agricultural Research Service (ARS), Range Sheep Production Efficiency Research Unit at the US Sheep Experiment Station, and the ARS SCINet Program and AI Center of Excellence. She holds a Masters in Applied Statistics from Colorado State University. She is the developer and maintainer of the CameraTrapDetectoR project. Her research focuses on developing user-friendly tools to advance animal, agricultural, environmental, and health science. Corresponding author:[email protected] Hailey Wilmer is a Research Rangeland Management Specialist at the USDA-ARS Range Sheep Production Efficiency Research Unit in Dubois, Idaho, USA. She completed MS and PhD in rangeland ecosystem science at Colorado State University. Prior to joining the US Sheep Experiment Station staff in 2021, Hailey worked at the USDA-ARS in Fort Collins and as a social scientist with the US Forest Service in Juneau. Her current research questions include: what are the long-term effects of grazing, climate, and other disturbances on high elevation rangeland plant communities? How can researchers and managers work together to conduct useful and useable science? How do ranchers and managers make sense of the complex systems they manage? Can mainstream science integrate local knowledge to support ranching livelihoods, biodiversity, and food production? Ryan S. Miller is a senior ecologist with United States Department of Agriculture Center for Epidemiology and Animal Health. He received a Doctor of Philosophy in Zoology from Colorado State University working with Dr. Colleen Webb and the Webb Lab, a Master of Science in Forestry and a Bachelor of Science in Forest Management. His research spans landscape and population ecology, with special interests in disease ecology and conservation biology, mechanisms that drive contact and transmission of pathogens at the wildlife-livestock interface, and how policy can be established to mitigate transmission. J. Bret Taylor is the research leader of the USDA ARS, Range Sheep Production Efficiency Research Unit at the US Sheep Experiment Station near Dubois, Idaho. He holds PhD and MS degrees (New Mexico State University) in animal science with emphases in ruminant nutrition, molecular biology, and toxicology and BS degrees (West Texas A&M University) in chemistry and animal science. His research focuses on developing ecologically-oriented sheep genetic resources applicable toward grazing utility of sheep originating from extensive rangeland systems and discovery and application of unique antibiotic-replacement products. Patrick E. Clark is a Rangeland Scientist with the USDA Agricultural Research Service Northwest Watershed Research Center in Boise, Idaho. His research interests span disturbance ecology, range animal behavior, pastoralism, and remote sensing. This manuscript was invited for submission by the American Society of Animal Science (ASAS). The views expressed in this publication are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the views or policies of the ASAS, the journal, or the publisher. A.B.: Conceptualization; Writing—original draft; Writing—review and editing. H.W.: Conceptualization; Visualization; Writing—review and editing. R.S.M.: Conceptualization; Writing—review and editing. B.J.T.: Conceptualization; Writing—review and editing. P.E.C.: Conceptualization; Writing—review and editing Conflict of interest statement. None declared.",W2887114371; W4210926750; W4223962547; W4321782581,3,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39739849,Dermatology diagnostic accuracy is improved by artificial intelligence‐generated differential diagnoses,10.1111/ijd.17591,,,,,,Paul C. Macklis; Benjamin Harris Kaffenberger; Rachel M. Kirven; Michela M. Paradiso; Kathryn T. Shahwan; Jennifer A. Sopkovich; Kelly Tyler; Vamsi Varra; Abraham M. Korman,W4405919547,"Artificial intelligence (AI) is a rapidly growing field with numerous potential applications in healthcare. Recent research has explored some of these applications within dermatology, including identifying melanoma,1 interpreting dermoscopic images,1 and even dermatopathology.2 However, few have examined the possibility of using AI chat interfaces to develop differential diagnoses for complex medical dermatology cases. In this study, we sought to determine if AI could be a useful clinical tool for diagnosing complex dermatology cases commonly encountered at inpatient consults. Fifteen cases were extracted from JAAD Case Reports and JAMA Dermatology, featuring common, complex cases seen during inpatient dermatology consults.3, 4 Each case was edited to remove any explicit reference to the correct diagnosis and compiled into a survey that was distributed to nine Ohio State University (OSU) dermatologists, seven of which (77.8%) ultimately completed the survey (three residents, four attendings). Participants were presented with edited text and associated images for each case and were asked to list their top three diagnoses. After submitting their initial differential diagnoses, the dermatologists were presented with an AI-generated differential diagnosis created with ChatGPT (Generative Pre-trained Transformer, version GPT-3.5), an AI model with a chat interface produced by OpenAI.5 The AI-generated differentials were created by presenting ChatGPT with the edited case text and the prompt “Based on the following case information, create a differential diagnosis featuring three possible diagnoses”. These prompts were provided to ChatGPT in January 2024. After viewing the AI differential diagnosis, dermatologists were asked to submit a second differential diagnosis for each case. Of the 15 total cases included in the survey, 10 were cases for which ChatGPT listed the correct diagnosis within its differential (“AI Correct”), while five were cases in which ChatGPT failed to list the correct diagnosis within its differential (“AI Incorrect”). Each participant's differential diagnosis was graded as correct or incorrect based on whether they included the correct diagnosis in their differential. The average score of participants before and after seeing AI differentials was 68.6% and 79.0% for all cases, 65.7% and 90.0% for “AI Correct” cases, and 71.4% and 60.0% for “AI Incorrect” cases (Table 1). In “AI Correct” cases, seeing the differential diagnosis provided by ChatGPT resulted in a 33.3% increase in correct answers for residents but only a 17.5% increase for attendings. However, in “AI Incorrect” cases, seeing the differential diagnosis provided by ChatGPT decreased the percentage of correct answers by 13.3% for residents and 15% for attendings (Figure 1). The effect size (Cohen's d) between the mean number of “correct” responses before and after viewing AI differentials was also calculated (Figure 1). Overall, our results suggest that AI-generated differential diagnoses can be helpful for dermatologists as they navigate complex cases but should be used with caution. While this study examined their effects on a clinician's top three differential diagnoses, the effect of AI differentials on a clinician's top diagnosis (and, therefore, clinical management) remains unclear. Given that therapeutics for one diagnosis may cause serious harm if used for a different entity on the same differential, it is important to recognize the limitations of this preliminary study and use AI differentials as a tool to expand a workup rather than dictate management. Additionally, while the net effect of AI differentials was beneficial on participant differentials, “AI Incorrect” cases were occasionally detrimental. Interestingly, having access to “AI Correct” differentials was more beneficial for residents than attendings, but both groups were equally led astray by “AI Incorrect” cases. We suggest that dermatologists critically assess AI-generated diagnostic information, understand its limitations, and use it as a tool to broaden differentials in conjunction with clinical expertise to guide accurate diagnosis rather than solely relying on this imperfect tool. Limitations to this study include sampling bias of participants who all practice at a large academic center as well as the small sample size of both participants and cases. Future studies could explore the impact of AI differentials on a larger group of participating dermatologists, cases of varying medical complexity, dermatology cases being evaluated by other medical specialties, or differences based on years of practice. During the preparation of this work, the author(s) used ChatGPT (GPT-3.5) in order to generate differential diagnoses that would be provided to survey participants. AI was not used to draft the text of the manuscript. Following the use of this tool/service, the author(s) reviewed and edited the content thoroughly and hereby declare(s) full responsibility for the authenticity of the contents of this publication.",W164320554; W2145519646; W2967473922; W3013387448,1,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38449865,Corrigendum: Experimental validation of immunogenic SARS-CoV-2 T cell epitopes identified by artificial intelligence,10.3389/fimmu.2024.1377041,,,,,,Lorenzo Federico; Brandon Malone; Simen Tennøe; Viktoriia Chaban; Julie Røkke Osen; Murat Gainullin; Eva Smorodina; Hassen Kared; Rahmad Akbar; Victor Greiff; Richard Stratford; Trevor Clancy; Ludvig  André Munthe,W4392012930,[This corrects the article DOI: 10.3389/fimmu.2023.1265044.].,,0,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37946638,KI‐basierte Bestimmung des PRO‐Scores in aktinischen Keratosen anhand von LC‐OCT‐Bilddatensätzen,10.1111/ddg.15194_g,,,,,,Janis Raphael Thamm; Fabia Daxenberger; Théo Viel; Charlotte Gust; Quirine Louise Eijkenboom; Lars Einar French; Julia Welzel; Elke Sattler; Sandra Jungblut Schuh,W4388573291,"Zusammenfassung Hintergrund Mit Hilfe des histologischen PRO‐Scores (I–III) kann das Risiko einer malignen Transformation aktinischer Keratosen (AK) abgeschätzt werden, indem er das Ausmaß der Undulation und basalen Proliferation der dermoepidermalen Junktionszone (DEJ) bewertet. Die konfokale Line‐Field optische Kohärenztomographie (LC‐OCT) ermöglicht eine nichtinvasive Bestimmung des PRO‐Scores in Echtzeit. Anhand von LC‐OCT‐Bilddatensätzen kann eine künstliche Intelligenz (KI) mit Hilfe von Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) zur automatischen Quantifizierung des PRO‐Scores von AK in vivo trainiert werden. Patienten und Methodik Convolutional Neural Networks wurden trainiert, um LC‐OCT‐Bilder von gesunder Haut und von AK zu segmentieren. PRO‐Score‐Modelle wurden in Übereinstimmung mit dem histopathologischen Goldstandard entwickelt und an einer Teilmenge von 237 LC‐OCT‐AK‐Bildern trainiert und an 76 Bildern getestet, wobei der von der KI‐berechnete PRO‐Score mit dem visuellen Konsens der Bildgebungsexperten verglichen wurde. Ergebnisse Eine signifikante Übereinstimmung wurde in 57/76 (75%) Fällen festgestellt. Die KI‐basierte Bewertung des PRO‐Scores korrelierte am besten mit dem visuellen Score für PRO II (84,8%) vs. PRO III (69,2%) vs. PRO I (66,6%). In 25% der Fälle kam es zu Fehlinterpretationen, die meist auf eine Verschattung der DEJ sowie störende Merkmale wie Haarfollikel zurückzuführen waren. Schlussfolgerungen Die Ergebnisse deuten darauf hin, dass CNN für die automatische Quantifizierung des PRO‐Scores in LC‐OCT‐Bilddatensätzen hilfreich sind. Dies könnte zur nichtinvasiven Bewertung des Proliferationsrisikos in der Diagnostik und Nachsorge von AK herangezogen werden.",W1985187105; W1988910572; W2053558823; W2064636851; W2108598243; W2293788037; W2549139847; W2557738935; W2581082771; W2743151536; W2772723798; W2789591216; W2809254203; W2885575017; W2893096276; W2924950805; W2963420686; W3003257820; W3009475271; W3166426377; W3195670432; W4224988997; W6676297131; W6743731764; W7073824294,0,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37498169,Artificial intelligence selection of viable oocytes from phase contrast images for in vitro maturation and fertilization,10.1093/biolre/ioad084,,,,,,Manoel Veiga; Beatrice Camia; Stefan Diepenbrock; CarloAlberto Redi; Andrea Casasco; Manuela Monti,W4385295042,"Legend: We established an artificial intelligence model to classify immature germinal vesicle oocytes as surrounded nucleolus or not surrounded nucleolus by looking only at a phase contrast image, with an accuracy close to 90%. This methodology lays the foundations for translational research in the field of human-assisted reproduction.",W2050922459; W2052014994; W2077280819; W2112731182; W2315881813; W2588763953; W3182380636; W3195124885; W4210343384; W4281734082; W4312113520; W4365445779; W6733573005; W6807546010,5,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
