37632402,Ethical principles of artificial intelligence in public health,10.1093/pubmed/fdad165,,,,,,Jeff Clyde G. Corpuz,W4386191570,"To the Editor, It is undeniable that the advancements in Artificial Intelligence (AI) have opened transformative opportunities in public health and have become an important force for their future development.1 This paradigm shift, undoubtedly a testament to human ingenuity, has empowered people to improve human lives in ways unimaginable. AI is rapidly changing the global field of science and technology. During the COVID-19 pandemic, AI-based medical devises and algorithms have shown great potential in surveillance, prevention, diagnosis and health management.1 In a previously published correspondence, the authors claimed the importance of addressing ethical considerations of AI in the context of public health.1,2 This correspondence adds to the discussion the urgent need to integrate the ethical principles on the use of AI in public health. The Pan American Health Organization (PAHO) and World Health Organization (WHO) state that Artificial Intelligence for Public Health (AI4PH) must be guided by ‘technical and ethical considerations’ to mitigate ethical risk in public health.3 There are eight guiding principles in AI4PH: (1) people-centered; (2) ethically grounded; (3) transparent; (4) data protected; (5) demonstrates scientific integrity; (6) open and sharable; (7) nondiscriminatory and (8) human-controlled technology.2 Additionally, the United Nations (UN) also presented eight areas of collaboration based on recommendations from a high-level panel to make technical cooperation operational in the age of digital interdependence. One of the key ethical principles is about AI. AI and any emerging technology should be part of public health policies.3 If used ethically, AI has the potential to eliminate human error, make advances to promote science in public health more accessible and advance global process toward the UN Sustainable Development Goals (UNSDGs).4,5",W4360942031; W4380422867; W4384155827; W4384925250; W6851429967; W6852986871; W6854203462; W6854753225,5,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37607158,How Brain Organoids Are Revolutionizing Neuroscience,10.1109/MPULS.2023.3294102,,,,,,Zara Abrams,W4386077046,"Measuring just a millimeter or two in diameter, brain organoids are a far cry from a fully functioning human brain. But these miniature tissues, typically derived from stem cells, are increasingly able to mimic the structure and function of our most complex organ, unlocking exciting possibilities for neuroscience, artificial intelligence, and beyond.",W2948836568; W3128443681; W3193479663; W4225278364; W4304695089; W4306411749; W4318985490,4,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
