34814182,Predicting ROP Severity by Artificial Intelligence: Pragmatic Versus Knowledge-Based Approach,10.1542/peds.2021-053255,,,,,,Alistair R. Fielder; Graham E. Quinn,W3216021833,"Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) blindness is largely preventable, with many countries having already established programs for its timely identification. However, ∼93% of infants screened do not develop ROP severe enough to require treatment.1 As reported in this issue of Pediatrics by Coyner et al.2 artificial intelligence (AI) is innovative methodology to optimize the timing of ROP examinations. For example, it can predict whether an eye will not require treatment, reducing the number of examinations needed while also predicting which eyes need closer surveillance. This is an important development because it would make ROP programs more effective and efficient, thus reducing the burden on infants, families, and the health care team.From the earliest descriptions, congestion and tortuosity of the posterior retinal vessels were recognized as prominent features of serious ROP.3 In the first international classification of ROP in 1984,4 the term “plus disease” was included to describe progressive vascular incompetence. Two decades later, after the recommendations of the Early Treatment for Retinopathy of Prematurity trial,5 plus disease became the major driver for treatment. With focus now directed to the timely identification of plus disease, the historical dichotomous categorization of normal versus plus disease was deemed inadequate and led to the introduction of an intermediate level, “preplus disease,” in 20056 and the subsequent critical acknowledgment in the 2021 classification7 that the vascular changes associated with ROP do not progress stepwise but are a continuous spectrum.However, categorizing the plus spectrum is subjective and subject to significant variation.8–10 In the late 1990s, the introduction of digital imaging into the NICU provided the opportunity to objectively measure what had long been seen, and this development spawned several methods of retinal vessel measurement.11,12 Despite revealing great early promise, these methods have yet to be incorporated into routine ROP screening worldwide.AI has recently been used to help interpret the complex images captured to monitor for ROP and other patient-specific risk factors. Coyner et al1 report the use of this technology to explore the spectrum of ROP-related vascular changes, and using deep learning (a type of machine learning that can be used to develop predictive rules), the Oregon team developed i-ROP (an algorithm to detect plus disease),13 from which a vascular severity score (VSS) was derived from zone I (the area closest to the optic disc).13,14 Coyner et al1 have devised a risk model to predict ROP requiring treatment using 2 components: gestational age and VSS. Based on images obtained at 32 to 33 weeks’ postmenstrual age, the model had 100% sensitivity and 80.8% negative predictive value to predict (on average, >1 month in advance of the diagnosis) an eye which would later require treatment. This is an exciting and important development. As a result, instead of a stepwise progression through normal, preplus, and plus disease, VSS permits a sliding scale and detailed analysis of the entire plus spectrum, which hopefully will permit fine tuning of the model in the future.To paraphrase Lord Kelvin (1883), when you can measure what you are speaking about, and express it in numbers, you know something about it; but when you cannot measure it, your knowledge is of a meager and unsatisfactory kind. Herein lies a dilemma: Terms such as AI and deep learning are now in common parlance, so much so that few of us are brave enough to express ignorance. By expressing it (VSS) in numbers, it is not clear that, currently, we fully understand what is being measured or if it has advanced our scientific understanding. Hopefully, this will become apparent in the future. Pragmatically, however, there is no doubt that AI-related techniques, including deep learning–derived VSS, are effective and will likely be important in ROP screening. For this to be incorporated into routine clinical practice, some challenges need to be overcome: a short processing time will be essential preferably within the time span of the NICU ward rounds. In addition, this research is North America based and needs to be validated in other countries in which neonatal care may well be more variable and larger infants develop sight-threatening ROP over a shorter time period. Coyner et al1 have introduced us to a new and exciting era of efficient ROP identification.",W336466729; W1546501060; W2008561891; W2017052783; W2167431761; W2338036999; W2345511416; W2741822194; W2800012666; W2891969356; W3034494591; W3129246287; W3148465273; W3182791891; W3215796619; W4236388884; W4248231685,1,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34755989,Characterizing dermatology mobile applications that use artificial intelligence.,10.5070/D327955147,,,,,,Varun Ranpariya; Steven R Feldman,W3210925086,,,5,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34633139,Can artificial intelligence save medical genetics?,10.1002/ajmg.a.62538,,,,,,Benjamin D. Solomon,W3206925134,"Genetics and genomics is one of the most exciting and quickly evolving fields in medicine. Relatively recent genomic discoveries have spread to impact more patients and practice areas (Green et al., 2020). To support this expansion, some areas of clinical genetics—like genetic counseling, have grown robustly, both in terms of the numbers of practitioners and in the clinical arenas where these practitioners work (Abacan et al., 2019; Baty, 2018). Unfortunately, despite the growing need, there has not been a parallel growth of physician geneticists (Jenkins et al., 2021; Maiese et al., 2019; Penon-Portmann et al., 2020). The causes of the stagnation of the physician geneticist workforce are multifactorial. These factors may involve lack of exposure during training, relative compensation, insurance-related challenges that impact patient care and clinical practice, barriers to entry for internationally-trained physicians, and others (Jenkins et al., 2021; Maiese et al., 2019). I am a proud and extremely happy physician geneticist. While working in different roles in academia/government, biotech, and healthcare (Slavotinek & Solomon, 2020), I have never regretted my choice of specialty. Though I am aware of the difficulties in medical genetics and have certainly had my own share of challenges and failures, I am more enamored than ever with the field. (I do admit that I tend to see the world through decidedly rosy lenses.) Sadly, though many of us are working hard to draw more trainees and others to genetics, I am not confident that current trends with the physician geneticist workforce will change, at least in the near-term. In some ways, this is not a bad thing. Though not intentionally, we are forcing other clinicians to “learn to fish.” That is, more types of clinicians will have to become fluent in genetics as they will not have a choice, including because they will encounter genetic issues more and more frequently, and will not have in-theflesh physician geneticist colleagues to whom they can easily turn. Fortunately, the flourishing of the genetic counseling profession will help tremendously, and it is likely that, as a group, genetic counselors will be able to take on expanding roles to work with an ever-larger swathe of healthcare. However, all of this will take time. That is, genetics will not be seamlessly integrated into many areas of practice immediately and genetic counseling services may not be able to grow quickly enough to address all care gaps. Aside from this, there will remain a substantial burden of medically complex patients where physicians who specialize in the care of patients with individually rare (but common in aggregate) genetic syndromes remain necessary, both for diagnosis and management (Ferreira, 2019; Gonzaludo et al., 2019). Again, medical genetics has always generated excitement because of the application of new technological advances (Snir et al., 2021; Solomon, 2021). One such advance, which may profoundly alter the face of biomedicine, is the broad realm of artificial intelligence (AI) (Topol, 2019). AI is not new, but, for a variety of reasons, including better computers, larger, shared datasets, and proven algorithms, AI is garnering increasing attention. The many versions and iterations of AI have already started to be coupled with medical genetics, including in ways that offer glimpses into great potential (a few of many example citations are given) (Brasil et al., 2021; Brasil et al., 2019; Clark et al., 2019; Dias & Torkamani, 2019; Dowsett et al., 2019; Duong et al., 2021; Gurovich et al., 2019; Jumper et al., 2021; Kruszka et al., 2020; Kruszka et al., 2017; Kruszka et al., 2018; Mak et al., 2021; Porras et al., 2021; Schaefer et al., 2020; Shchelochkov et al., 2021; Tekendo-Ngongang et al., 2020). For example, the use of AI can help develop a differential diagnosis for a patient, can support the analysis of genomic data for that patient, and may help researchers studying possible treatments to select compounds of interest for further study. The word “save” in the title of this piece may be a bit intentionally provocative, but there are multiple reasons why medical genetics may be especially ripe for AI, and which point to ways that AI may bolster medical genetics in important and impactful ways. The integration of AI into medical genetics may occur, both because and in spite of the shortage of physician geneticists. First, there is a strong track record in medical genetics (perhaps here it would be more appropriate to use the term “medical genomics”) of large-scale, multinational, collaborative endeavors that involve different facets of data sharing, and which benefit enormous numbers of individuals. Efforts like ClinVar (https://www. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/clinvar/), Face2Gene (https://www.face2gene. com/) (which rests on AI approaches, and which has been adopted by many in the genetics community), GeneMatcher (https:// genematcher.org/), and gnomAD (https://gnomad.broadinstitute. org/), to name just a few, have been extremely impactful, and show how cooperation among diverse entities, including government, academia, healthcare, and private industry can sustainably improve outcomes and enrich the field. Second, clinicians can leverage AI tools for medical genetics purposes without themselves being experts. Given the individual rarity of many genetic conditions, even full-time medical geneticists will frequently encounter patients with conditions they have never seen before (Ferreira, 2019; Solomon et al., 2013). Aggressive behind-thescenes, work can be done to scrub and annotate complex and esoteric Received: 13 September 2021 Accepted: 25 September 2021",W2002655468; W2320762908; W2740863414; W2791041639; W2795749588; W2800894548; W2895534446; W2905096637; W2905441841; W2909286381; W2912168443; W2921313412; W2942272601; W2955653438; W2968029180; W2971632170; W2975298736; W2989824398; W2990290777; W2995374703; W3033581476; W3034025577; W3088170688; W3094198227; W3095580221; W3110688245; W3117361835; W3132957360; W3157084146; W3159457487; W3159842308; W3163890211; W3177828909; W3187467055; W3194184045; W3196480531; W3196566835; W6651108094; W6741763056; W6751016873; W6755370354; W6758203264; W6760531970; W6771327245; W6778937220; W6788147211; W6799395112; W6811624559,11,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34498686,"Letter: Machine Learning and Artificial Intelligence in Neurosurgery: Status, Prospects, and Challenges",10.1093/neuros/nyab337,,,,,,Mervyn Jun Rui Lim,W3197612343,"To the Editor: I read with great interest the article by Dagi and colleagues.1 I would like to congratulate the authors on introducing artificial intelligence (AI) to neurosurgeons and presenting a comprehensive overview of AI concepts, applications, and challenges to the adoption of AI algorithms in clinical practice. AI has the potential to revolutionize diagnostics, clinical decision making, prognostication, and operative interventions in neurosurgery and has an important role to play in advancing precision medicine for neurosurgery in the future.2-6 Dagi et al highlighted that as of 2018, only 23 neurosurgical studies reported using machine learning (ML) or AI in the neurosurgical literature,7 although this number may be under-represented and may be closer to the order of 70 to 155 studies in recent years.8,9 Despite the exponential increase in the number of publications on the use of AI in medicine and neurosurgery,10,11 actual clinical applications and medical devices using AI algorithms remain limited.12,13 There is still a large gap between expectations of AI and the reality of applying AI in neurosurgical clinical practice. In their article, Dagi et al1 had introduced the need for validation and verification of AI algorithms, and highlighted the regulatory challenges of translating AI algorithms into real-world applications. In order to bridge the translational gap for AI algorithms to be used in neurosurgery, I would like to emphasize that, first, the development of accurate and unbiased AI algorithms is needed.14 This allows the neurosurgical community to trust the use of AI algorithms in clinical practice, and to mitigate the risks of unintended and negative consequences in using AI in neurosurgery.5 These concerns are real, and existing reviews on AI algorithms in neurosurgery have highlighted methodological issues related to using biased data for AI model development and applying models outside of populations intended for clinical use.7,15,16 Furthermore, the risks of widespread implementation of inaccurate AI algorithms have been illustrated in a recent example of a proprietary sepsis prediction model that was shown to have poor discrimination and calibration in predicting the onset of sepsis, raising fundamental concerns about its use nationally.17 Thus, for AI algorithms to be translated into real-world applications in neurosurgery, challenges related to algorithm development and ML science would need to be addressed.18 In order to facilitate the development of AI algorithms for use in neurosurgery, the neurosurgical community would need to better understand the algorithm development pipeline,19 AI performance metrics,20,21 and potential bias in AI model development,22 and collaborate with data scientists to address problems suited for AI solutions in neurosurgery. As neurosurgeons best understand the myriad clinical problems related to the management of neurosurgical diseases, they play a pivotal role in guiding the development of AI algorithms for clinical application in neurosurgery. Importantly, before widespread adoption and translation into clinical practice may occur, AI algorithms would also need to be understood and trusted by neurosurgeons.18 Thus, the perceived opacity of AI algorithms, described by Dagi et al as “a black box whose internal logic may be elusive” is a barrier to the translation of AI algorithms into neurosurgical practice.23,24 To enable the neurosurgical community to better understand how AI algorithms work and to reach a minimum level of trust that allows AI algorithms to be applied and used in neurosurgical practice, I would like to introduce the concept of explainable AI.25-27 Explainable AI is focused on the understanding and interpretation of the behavior of AI algorithms, which aims to create insight into how and why these algorithms produce predictions, while maintaining high predictive performance levels.28,29 Thus, an AI algorithm is explainable if neurosurgeons are able to understand how these algorithms make their predictions. Methods used to develop explainable AI may be classified into explainable modeling and post-hoc explanations, and these methods are able to provide global or local explanations of the AI algorithms being used.28 Examples of explainable AI have been reported in the medical literature,30-32 and in neurosurgery, an explainable deep-learning algorithm was developed for the detection of acute intracranial hemorrhage on noncontrast computed tomography scans.33 Further developments in the field of explainable AI may allow both neurosurgeons and neurosurgical patients to better understand how AI algorithms work, and enable the translation of these algorithms into neurosurgical practice. Lastly, the true test of clinical applicability for AI algorithms may occur in the form of robust clinical evaluation using real-world randomized controlled trials (RCTs).18,34 Such RCTs for AI are an invaluable tool to demonstrate causal inference and the effectiveness of AI algorithms in improving clinical outcomes in neurosurgical practice.34 Data scientists cannot conduct such RCTs in neurosurgical practice. Neurosurgeons are thus best positioned to lead the development of AI solutions in neurosurgery, to ensure that the neurosurgical community understand the intended use of these algorithms, and to translate these algorithms into real-world neurosurgical applications. I hope that this letter would be complementary to Dr Dagi's article1 to highlight further challenges to the clinical application of AI algorithms in neurosurgery, introduce explainable AI to neurosurgeons, and encourage the development of AI algorithms for neurosurgical practice in the future. Funding This study did not receive any funding or financial support. Disclosures The author has no personal, financial, or institutional interest in any of the drugs, materials, or devices described in this article.",W2167955381; W2763556273; W2888109941; W2901558604; W2905307056; W2905322176; W2908201961; W2921613140; W2943491685; W2944218525; W2953532875; W2966977115; W2982580298; W2989744286; W2990768863; W2997217231; W3005745869; W3011571399; W3024173558; W3036196393; W3036319923; W3038004684; W3046238651; W3046761449; W3070313384; W3086667591; W3092857159; W3095202475; W3116286104; W3122548859; W3131569973; W3148271110; W3161909910; W3174786846; W6780055081,10,2021,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
