39742397,Could human pharmacy and therapeutics committees be replaced with artificial intelligence systems?,10.1093/ajhp/zxae413,,,,,,Nada Abu-Shraie,W4405957421,"Artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare has evolved markedly, leading to significant advancements in various medical fields.1 For example, AI has significantly impacted clinical decision-making, improved the accuracy of disease diagnosis, streamlined healthcare providers’ workflows, increased clinical operational efficiency, and enhanced therapeutic drug monitoring, ultimately leading to better patient outcomes.2 The pharmacy and therapeutics (P&T) committee is an integral part of the healthcare system, primarily ensuring safe, clinically effective, and cost-effective formulary drug management. The P&T committee optimizes patient care through activities such as medication evaluation for formulary addition or deletion, development of clinical therapeutic guidelines, provision of drug information, adverse drug reaction monitoring, medication error identification, construction of clinical decision support systems for computerized prescriber order entry, patient education, and interprofessional collaboration. With rapid advancements in the healthcare sector, the number of medications to be added to or deleted from the formulary has increased noticeably along with the complexity of the evidence-based evaluation process and administrative tasks. As a result, there is an increasing demand for advanced technological solutions that can assist healthcare professionals in their responsibilities and optimize formulary management.3",W3025142504; W4387842909; W4401548054; W6857929706,4,2025,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39483109,"A new chapter in traditional, complementary, and integrative medicine: Advancing the Journal of Complementary and Integrative Medicine through open science, meta-research, artificial intelligence, and early career researcher involvement",10.1515/jcim-2024-0364,,,,,,Jeremy Y. Ng,W4403681703,"Article A new chapter in traditional, complementary, and integrative medicine: Advancing the Journal of Complementary and Integrative medicine through open science, meta-research, artificial intelligence, and early career researcher involvement was published on October 24, 2024 in the journal Journal of Complementary and Integrative Medicine (volume 0, issue 0).",W4391687152; W4396767592; W4400411385,2,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
39072266,Artificial intelligence tools may be superior to traditional approaches for translating patient handouts: A pilot study,10.1016/j.jdin.2024.04.007,,,,,,Sydney Dong; Sijia Zhang; Helen Park; Jessica De La Rosa; A. Rahman D. M. H. Alnabti; Vy Xuan Pham; Andres Cedeno; Isabella Toledo; Anika Jallorina; Jennifer Laborada; Bryan Sun; Regina Wang; George Hightower,W4396668082,"To the Editor: Idioms are phrases that have an intended meaning beyond the literal interpretation of the individual words. American idioms used to discuss skin color and skin cancer risk tend to conflate race with biology, potentially confusing non-English speakers.1,2 A 2022 US Census survey revealed that 1 in 5 children live in households where languages other than English are spoken, with Spanish, Chinese, Tagalog, Vietnamese, Arabic, and Korean among the most common non-English languages.3 For these children and families, artificial intelligence (AI)-powered tools offer promise in helping dermatologists with patient-provider communication and patient education.",W3116899544; W4220845736; W4367310920,3,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
