37402289,A commentary on ‘Advances in artificial intelligence (AI) based diagnosis and treatment of liver diseases – correspondence’,10.1097/JS9.0000000000000585,,,,,,Mohammad Abavisani; Farhad Dadgar; Farzaneh Peikfalak; Masoud Keikha,W4383092991,"Abavisani, Mohammad; Dadgar, Farhad; Peikfalak, Farzaneh; Keikha, Masoud Author Information",W2134182464; W2809596283; W2998368831; W3119555742; W3208636500; W4380714841; W4380714844,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37340656,"Colorectal cancer invasion calculation, a colorectal tumor depth diagnostic artificial intelligence, is promising, but its diagnostic accuracy remains a challenge",10.1111/den.14602,,,,,,Yasuhiko Mizuguchi; Yutaka Saito,W4381470306,"Yao et al.1 developed the colorectal cancer invasion calculation (CICC), an artificial intelligence system for diagnosing colorectal tumor depth. It has achieved a sensitivity of 79%, specificity of 96%, and accuracy of 90% for test data including advanced cancer, demonstrating efficacy equivalent to that of expert endoscopists and superior to that of nonexperts. The system comprises two steps: first, three classifications (NICE, Paris classification, and laterally spreading tumor) are determined on white light endoscopy (WL) or image-enhanced endoscopy (IEE) using the convolutional neural network (CNN). Second, the machine learning algorithm (k-nearest neighbors classifier [KNN]) uses these three classifications and four clinical data points (age, sex, and tumor size and location) as input to determine whether endoscopic resection is indicated. The first step, the prediction of endoscopic classification on images by the CNNs, is a straightforward application of CNN. The second step, a machine learning model for predicting tumor depth diagnosis according to multimodal features, has also been attempted in the past. This system is made by combining these two models. In terms of predicting invasion depth, the KNN is the essence of this system and the CNN is used only as an assistant to automatically provide input for the KNN. In Japanese clinical practice, the indication for endoscopic resection of colorectal tumors is determined by an algorithm that is a combination of the Japan Narrow Band Imaging Expert Team (JNET) classification and the pit pattern, which are two IEE classifications.2 Recently, the performance of machine learning models that take into account multimodal information such as macroscopic types in addition to these IEE classifications has been reported.3, 4 The decision on the indication for endoscopic resection must be made in real time. Hence, these models were implemented as tree-based models that can be mentally calculated by the endoscopist. However, it is challenging for endoscopists to think about these processes in their minds during endoscopy in real time. In contrast, the user interface of CICC is remarkable. The system can predict endoscopic classification using images in real time; therefore, it can help determine whether endoscopic resection is indicated without manual labeling of endoscopic findings. These machine learning systems using CNNs as an assistant are ideal approaches for using machine learning models in real time when it is difficult for endoscopists to perform mental computations. With the increased clinical need to use higher performance and more complex models, semiautomated interfaces, such as the one used here, will become extremely important. The predictive performance of the CICC for indicating endoscopic resection has a sensitivity of 50% if advanced cancers are excluded, which is not high enough to be useful for clinical decision–making. This is probably because the training set includes only a few submucosal invasive (T1) cancer cases, which are the most challenging to diagnose and treat via endoscopy. An endoscopist who is experienced in intramucosal lesions or advanced cancers but has never diagnosed T1 cancer will have difficulties determining the indication for endoscopic resection. We believe that the same applies to machine learning models. To improve performance, a larger number of T1 cancer cases should be included. Moreover, the data used as input for KNN should be re–examined. Previous research has found that tumor size, age, and sex are ineffective in determining cancer depth. The inclusion of factors with low interobserver agreement, such as macroscopic type, may affect model performance. Further, a more detailed IEE classification, such as the JNET classification with magnified narrow band imaging and pit pattern classification is important for developing artificial intelligence for precise cancer depth prediction. The authors previously developed and reported Endo-CRC,5 a CNN-based depth-diagnostic model of colorectal cancer with WL and IEE as inputs. Endo-CRC and CICC are similar (i.e., both are real-time depth-diagnostic systems with WL and IEE images as inputs); however, they also have differences (e.g., CICC uses not only endoscopic images but also clinical data as input). Furthermore, CICC uses CNN only to predict three endoscopic classifications, and it is basically a machine learning model with clinical data and three classifications as input. If sufficient training images are available, the most intuitive machine learning model for predicting tumor depth using clinical data and endoscopic images can be a one-stage machine learning model that predicts the output using all inputs. However, CICC is not such a model. This means that it uses only endoscopic classifications familiar to endoscopists from various features in the final layer of the CNN and discards other features. Some discarded features could have been useful for determining the tumor depth. In other words, not using these features could have compromised prediction performance. Interestingly, the authors emphasize that they have designed a system that can be understood and interpreted by human experts. There are several important issues with machine learning systems in clinical applications, including that they cannot predict unknown data that differ considerably from the training data, and whether data are outliers cannot be confirmed. These inherent limitations are attributed to that machine learning systems are inductive inference mechanisms. To diagnose, humans must combine inductive learning and deductive thinking based on basic theory and background knowledge; therefore, machine learning can inherently fail. Even if machine learning has a similar or slightly higher performance on average than humans, the latter can accurately validate results, which can be used in clinical decision making. The CICC limits the use of deep learning to automatically determining features that can be interpreted by humans; therefore, at least the first part can be verified by humans. This makes the system more clinically acceptable and understandable by humans. However, whether the inference mechanism in the second stage is understandable remains a question. As machine learning systems evolve, more complex models with higher predictive power are emerging. If a model becomes more complex, it becomes more like a “black box” that cannot be understood. Can we make clinical decisions with models that are extremely complex to understand? If understanding means clarifying the chain of causal associations one by one, then it will be impossible for humans to understand complex models such as neural networks. However, small models, such as the general linear regression model, are already being used in clinical settings, and we cannot say that we truly understand them. Therefore, understandability is subjective and does not necessarily require a complete comprehension of the model for clinical use. If a model is used repeatedly and found to be useful, then clinicians will be willing to use it. Considering the remarkable advancements in machine learning models recently, it will not be long before clinicians start using models that they do not understand in clinical decision making. Until then, technologies that bridge the gap, such as those presented here, will become increasingly important. Authors declare no conflict of interest for this article. None.",W2498208153; W2895090199; W4200192474; W4289262236; W4310938394,1,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
37318843,GPT-4: the future of artificial intelligence in medical school assessments,10.1177/01410768231181251,,,,,,Lois Haruna-Cooper; Mohammed Ahmed Rashid,W4380786362,"In recent years, artificial intelligence (AI) has been rapidly advancing in many fields, including medicine. One AI tool that has gained particular attention in the medical community is Chat-GPT, a large language model developed by OpenAI, which was first launched in November 2022, and its updated version, GPT-4, which was launched in March and which promises to be even more powerful and accurate in its capabilities. GPT-4 is a powerful tool that can generate human-like responses to a wide range of questions, prompts and images, and create academic articles, such as the one you are reading now. Using Chat-GPT to develop early iterations of this article and its title, some of which are shared in Appendix 1, highlights its potential to shift how we assess and prompt a careful reconsideration of the potential role of AI in medical education. Medical school assessments are crucial to ensuring that newly graduated doctors are competent to provide safe and effective care and at an appropriate level to pursue postgraduate medical training. Traditionally, medical school examinations have been packaged into programmes and systems of assessment of written, practical and workplacebased assessments, overseen and graded by human examiners. However, the use of AI tools like ChatGPT can provide new opportunities for medical school assessments. The disruption caused by COVID-19 caused a shift in the delivery of medical education, with a review of how teaching is delivered, and many assessments being adapted to online versions. This led to increased innovation in assessments and sparked discussion about the potential impact of cheating and the ethics surrounding the increased use of technology in assessment. The idea of cheating forces greater interrogation of the purposes of assessment in a professional education programme like medicine and how students view their high-stakes exams. Although workplace-based assessments have received great attention and priority in recent years because of their authenticity to ‘real-world’ clinical practice, students often focus on their performance on written and practical assessments. This may reflect that these occur less frequently than workplace-based assessments, and therefore there is less opportunity to view written and practical exams as assessments for learning, rather than assessments of learning. This view may influence how students behave in their assessments, as they perform to satisfy their assessor, rather than patient-centred care being the focus. With GPT-4’s ability to analyse diverse data, we must consider its potential to become an assessor, and how this could radically shift how students view and interact with assessment. While AI may not have all the answers, it can be utilised as a tool to enhance current assessment models. If we consider the breadth of output that the current GPT-4 prototype offers, we can begin to imagine the potential it could bring to medical school assessments. Below are some examples of how it can be utilised in current assessment models:",W2623879525; W3035954019; W3118565648; W4283730835; W4319662928,25,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
