37651680,Practice Makes Perfect: Why Surgical Residency Programs Must Embrace Artificial Intelligence,10.1097/ACM.0000000000005442,,,,,,David T. Guerrero; Malke Asaad,W4386346614,"To the Editor: The surgical profession faces new realities that pose a challenge to the traditional apprenticeship training model. Residents have fewer opportunities to gain experience in the operating room because of duty-hour restrictions and the ongoing surgeon shortage. The COVID-19 pandemic illustrated how substantially in-person learning can be affected when circumstances change. If we do not start embracing new training modalities, surgical residents will not get enough practice and feedback to perfect their skills. Artificial intelligence offers a solution to this problem through intelligent tutoring systems (ITSs). By incorporating machine learning into surgical simulators, ITSs can provide residents with objective, real-time, and personalized feedback that might otherwise be unavailable. In addition to facilitating practice opportunities, several 2022 studies show that ITSs can also provide more effective instruction than humans. Students receiving training on brain tumor removal from a virtual operative assistant learned the skills 2.6 times faster and achieved 36% better performance when compared with receiving human instruction.1 In another study, an Intelligent Continuous Expertise Monitoring System (ICEMS) replicated the role of human instructors by providing skill assessments and risk alerts in real time for the subpial resection of brain tumors. The ICEMS distinguished between expert and novice performance and provided coaching comparable to that in the operating room.2 In the field of endodontic surgery, the Surgical Decision-making Mentor (SDMentor) was developed to teach trainees the rationale for surgical decision-making. A study comparing the SDMentor tutorial content to feedback from 10 experienced human tutors found that the quality of the SDMentor interventions was significantly better than that from the human tutors.3 Traditional methods of training will continue to have learning value, but ITSs can give residents a safe environment to practice and perfect their techniques as an adjunct to their in-person learning and need to be more fully incorporated into surgical residency programs. David T. Guerrero, MS Third-year medical student, The University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine, Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania; [email protected] Malke Asaad, MD Second-year plastic surgery resident, Department of Plastic Surgery, University of Pittsburgh Medical Center, Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania",W3202216058; W4212922284; W4224995026,5,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
