38488329,Can generative artificial intelligence facilitate illustration of‐ and communication regarding hallucinations and delusions?,10.1111/acps.13680,,,,,,Søren Dinesen Østergaard,W4392884089,"In the context of artificial intelligence (AI), the term ""hallucinations"" is used to refer to false responses generated by conversational agents/chatbots or other generative AI tools (artificial intelligence/machine learning models capable of generating content such as text, speech, images and video).1, 2 This metaphor is unfortunate as it is both imprecise and, due to its clear negative connotation, stigmatizing for the many individuals experiencing hallucinations—those with schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders in particular.3 While the stigma associated with the misunderstood use of the term hallucinations in relation to generative AI is highly unfortunate, this editorial will propose that it is, however, possible that generative AI may also be helpful and reduce stigma for those experiencing hallucinations and/or delusions. For individuals with schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders it can be difficult to communicate the nature and quality of their hallucinations and delusions to relatives, friends as well as to the healthcare professionals involved in their treatment—often due to the recipients not handling this communication well enough.4 This may lead to lack of understanding of the suffering associated with these symptoms and could, in turn, contribute to detachment from loved ones, stigma and suboptimal treatment.5 Therefore, tools to facilitate communication regarding hallucinations and delusions are sorely needed. This editorial will propose that AI tools capable of generating images (e.g., DALL·E6) and video (e.g., Sora7) may be used to facilitate (highly affordable) illustration of- and, thereby, communication regarding hallucinations and delusions experienced by people with schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders. Interestingly, this approach has recently been described in an ophthalmological case report, where Woods and colleagues report on the diagnosing and treatment of a patient with monocular Charles Bonnet syndrome secondary to optic neuritis, where generative AI was used to successfully illustrate the patient's visual hallucination.8 Figure 1 shows three hypothetical examples of hallucinations and delusions illustrated using the version of DALL·E6 embedded within ChatGPT-49 at the time of writing—along with the exact prompts that were used to generate the images. Two images of each set of symptoms are shown (read from left to right)—highlighting the importance of the wording of the prompts and the possibility to revise images if they are not accurately portraying the symptoms in question. While this is, by no means, a formal assessment of the quality of the generated illustrations or their usefulness, it does seem that this approach has the potential to aid communication regarding hallucinations and delusions between individuals experiencing psychotic symptoms and the people they interact with both privately and in the context of their treatment. Whether this is indeed the case should, of course, be subjected to formal study, as is currently being done for the application of virtual reality interventions to reduce auditory hallucinations—an approach somewhat related to that proposed here—as it involves illustration/creation of an avatar with a voice resembling the hallucinatory voice experienced by the patient.10, 11 The hope would be that the results of such studies would document that the facilitated communication regarding hallucinations and delusions due to the AI-generated illustrations would both reduce stigma (showing the illustrations to other people will increase understanding of/demystify psychosis) and improve patient-centered treatment. Indeed, with regard to the latter, it seems likely that the illustrations could be used to provide an exhaustive ""map"" of a patient's psychotic symptoms in the beginning of the course of treatment, a map which can then be revised as needed over time—and provide the basis for psychometric rating (""how severe has the symptom illustrated by this image been over the past week?"")—thereby facilitating measurement-based care.12-14 With regard to the practical process of generating illustrations of the specific hallucinations/delusions experienced by individuals with schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders, it would seem advisable to have this aided by a healthcare professional with some experience in prompting, for at least three reasons. First, generating images matching the psychotic symptoms described by a patient will require some practice in prompting (""prompt engineering"" is becoming a discipline in itself).15 Second, it cannot be excluded that some patients may experience worsening of, for example, paranoid thoughts if an image is either too close to his/her symptoms (""how can the AI possibly portray my inner world so well? There must indeed be cameras installed in my apartment!"") or even evoke a new delusion if an image is off target (""I hadn't thought of the possibility that there could also be a spy camera installed in my own camera… I have to destroy it immediately."").16 Therefore, talking the experience of generating the illustrations through with a healthcare professional who, if needed, can explain the basics of how the AI technology works (trained on a tremendous amount of text and images) and resolve any misunderstandings/misinterpretations, will likely be ideal and may even create an empowering sense of co-creation for the patient. Third, there is also a privacy issue to take into account. Specifically, during prompting, personal information potentially linking the individual patient to the portrayed hallucinations and delusions should not be disclosed. The healthcare professional will have the required experience in handling personal information to prevent this from happening. In summary, this editorial proposes that image generation using AI may provide highly affordable, yet very valuable, illustrations of the hallucinations and delusions experienced by individuals with schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders. These illustrations may ease the complex communication regarding these symptoms and, thereby, have the potential to reduce stigma and improve the treatment of psychosis. There was no funding for this work. Outside this work, SDØ is supported by the Novo Nordisk Foundation (grant number: NNF20SA0062874), the Lundbeck Foundation (grant numbers: R358-2020-2341 and R344-2020-1073), the Danish Cancer Society (grant number: R283-A16461), the Central Denmark Region Fund for Strengthening of Health Science (grant number: 1-36-72-4-20), The Danish Agency for Digitisation Investment Fund for New Technologies (grant number 2020-6720), and Independent Research Fund Denmark (grant number: 7016-00048B and 2096-00055A). These funders had no role in this work, nor in the preparation, review, or approval of the manuscript or the decision to submit for publication. SDØ received the 2020 Lundbeck Foundation Young Investigator Prize. SDØ owns/has owned units of mutual funds with stock tickers DKIGI, IAIMWC, SPIC25KL and WEKAFKI, and owns/has owned units of exchange traded funds with stock tickers BATE, TRET, QDV5, QDVH, QDVE, SADM, IQQH, USPY, EXH2, 2B76, IS4S, OM3X and EUNL. Not applicable as there was no data involved in this work.",W1835874450; W1999015105; W2101028093; W3025757190; W3089640075; W3129649050; W4295754157; W4377691005; W4386158236; W4391722425,3,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38481072,Assessment of patient perceptions of artificial intelligence use in dermatology: A cross‐sectional survey,10.1111/srt.13656,,,,,,Alexander T. H. Wu; Madeline Ngo; Cristina Thomas,W4392813893,"The use of artificial intelligence (AI) in medicine has grown in recent decades, with deep neural networks demonstrating accuracies comparable to dermatologists when classifying melanoma, keratinocyte carcinomas, and other common skin conditions. 1 , 2 , 3 With the future possibility that AI will be integrated into dermatology practice, it is important to understand how patients view these possible changes. Although prior studies have shown that patients are open to the use of AI in the diagnosis of skin cancer, little is known about patients' trust and perception of AI accuracy in general dermatology. 4 , 5 This survey study aimed to gather opinions from a diverse dermatology patient population on AI use in dermatology and establish a specific accuracy at which patients would be comfortable receiving a diagnosis solely from an AI tool.",W1994603799; W2581082771; W2973839374; W3012449740; W3013387448; W3025011581; W3085870326; W3105070630; W3203010750,20,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38339876,How artificial intelligence can assist with ischaemic heart disease,10.1093/eurheartj/ehae030,,,,,,Jamol Uzokov; Anis Alyavi; Bakhromkhon Alyavi; Akbar Abdullaev,W4391718864,"Ischaemic heart disease (IHD) is the most common type of cardiovascular disease (CVD) and the leading cause of death and morbidity among patients with CVD. Ischaemic heart disease is considered a major threat in the 21st century.1,2 The majority of patients with non-fatal IHD live with chronic conditions and impaired quality of life, which in turn leads to increased load on healthcare systems and demands effective and prompt treatment of the disease from doctors. It is predicted that the rate of IHD will increase not only due to population growth and increasing life expectancy but also due to advanced diagnostic techniques and an increased number of risk factors such as diabetes, obesity, and socio-economic conditions.3 Considering the high prevalence, morbidity, and mortality of IHD and its subsequent impact on healthcare, it is essential to quickly and timeously identify and stratify patients with IHD for further management using cloud-based innovative digital technologies and artificial intelligence (AI). With advances in technology and healthcare, AI has emerged as a powerful tool in medicine, demonstrating its potential to transform the landscape of medical diagnostics. In particular, AI holds promise for assisting the medical field in the early, timely, and precise identification of IHD, paving the way for more efficient intervention and improved patient outcomes. Artificial intelligence excels in processing vast amounts of medical data, including patient histories, lab results, and imaging studies. Machine learning algorithms can identify subtle patterns and correlations that might escape the human eye, enhancing the diagnostic accuracy of IHD. The electrocardiogram (ECG) is a fundamental tool in diagnosing ischaemic changes of the myocardium. Artificial intelligence algorithms can analyse ECG data swiftly and accurately, identifying any abnormalities in the heart’s electrical activity that may suggest ischaemic changes. This, in turn, may allow for quicker diagnosis and intervention. Artificial intelligence algorithms can be trained to interpret echocardiograms, a key imaging modality for assessing cardiac function. By analysing the intricate details of heart images, AI can detect abnormalities, such as reduced cardiac output, any kinetic changes, or impaired ventricular function, indicative of IHD. Coronary angiography analysis by AI represents a huge frontier in IHD diagnosis. Leveraging the power of AI, this modern approach offers a complicated and precise examination of the coronary arteries. By employing advanced algorithms, AI can rigorously evaluate angiographic images, identifying subtle anomalies and providing quantitative insights into vessel dimensions, flow patterns, and potential obstructions. This transformative technology not only enhances the efficiency of coronary angiography interpretation but also holds promise for early detection, personalized treatment planning, and improved patient outcomes in the realm of IHD. Artificial intelligence can assist in stratifying patients based on their risk of developing IHD. By analysing existing risk and medical history, AI can help identify individuals who may benefit from closer monitoring and preventive measures and it can also define how they might benefit from such measures. Seamless integration of AI with electronic health records (EHRs) and the above-mentioned diagnostic tools streamlines the diagnostic process. Artificial intelligence algorithms can analyse patient data stored in EHRs, interpret existing diagnostic tool results, and integrate their data for further processing. In addition, AI can provide healthcare professionals with comprehensive insights into a patient’s cardiovascular health and aid in the early detection of IHD and apply precise management strategies. Recently, Professor Alyavi A.L. and his team were awarded a Research Excellence Program Grant by the World Bank Group in the framework of Modernizing Uzbekistan National Innovation System (MUNIS) for their proposed project. The project brings together a nationwide community of cardiovascular and digital health researchers as well as physicians across tertiary care to study the early diagnostic and stratification possibilities of AI in IHD. The project’s prime objective is to solve problems with diagnostics and stratification of patients with IHD with regard to applying advances in digitalization. It aims to develop, design, and implement a personalized, innovative computer-based platform further integrated with digital tools that will be driven by AI. In the proposed project, three major diagnostic tools, such as ECG, echocardiography, and coronary angiography, will be used in one application to accurately diagnose and stratify patients with IHD for further management (Figure 1). Project overview Even though several studies have been conducted to diagnose IHD with specific diagnostic tools using AI, to date, there is no single study or development using three major diagnostic methods that are ECG, echocardiography, and coronary angiography in an integrated application to diagnose and stratify patients with CVD. All available studies are either limited to only one diagnostic method or include restricted cardiovascular conditions, which in turn creates a gap in terms of using AI and machine learning to diagnose and stratify patients with IHD. These issues and challenges encourage the team to focus on the proposed project and create an AI-based platform to diagnose and stratify patients with IHD using three major diagnostic tools. Project specific aims, its design and study cohort are summarised in Table 1. Project specific aims and study cohort The study brings together the two medical centres in Uzbekistan, the ‘Republican Specialized Scientific Practical Medical Center of Therapy and Medical Rehabilitation’ and the ‘Republican Specialized Scientific Practical Medical Center of Cardiology’, along with Tashkent Pediatric Medical Institute as well as researchers across three research institutes of the country. Furthermore, two leading cardiovascular and digital health key opinion leaders have been invited to form an advisory panel and two local, experienced doctors will test the developed model. Finally, reflecting the promptness of increasing attention on AI in the diagnosis of CVD particularly, IHD, plans and projects are also underway to run parallel studies in Uzbekistan and abroad. The implementation of AI in the diagnosis of IHD represents a significant step towards more effective and timely healthcare interventions. By harnessing the power of deep learning and data analysis, AI has the potential to increase diagnostic accuracy, improve patient outcomes, and contribute to the global effort to combat the burden of IHD. As research and technology advances, the collaborative partnership between AI and medical doctors holds great promise for a future where early identification and management become the norm in the fight against CVD. The authors would like to thank all Project Implementation Unit of ‘MUNIS’ and management of the ‘Republican Specialized Scientific Practical Medical Center of Therapy and Medical Rehabilitation’ for their continuous support. All authors declare no disclosure of interest for this contribution. This study is funded by REP25112021-84 grant under the MUNIS Project, supported by the World Bank and the Government of the Republic of Uzbekistan.",W1821665499; W2580792129; W3044853938; W3113178943; W6780534366,3,2024,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
