37534877,Editorial for “The Effect of Image Resampling on the Performance of Radiomics‐Based Artificial Intelligence in Multicenter Prostate MRI ”,10.1002/jmri.28939,,,,,,Dimitri A. Kessler; Martin John Graves,W4385514020,"In recent years, radiomics-based image analysis has emerged as a rapidly evolving field in medical imaging, extracting quantitative morphological and textural features from medical images. These features, when combined with clinical data, have shown potential in characterizing disease phenotypes and aiding clinical decision-making.1, 2 However, the robustness and generalizability of radiomic-based predictive models, particularly in multicenter studies, are focuses of ongoing investigation as these pose challenges for clinical implementation. Standardization of image acquisition parameters is difficult in clinical settings, in which patients may undergo imaging with different protocols and MR systems. Preprocessing steps such as image resampling, discretization, and spatial filtering alter the acquired image data and are known to affect radiomic characteristics.2-4 It is essential to understand the potential dependence of radiomic features on these factors to ensure their reliable utilization. Resampling and interpolation are commonly used to standardize pixel sizes and slice thicknesses, ensuring robustness in retrospective, multicenter studies.5 The study “The Effect of MR Image Resampling on the Performance of Radiomics AI in Multicenter Prostate MRI” by Roest et al6 published in this issue of the Journal of Magnetic Resonance Imaging evaluates the impact of isotropic image resampling using different interpolation methods on radiomics-based predictive modeling performance in a multicenter prostate MRI setting. Biparametric MRIs (bpMRIs) of the prostate, combining axial T2 weighted imaging (T2WI) and diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) sequences, with a total of 737 lesions encompassing all Prostate Imaging–Reporting and Data System (PI-RADS) lesion scores were included in the development of the radiomics-based predictive models. Of the included lesions, 461 were classified as nonclinically relevant (International Society of Urological Pathology [ISUP] grade < 2), while the remaining 276 were clinically relevant prostate cancer lesions (crPCas). This is a larger and more diverse dataset compared with previous studies performing predictive modeling using radiomics-based features.7 Three isotropic voxel spacing options were selected for the T2WI (0.35 mm, 0.5 mm, and 0.8 mm) and for the DWI sequences (1.37 mm, 2 mm, and 2.5 mm) with resampling performed using four different interpolation methods: nearest neighbor, linear, b-spline, and Blackman windowed-sinc interpolation. Similar to previous studies, which assessed the robustness of radiomics features to image resampling,2-4 the results of this work demonstrate the significant effect of image resampling on the performance of the radiomics-based predictive models. In a uni-parametric preselection experiment, the authors reported that the T2WI and DWI radiomics-based predictive models were varyingly influenced by resampling size, interpolation method, and dimensionality (2D or 3D). They observed a consistently lower performance of the T2WI models compared with the DWI, indicating that T2WI radiomics features are less important compared with DWI imaging features in differentiating crPCas from non-crPCas. A further multiparametric investigation, combining the selected T2WI and DWI strategies in a complete bpMRI radiomics-based predictive model demonstrated significantly higher performance of 2D and 3D models with isotropic resampling, compared with respective baseline models without isotropic resampling. The models achieved AUCs comparable with previously reported performances.7 Interestingly, no statistically significant difference was observed between the 2D and 3D bpMRI models, questioning the importance of 3D radiomics features when isotropic resampling is used. Finally, the authors provide recommendations for isotropic resampling size and interpolation method as starting points for developing 2D and 3D bpMRI prostate radiomics-based predictive models for use in future studies. This study highlights the importance of isotropic resampling for the development of predictive models using radiomics features, particularly in large multicenter datasets. The standardization of imaging data is critical for the robust use of radiomics-based analysis. This paper demonstrates that retrospective resampling, to achieve isotropic pixels/voxels, is an acceptable method to improve the performance of multicenter prostate radiomics-based predictive models.",W2763355946; W3128958754; W4220782222; W4283028665; W4294912031; W4311361705; W4385783924,0,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
