38165840,"Automation, artificial intelligence and robotics: emerging issues and debates in human factors and ergonomics",10.1080/00140139.2023.2296176,,,,,,Gemma J. M. Read; Patrick E. Waterson,W4390514517,"Forty years ago,",W2008653298; W2082577695; W2748347163,5,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
38031294,AI and ethics in mental health: exploring the controversy over the use of ChatGPT,10.1093/pubmed/fdad254,,,,,,Paul Arjanto; Feibry Feronika Wiwenly Senduk; Umi Nahdiyah; Mukti Sari Utami,W4389179035,"Dear Editors, The article titled ‘Epistemic Justice of ChatGPT in First Aid for Child and Adolescent Mental Health Issues’ is intriguing. The call for the use of ChatGPT as an alternative mental health service does indeed spark several debates. The main argument concerns the potential disruption of epistemic justice in psychiatric practice and first aid services for mental health patients.1 However, I have a different perspective that encompasses various other aspects such as ethics, methodology and the effectiveness of ChatGPT. It's undeniable that AI like ChatGPT has limitations in providing true emotional interpretation.2 But it is important to remember that the main goal of ChatGPT is not to replace psychiatrists or psychologists, but to serve as an initial source of information and first aid for those experiencing mental distress.3,4 If used wisely and within appropriate limits, ChatGPT can be an effective tool in supporting mental health services, not damaging epistemic justice. Regarding the affective value and ethical aspects, the assessment that AI like ChatGPT lacks these can be viewed from two sides.5 On the one hand, it's true that AI does not possess ethics and reason like humans and therefore has limitations in dealing with affective issues. However, on the other hand, AI also does not have the prejudices and biases that often influence human decisions. In this regard, ChatGPT can be an objective and unbiased tool, as long as it is confined in its role and is not a substitute for psychologists or psychiatrists. Speaking about mental health diagnoses, the role of AI like ChatGPT is not to make diagnoses, but rather to provide preliminary knowledge and information.6 AI is neither competent nor equipped to make mental health diagnoses. In this case, the use of ChatGPT must be overseen and limited by licensed and experienced mental health practitioners. Considering the pros and cons, every new technology will have both. Just like ChatGPT, there are advantages and disadvantages. However, as long as its use is supervised and used wisely, the disadvantages can be minimized, and the benefits can be maximized.7 In the end, it's important to see ChatGPT as a supportive tool, not a replacement, in the field of mental health. Using AI in this context requires a deep understanding of its limitations and strict oversight by licensed and experienced mental health practitioners. Without this, the risk of misuse and misunderstanding will undoubtedly increase. The authors declare no conflict of interest in this paper.",W2985543011; W3127924821; W4220964802; W4221106857; W4293918026; W4303986529; W4382515062; W6790760347; W6809563816; W6854020706,6,2023,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
