36253024,The Future of Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Kidney Health and Disease.,10.1053/j.ackd.2022.09.001,,,,,,Girish N. Nadkarni; Peter Kotanko,W4306247382,,,1,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35869894,Artificial intelligence in proteomics: new frontiers from risk prediction to treatment?,10.1093/eurheartj/ehac391,,,,,,Matthias Unterhuber; Karl‐Patrik Kresoja; Philipp C. Lurz; Hölger Thiele,W4286715996,"In cardiology, risk scores are used daily to estimate the cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk of a patient and eventually guide clinical decision making. To create accurate scoring systems, large populations are used, and clinically measurable parameters are included. These scores and calculations represent mostly linear and proportional relationships which correspond to the idea of ‘more A, therefore more B’. This allows for simple interpretability of causal relationships of risk factor and observed events, e.g. smoking or high blood pressure are harmful. Clinical risk models tend to perform well on average but show poor precision for individuals. There are subjects who despite having a high-risk profile will not develop significant CVD or experience events. Those ‘outliers’ may have a different clinical response to external conditions, e.g. heavy smoking, and still not develop any disease manifestation. Some may attribute this to fortune, but it is more probable that the individual is predisposed to a ‘natural resistance,’ or the exposure to a specific risk factor was not sufficient to cause damage. This naturally limits classical clinical risk scores, where the risk factor is always handled in the same manner depicting an overall risk, where outliers cannot be identified. To increase the precision of prediction, it is important to gain knowledge on which constellation of properties is hazardous and which is protective: For example, instead of mere smoking status, a more sophisticated approach is necessary which assesses whether smoking shifted the physiological constellation of an individual towards a hazard or not. This requires the use of novel techniques, and machine learning (ML) suggests itself for the task as it can identify outliers by deciding on an individual basis according to ‘When A is high, then B can be lower or higher, depending on C.’. Therefore, combining the analysis of circulating proteins with ML makes it an attractive method. The proteome is the result of the genomic composition, epigenetics, and environment and can therefore be seen as a surrogate marker for the product of our genetic legacy, predisposition, and external influences (Figure 1). Proteome as a link between genetics and the environment. The information contained in the proteome can be unravelled by reverse engineering through Machine Learning and used to predict the cardiovascular risk, find clinically meaningful phenotypes and drug targets. In a study, the proteome of 1524 healthy individuals was used to predict cardiovascular events such as myocardial infarction and stroke.1 Multiple ML models were derived and compared with clinical risk scores in 702 subjects of an external cohort. The area under the curve (AUC) achieved by the proteomics model was 0.71 in comparison with the 0.61 of the clinical risk scores (P < 0.001). A step further has been taken by constructing ML models with proteomics and all-cause mortality as outcome. Multiple algorithms analysed proteins in a cohort comprising 1998 patients and compared their performance with clinical risk scores such as the Framingham and European Risk-Chart-SCORE. The models were validated in an independent cohort of 772 subjects. The AUC increased from 0.65 as seen in conventional statistical models to 0.94 in the neural network (P < 0.001).2 These study results highlight four fundamentals: the necessity of using reproducible and standardized input data for ML models. the need to use most precisely defined outcomes, whereas death might be the most well-defined outcome of all. the observation that some proteins may interact differently based on the state of additional conditions. lastly, ML models performed better than the clinical risk scores without the need of including clinical parameters such as age, smoking status, or gender. This suggests that the proteome may be an unbiased and accurate reflection of the actual risk-influencing factors. By using Shapley values and plotting outcomes predicted by the models in different states of the proteins, we were able to show that assuming linear interactions between the variables leads to over-simplifying the responsible mechanisms. Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) was the most influential peptide for the ML algorithm to predict death, albeit it did not reach statistical significance in the multivariable Cox and logistic regression models (odds ratio 1.23; 95% CI 0.99–1.53, P = 0.058 and hazard ratio 1.20; 95%CI 1.00–1.45, P = 0.051, respectively). The reason was demasked as BNP was linearly correlated with death only within a pre-specified range; otherwise, it depended on other peptides involved in other processes such as tumor-necrosis-factor-related-apoptosis-inducing-ligand-receptor-2 (TRAILR2). TRAILR2 has been found to counterintuitively regulate also other non-canonical cellular effects and play a critical role in pulmonary hypertension3 and to be involved in protein changes in smokers.4 Outside of the pre-specified range, BNP showed a curvilinear mortality–probability relationship (Figure 2). All-Cause Death probability is influenced in a non-linear fashion. Simplified relationship graph depicting the probability of death in function to brain natriuretic peptide and its interaction with tumour-necrosis-factor-related-apoptosis-inducing-ligand-receptor 2 levels, adapted from.2 Death probability increases linearly only within a small range of brain natriuretic peptide levels ( = linear relationship zone). In lower brain natriuretic peptide levels, higher tumour-necrosis-factor-related-apoptosis-inducing-ligand-receptor 2 levels can be beneficial on mortality; in higher brain natriuretic peptide levels it can act adversely. These relationships are modelled by machine learning with infinite dimensions; therefore, interpretation with more than three variables can be difficult. When focusing on a specified target, it is possible to aim for pathways involved only in the outcome of interest and to follow the steps undertaken by specific peptides, all the way up to their origin in the genome. This has been already achieved in training neural networks to predict splicing from a pre-mRNA out from a genomic sequence to detect noncoding mutations in rare genetic diseases, paving the way for a reverse engineering of the human genome-protein processing.5 Chemical compounds can be designed to modify specific targets, but also to influence properties like solubility and bioactivity.6 This could lead to treatment response prediction, virtually interacting with the proteins to find novel drug targets, to monitor and predict response, towards a patient-tailored, cost-effective precision medicine. Complex events like major cardiovascular events or even the death of a human being are associated to and may by a substantial part be determined by circulating protein levels. Proteomics are a way to efficiently gain multidimensional information from a drop of blood, yielding promising results when combined with ML techniques. They represent a surrogate marker of the genetic predisposition, environmental influence, and the behaviour of an individual and can be used to predict the probability of cardiovascular events. By putting an effort in unravelling ML’s black boxes, reverse engineering of human physiology at a protein level might be a feasible way to gain new insights on manifold applications. This field merits further attention, as currently we only scratched on the surface of all the possibilities. None declared. Conflict of interest: The authors declare no conflict of interest. This article did rely solely on information based by the papers which were cited, therefore no data availability statement can be done.",W2519561652; W2884446054; W2909194804; W3066114734; W3206566542; W3209416355,2,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
35699584,Human-in-the-Loop Artificial Intelligence in Cardiac                     MRI,10.1148/radiol.221132,,,,,,Bharath Ambale-Venkatesh; João A. C. Lima,W4282841348,,W2079379784; W2082743257; W2105687765; W2300445845; W3108678668; W3165494136; W3206167738; W4220819350; W4282835526,3,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
34985016,Artificial intelligence in bladder cancer: current trends and future possibilities,10.1097/CM9.0000000000001830,,,,,,Jun; Deep K. Vaishnani; Rixu Lin; Jiayu Lyu; Bingyan Ni; Yang Zhang; Mengjun Hu; Guorong Chen,W4205494710,"To the Editor: Bladder cancer is the most common malignant tumor of the urinary system in the Asian population. In China, it is reported 80,000 new cases each year with an increasing incidence every year. Among these cases, 75% are of non-muscle invasive bladder cancer (NMIBC). In muscular invasive bladder cancer (MIBC) and NMIBC patients, radical cystectomy is considered as a primary approach of treatment due to the higher risk of tumor progression. Furthermore, cystoscopy and traditional transurethral resection are the main approaches for diagnosing and staging of bladder cancer. However, these approaches are relatively risky because of their complications and high peri-operative mortality.[1] Moreover, the most critical limitation of cystoscopy is its inefficient ability to differentiate between malignant tumors and healthy urothelium considering the multifocal aspect of the disease and inconspicuous but significant lesions (such as carcinoma in situ). Utilizing artificial intelligence (AI), we can accurately identify the distribution of heterogeneous tumors with the aid of a noninvasive, 3D image-based feature of computed tomography, or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). Machine learning (ML), when incorporated with 3D image-based MRI, can help differentiate low- and high-grade tumors. Furthermore, it enables physicians to perform less invasive surgical procedures, reduce intra-operative blood loss, shorten hospital stays, accelerate gastrointestinal function recovery, and reduce overall complications. The ML-based approach has been implemented in patients with MIBC to accurately quantify the tumor buds in immunofluorescence-labeled slides. Tumor budding is correlated to the tumor-node-metastasis staging system. All MIBC patients were classified into three novel staging standards based on their disease-specific survival rate.[2] Moreover, quantitative analysis of tumor buds using automated slide analysis can provide an alternative staging model with prognostic value for MIBC patients. The ML algorithm has been adopted to build predictive models of recurrence rate and survival time from imaging and surgical data. Both the sensitivity and specificity of predicting patients’ recurrence rate and survival rate after 1, 3, and 5 years of cystectomy are found to be >70%.[3] By optimizing surgical data acquisition, these predictive models can help physicians develop patient follow-up plans, provide assistance in treatment, and deliver improved care possibilities. Moreover, to generate the mathematical model classifier for outcome prediction, we can integrate the genome-wide atlas of frozen NMIBC specimens into the genetic programming algorithm. Although multiple ML and deep learning (DL) studies have been conducted to predict the prognosis of patients with bladder cancer, these models are rarely used in clinical practice. The main challenges in adopting these models in a clinical environment are incorporating standardized parameters, adjusting device differences, and collecting data from multiple institutions to ensure the models’ universality.[4] Furthermore, the formulation and recommendations of diagnosis and treatment plans are based on numerous evidence-based scientific papers and physicians’ clinical experience. Unfortunately, the development of these guidelines for diagnosing and treating most diseases in many countries has significantly poor data research considering their population for reference. In contrast, differences in ethnicity and other factors that reduce reference value are often found in various relevant foreign studies. Furthermore, ML and DL predictions should be further incorporated as data and model retraining while providing personalized patient care. After resolving these issues, ML and DL models can be trained using the bladder cancer dataset along with pre-operative, intra-operative, and post-operative data to accurately predict each patient's prognosis. For understanding various disease processes, extensive patient data sets and electronic medical records can be semiautomated, thus providing real-time predictive analysis. However, the prediction accuracy depends mainly on the effective data integration acquired from different sources. Although these models will not replace shared decision-making, they can complement the information that patients obtain from traditional methods. Moreover, each country should encourage healthcare organizations for collecting the data, enabling physicians to provide more reliable clinical and evidence-based treatment for their patients. Furthermore, organizations should take the initiative for collaborating with technology companies for executing a variety of AI-based research. The application of AI is still in the initial phase in the healthcare system, but it has tremendous potential in treatment measurements and has various applications in the diagnosis of bladder cancer. Similar to all the medical interventions, the application of ML for cancer diagnosis has both advantages and disadvantages. AI can improve the speed and consistency of diagnosis; however, it may also exacerbate overdiagnosis. ML cannot solve the gold standard problem, but it can further expose the predicament. Ultimately, what matters to patients and physicians is whether the diagnosis of bladder cancer is related to the length and quality of life. Before this technology is widely adopted, serious consideration should be given to the possibility of training ML algorithms for identifying the intermediate category between “cancer” and “noncancer.” The application of AI in the clinical field will develop and modify the diagnosis and decision-making process. Undoubtedly, physicians’ experience and evidence-based scientific papers will continue to play a vital role in AI future development to ensure that these systems operate as expected. Funding The work presented in this article was supported by the Zhejiang Provincial Medical and Health Science and Technology (No. WKJ-ZJ-1929). Conflicts of interest None.",W2916823992; W2917274988; W2921449823; W2976707734,2,2022,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Asano-original-simplified.csv
